      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 19, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 19, 1914.</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS FESTIVAL</p>
        <p>Our Tradesmen's Patriotic, Seasonable Endeavours.</p>
        <p>We'll keep our Christmas merry still; Each age has deemed the new-born year The fittest time for festal cheer.--Scorr.</p>
        <p>Despite the clash of arms, the roar of the death-dealing cannon—so loud and vengeful that it almost reaches our very ears; despite the sorrows that this horrible war has brought in its train; despite the stricken homes from which husband, son and brother have gone forth to vlorious death, we are, with that buoyancy of spirit that characterises our race, hurrying forward our preparations for cele- brating in good old English style, though in a somewhat subdued manner, the great festival of Christmas—than which no festival in the calendar is more honoured in the observance. Proof of the fact is on every hand. The very butcher's boy ceases his inharmonious "siffieur- isms " and knocks with chastened knock at the front door when lie brings the diurnal chops or steak, visions of the "Christmas box" ever before his eyes. The men you meet in the street have begun to wear the annual "Merry-Christmas-same-to-you " air, insepar- able from the season; there is that eagerness of expectancy about the very young folk that causes paterfamilias to count the contents of his purse and go forth and—buy! The shop windows are a source of temptation to the passer-by, and, when not of temptation, of envy. The fault is theirs. One cannot help thinking that at Christmas time they are spe- cially designed in order to tempt man, and, eke, woman, into venial sin—the -sin of envy. Howbeit, the fact remains:—Christmas, 1914, is but a few days off, and the people are making ready to forget for the nonce—the very, very, brief P once—all that surrounds it, and to "celebrate according." Christmas is a time for giving, and, conse- quently, present-buying is a part of the un- written ritual that precedes the celebration proper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Ft FINE SHOW.</head>
        <p>Ft FINE SHOW.</p>
        <p>the reflection that there is nobody so easily satisfied as a grown-up, when it comes to a mat- ter of Christmas presents, for he, or, maybe, it is a she—and we rather suspect, somehow, that most presents at this time of the year (as at all other times) find their way to members of the genus feminine—is certain to take "the will which prompts the deed" as a set-off against the gift, even if, by inadvertence, you send her a costly box of chocolates just when she is " down" with the most distressing attack of toothache that she ever had, or him a safety razor at the very moment when his one ambi- tion in life is to grow a " distingue" beard. An article of jewellery makes a most accept- able present—especially if it is "a ladye fayre" that you seek •to propitiate; though we must confess that we have never yet found the wife, the husband, or the gentleman friend who was averse to taking a gift "o' gold or siller, wi' jewel blazoned," or, for the matter of that, just gold or silver by themselves, without the pre- cious stones! You want a gift along these lines? Then why not try Messrs. Miller and Co.'s establishment at 10, Sankey-street; Mr. John Finlayson's shop at 42, Horsernarket-street; Messrs. Eustance and Co.'s richly laden 'em- porium in Sankey-street; or Mr. Jackson Moss's shop in London-road, Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>WHERE TO BUY "THE NICE THINGS."</p>
        <p>Just as the inner-man is, so to speak, "the executive branch of corporeal existence." so, also, is the culinary-cum-comestible side of Christmas a matter of supreme importance, for, as Albert Chevalier used to ask, " Wot's the good of 'er Chrismuss wivout the Chrissmus grub?" The housewife's first consideration, if she wants to give the real flavour to the Christ- mas dinner is the holly-decked pudding, com- pounded of the fruits and spices of the myste- rious East—the East where lived the Man Whose coming the great festival commemorates. For pudding ingredients she turns to the grocer, as, also, does she for many of the other concomi- tants that go to make up what Dickens called "the feasting side of the festival." She will find all she wants in the shape of Os vel necessary—in fact, indispensableroc,o.,,is at Messrs. Henry Milling and Co.'s shop in Horse- market and Corporation-streets; at Messrs. Win. ' Owen and Son's, Sankey-street; at Mr. J. Cucksey's, Lyme-street; at Price's, Butter- market-street; at the Co-operative Society's Stores, in Sankey-street, and at their numerous branch shops; at Mr. Peter Leigh's, Horse- market-stieet (where also the beverages that so many konsider indispensable at Yule-tide may be procured); or at Messrs. Povey and Sons' shop, in Bridge-street</p>
        <p>APPETISING TABLE FARE. A near neighbour to th: groceries are the con- fectioneries, and for these one could not do better than go to one of the Warrington Cafe Co.'s establishments—either that in Sankey- street, or that in Bridge-street; or to any of the following well-known shops , John Toft and Sons, Bridge-street; Messrs. Carter and Sons, Bridge-street; and Messrs. Hayes Bros., London-road, Stockton Heath; or to Atkinson's Cafe, in Bridge-street; Prince's Cafe, in Sankey-street; or at Hayes and Co.'s, London- road, Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>THE SOLID PART OF THE CHRISTMAS DINNER. "Meat and mirth never hindered no man," wrote an old versifier in the days of the sturdy trenclimen, and there will be many round the Christmas dinner table who will subscribe to his view. To most people meat is an essential —to the great majority the essential—part of the chief meal of the day, and for the Yule joint you could not easily do better than extend your Patronage to Mr. T. Baldwin, Padgate-lane, Warrington, and Warrington-road, Penketh ; or to Mr. W. Cooper, Buttermarket-street ; or if it be something in the way of game or poultry that you require, give a call at Messrs. F. and J. Riley's establishment in Sankey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>FOR LADIES ONLY.</head>
        <p>FOR LADIES ONLY.</p>
        <p>The milliners, ladies' outfitting and drapery shops are bound to be a source of special at- traction to the fair sex at this period of the year; indeed, they are a special attraction all the year round—according to mere man, who has to do most of the paying! Armed with the household purse and a resolute determina- tion, the good wife—and, for the matter of that, the young lady who hopes, some day, to adopt womenkind's most honourable calling; though she, at present, must go furnished with her own money—could not do better than direct her search for yule-tide bargains and useful and tasty gifts in the direction of any, or all, of the following well-known establishments : Jessie Dewitt, Bridge-street; Miss Laws, Bridge- street; Mrs. Walsh, Sankey-street; Hodgkin- son's, Bridge-street; Messrs. Dutton, Sankey- street; W. T. Williams, Sankey-street; J. Banks, Horsemarket-street and Scotland-road; C. Aloun- ' field, opposite Parr's Bank; R. W. Woods, Mar- ket-place ; the Co-operative Society, Sankey- street and Cairo-street; the Manchester Ware- house, Buttermarket-street ; Madame Lillie's, Bridge-street; M. A. and IC. Brown's, Friars' Gate; and Corbett's, Bridge-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WHAT ABOUT BOOTS?</head>
        <p>WHAT ABOUT BOOTS?</p>
        <p>Tinder the Clock," in Bridge-street, will be found the Public Benefit Boot Company's store, and there one desirous of purchasing a really ,seful present for himself—or for a friend, or relative, for the matter of that—will find a large stock of the latest and most durable things in footwear. Messrs. R. Roberts and Sons, of 19, Bridge-street, put forward a strong claim for Public patronage in the same direction, for, in spite of the increased price of leather, they are in the happy position of still being able to offer large stocks of hoots, shoes and slippers at the prices which ruled before the war. Their stock should be worth inspecting!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>SOMETHING FOR THE HOME.</head>
        <p>SOMETHING FOR THE HOME.</p>
        <p>Sample.</p>
        <p>An umbrella is always a useful gift, and is doubly useful at this time of the year. and if you want to acquire one for yourself, or to confer a real boon upon a friend by making him a present of one, it is a good tip to drop in at Afounfield's, opposite Parr's Bank. Verily, of Christmas presents for grown-ups there is no end, and the number of articles, useful and ornamental, from which you may select, is legion. Nlaybe you want to give a serviceable gift to a relative or a friend with a home of his or her) own. If so, what is amiss with an article of furniture—something that shall be a permanent reminder of your good- will and good-wishes? Or why shouldn't you treat yourself and the wife to "something for the home"? If your inclinations and your purse point in this direction, why not give a call on Mesears. Pendlebury Co.; Tanner</p>
        <p>1 Continu lir 4444 +++4,41'14'14)14,7.-01144;14 4414444+44+</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>FIRST DUTY TO SANTA CLAUS.</head>
        <p>FIRST DUTY TO SANTA CLAUS.</p>
        <p>Come with us on a tour round "the seats of merchandise "—the places where you may buy, and, buying, receive satisfaction. Your first mission, say, is the purchase of a toy, or, may- be, abook—" two covers, close-packed with printed page of fairy-lore, bedight with coloured scene" to enchant your own little boy or girl— or somebody else's youthful ditto, ditto. Your first duty at Christmastide—the Children's Festival even as much as your own, remember! —ought always to be to discharge your account with Santa Claus, that benevolent, white- whiskered, un-canonised old saint, whose very special duty, at the time of the year now so closely at hand, is concerned with the "kinder- kind "—if you will pardon the writer dropping into what is rapidly cominito be regarded as an uncivilised language; his excuse being that the people who speak the tongue in question had the good fortune to be the first discoverers of the postal address of Santa Nicholaus. Come, then, to the toyshops, where they keep inexpressible happiness, and, considering its immense value, sell it out for such trifling con- siderations. Have you never seen the rapturous sparkle in the big, blue, wide-open child-eyes; have you never heard the catch in the voice— the "three-holed clutch" effect svmphonised in the "O -o-h, dada, dear !" (or " mother, dear," or "uncle, dear," or "auntie, dear," as the case may be)? You that have, will know what to do this Christmas; you that haven't, have at least one joy to experience. All the child-hearts of Warrington turn at this time of the year to one class of shop window. Come now, father, mother; grandfather, proud grandmother, aunt and uncle—it's up to you; and a child's benisons, a something above price, is yours, as says the prosaic advertisement, "at prices ranging from one penny upwards." 'An it's toys you want, you cannot do better than try one or other—or the whole—of the following branches of the old-established firm of Santa Claus and Co., Unlimited :—Pendle- bury's, near the Central Station; or the Co- operative Stores, in Sankey-street.</p>
        <p>GIFTS FOR " HER "—OR "HIM." SERVICEABLE PRESENTS. It's a grown-up you want a present for, is it? Maybe, the present required must be of a Well, your range of selection is a wide one, and, useful and a lasting character. If so, what is even if it were narrow, you would be secure in the matter with a fur-coat—than what could be +OlOl4 4,44++ 41014 014 014 44 44 014?i4i4 014i10/1?HoD ?ll++ 4141441"H4 011i4 44 44 4014 401444,141</p>
        <p>more useful, in face of the present cold nip and the even colder ones that, our experiences of the English winter teaches us, are yet to come? A. fur coat it is? Then the addresses you want are: Messrs. Dutton's, Sankey-street,; or Messrs. W. Hodgkinson and Co.'s, Limited, Bridge-street, who can also fix you up with any other kind of drapery and millinery that you may want to wear, or to give. The Co-operative Society also have on hand a large stock—as attractive as it is large—of winter wear of all descriptions, and the Manchester Clothing Depot, 10, Horsemarket-street, offer boys' and men's overcoats in large variety—most useful gifts these! At the Grand Clothing Hall, in Bridge-street, overcoats and other welcome articles of apparel are to be had at seductive prices. •Mr C. Mounfield has a large and at- tractive stock of hats and caps adapted to all shapes and sizes of the masculine head, and, in addition; he specialises in gentlemen's under- wear, hosiery, neckwear, ladies' gloves and wraps, &amp;c., and satisfaction can be guaranteed to every purchaser in search of useful and bound-to-be-appreciated presents—either for himself, herself, or anybody else—who decides to trade with J. Banks, at his shops in Horse- market-street and Sankey-street; or at Kellett's or Boydell Bros., in Bridge-street.</p>
      </div>
