      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>War Stories : By Letter I Interview</head>
        <p>War Stories : By Letter I Interview</p>
        <p>MISS TRUDA CROSFIELD On Duty in a French Military Hospital.</p>
        <p>Miss Truda Crosfield, who is engaged in a hospital in Cherbourg, France, writing home, gives a vivid description of the class of work she is performing and the awful human wreck- age of war she witnesses daily. Miss Crosfield motors a great deal on the business of the hos- pital, and must prove herself of great value in this respect. She must have been severely tried, for she speaks of the blinding hurricanes through which she has travelled on many occa- sions lately. "I finish the beds with Mary," says Miss Crosfield, "generally by 10.30, and then spend the morning slicing carrots and onions, and, later, pears, in the ancient kitchen. Otherwise I have had two mornings in Ward ' D ' under Dr. Hancoer and Nurse Kennedy, the only spot where the orderlies can sit or do anything save their unfurnished bedrooms. After lunch, so far, I've always had to take some down town and help them with their shopping, Dr. Watts, young, clever and brusque, espe- cially. Bring them back, get teat and take down our party at nightfall. By the time the car is garaged (I garage the car as best I may) and I walk back here over the port-bridge and along the quay, it is generally six o'clock, and I am so tired with all the wind and the tension of driving in the dark that I simply can't write letters "In Ward 'D' is a youth, Louis, only 21, shot completely through the mouth and right cheek. I'm glad to find I can see these wounds without squirming at all—they have become so much worse because frequently five or six days go by before the cases can reach the hospital. . Louis is my admiration; he's hardly flinched. I turned the electric torch on that , jagged, gaping mass which was his lower cheek. ... . . . , And Miss Crosfield goes on to chat interest- ingly of the cases upon which she is engaged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>SOON PROMOTED.</head>
        <p>SOON PROMOTED.</p>
        <p>Local Footballer With the R.F.A.</p>
        <p>We have received an interesting letter from Bombardier Peter Broadhurst, of the Royal Field Artillery, who, prior to joining up, was a prominent member of the Emmanuel Insti- tute F.C., keeping goal for them, tie was also a member of the Emmanuel Church and School. After mentioning that lie has "The Exam- iner "—"which I love to read "—sent him every week, he goes on to say: "I enlisted in Cairo-street on September Ist, 1914, and the following night .I was drafted to Preston for a week, after which I was sent with a batch of five hundred others to Bulford Camp, near &amp;dignify. When drafted to Bulford I was a private; now I am glad to say I have been promoted to Bombardier. We are at it from i six o'clock n the morning until five in trie evening; manceuvring on the Plains 1$ ith the horses and gun teams We are going at it ding-dong, getting ready for the front. I am pleased to say I shall be home on Christmas furlough. . . I like the R.F.A. first-class; it's just the job to bring a fellow out We are only waiting to have a smack at the Germans. . . I hope that the men of Warrington will respond to the call to fight tor King and country, and rally round the flag." "I am only 19 years of age;" adds, the Bombardier "and I stand Mt. 9in. and weigh- /Ist. having gained a stone and a half in weight:"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>AN EXCITING RIDE.</head>
        <p>AN EXCITING RIDE.</p>
        <p>Warrington Man Who Rode Through the Snipers.</p>
        <p>Private F. Wadsworth, Royal Welsh Fusiliers, whose home is at 5, Forshaw-street, Warring- ton, where his wife and daughter are residing, was invalided home with rheumatism and is now fit for service again. He was treated at Lord Rosebery's house in Scotland, "Dalmeny," which, like so many mansions in Great Britain, has been converted into a temporary hospital, and was then given a holiday in Warrington. In civil life Private Wadsworth is a joiner and is in charge of the circular saw in the joiners' shop at the works of Rylands Brothers. During his stay in Warrington he was a fre- quent visitor to the Rylands' Recreation Club and grounds, in which he has always taken a keen interest. On one of these visits Mr. G. E. Knott, on behalf of the members, made a suit- able and kindly presentation to Private Wads- worth, who said how much he would value the gift from his "Rec." friends, who were a true lot of sportsmen. Private Wadsworth was at Mona and had the advantage of serving in the infantry regiment that sustained the least number of casualties of any regiment that took part in the retire- ment to the neighbourhood of Paris. This he attributed to good leadership and a knowledge of the country by those in command. He also took part in the advance upon Soissons, and saw a seven-mile long German convoy captured with many prisoners. My officer," said Private Wadsworth, "who had instructions on one occasion to go on ahead by motor ,car, left me with his horse and told rue to follow him. I mounted the horse, and said to myself 'Here goes!' I went full canter for four miles, passing woods from which the hidden enemy fired continuously. I got accus- tomed to the whistle of bullets and lost my „p. When I came to a French village I in- quired where our men were, and they directed me straight on.' I came to three roads and I took one by chance and came up to my officer two miles further on. Those who had tried to hit me were German snipers who had been cut off." Private Wadsworth was transferred with the</p>
        <p>British Army from the Aisne trenches to the West. This movement, carried out so success- fully under the eyes and within sound of the German trenches, he described as marvellous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>LATE MR. JOHN CROSEHILD.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. JOHN CROSEHILD.</p>
        <p>His Family's Gift of a Bust to</p>
        <p>the Town.</p>
        <p>When Mrs. John Crosfield opened the John Crosfield Memorial Hall she suggested that the members of the family and near relatives should add a bust of her late husband to the building, raised in his memory. Members of the Town Council immediately made representations to the effect that in view of the public work Mr. Crosfield did for Warrington, the bust should be placed in the Town Hall. Mr. A. H. Crosfield consulted Mrs. Crosfield, who readily assented to the proposal, and the Council unanimously agreed that the bust should be placed in the vestibule of the Town Hall, along with those of the late King Edward, the late Lord Win- marleigh, and the late Mr. George Crosfield. The well-known sculptor, Mr. Pegram, was commissioned to make the bust in white marble. Mrs. Crosfield, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, and his eld- est sister, Mrs. Ryan, have had many consulta- tions with the sculptor to help him to make a real success of what must be one of the most difficult of tasks, the creation of posthumous work from photographs. When discussing the question of the bust with Mr. A. H. Crosfield this week, that gentleman informed us that Mr. Pegram was an expert at this class of work, and, in fact, had no superior in England. "Has the sculptor made much progress?" we asked.</p>
        <p>"Well," replied Mr. Crosfield, "lie finish&amp;l. the clay model a fortnight since, and a speaking (one might say lifelike) likeness of my father he has given us—my father as he was in the full vigour of middle life. Besides the number of visits Mrs. Crosfield and I paid to Mr. Pegram, Mrs. Ryan, my eldest sister also came to the studio and gave valuable advice and help; I can assure you no pains have been spared in doing what, in every sense of the phrase, has been a labour of love."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>AN INDIAN STORY.</head>
        <p>AN INDIAN STORY.</p>
        <p>Private Wadsworth 'is' itr-o;ci of .the fact that his was the regiment selected by Lord Kitchener to march from Chakrata to Agra, in India, a distance of 250 to 270 miles, in a specified time. He also has an interesting story with a moral of a card gambling scene in India, in which a loser murdered the winner, confessed to the murder of his mother, became repentant, and on the scaffold referred his comrade to the story of the Prodigal Son, and was the direct cause of 200 soldiers being confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>THANKS TO ST. JOHN NURSES.</head>
        <p>THANKS TO ST. JOHN NURSES.</p>
        <p>Private Wadsworth wishes us to thank the members of the St. John Ambulance Nursing Division, of which Mrs. Fisher is the secretary, for the attention they have shown and the help they have given to ' soldiers coming home wounded and invalided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>BURIED ALIVE IN TRENCHES.</head>
        <p>BURIED ALIVE IN TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Who Had to be Dug Out.</p>
        <p>Rifleman G. Thorniley, of 29, Howley-lane, Warrington, the son of the late Mr. Robert Thorniley--who was a well-known Liberal in his day—returned home last week wounded, after serving in the firing line since the commence- ment of the war, when he enlisted in the King's Royal Rifles. "We first served for a fortnight at Mons," Rifleman Thorniley told "The Examiner" repre- sentative, " and there we lost a terrible num- ber, including officers. With great difficulty we proceeded from trench to trench, contending against many charges on the part of the enemy; we were then falling back into positions, under terrific shell fire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Buried Alive.</head>
        <p>Buried Alive.</p>
        <p>"We soon commenced advancing up the coun- try, the shell fire continuing. One day a shell burst over the trench I was in, and blew in all the side, burying the lot of us. We had to be dug out by our comrades, but were little the worse for our experience, for we went on fight- ing just the same, gradually driving the enemy out of their positions, and digging our trenches as we went along. "Save the military, there was never anybody in the neighbourhood. All the houses had been blown away, and dead boclies lay all over the country—l have seen thsm used for cover. Nobody could describe the place; it is nothing to see a man with his head, or arms, or legs blown off. But what upset me most was to see the women and children driven out of their homes everywhere the firing took place. The Germans usually gave them about two hours to get away in, and, if they had not left their homes behind by that time, they were shelled out. They wandered about the country, and slept any- where they could along the roadsides. The children wondered what it all meant!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Snipers.</head>
        <p>The Snipers.</p>
        <p>. - "The British soldiers' only fear was of the snipers, who were stationed everywhere round about the trenches. If one of us put his hand above the trench it would be shot off immedi- ately, and many of our men were shot in the trenches. The snipers fired at our faces when we put them to the loopholes. And all the time shells were bursting overhead, and we had to keep throwing ourselves under the sides of the trenches. On one occasion a piece of shrapnel knocked my rifle out of my hand, and yet never hit me. Between the shells and the snipers, men were falling every minute. It made us very careful,, I can tell you!</p>
        <p>" What's for Dinner?"</p>
        <p>..... " There was plenty of good food for us if only it could be 'got through' to the trenches, but it could not, although some of UR volunteered to try to bring it during the night-time. The conversation got something like this 'What's for breakfast this morning?' 'Rifle shotT 'What's for dinner?' Jack Johnsons!' 'What's for tea?" Shrapnel !" Well, then, supper?' 'Stand to!' "I received my wound at Ypres, when we had been there two days. At night I went out with a scouting party, and a sniper's bullet—a dum- dum—hit me in the back, and, after exploding, embedded itself in a bone. There was no more fighting for me, then."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>WITHIN AN ACE OF DEATH. Warrington Private Tells of Narrow Escapes.</head>
        <p>WITHIN AN ACE OF DEATH. Warrington Private Tells of Narrow Escapes.</p>
        <p>Three remarkable escapes from death which he has experienced, are described by Private Andrew Topham—whose father, the late Ser- geant Topham, of Warrington, saw 23 years' service in the 3rd Battalion of the South Lanca- shire Regiment—in a letter written to his brother (Private Arthur Topham) and Ms sister. The gallant private says that on one occasion piece of a shell struck him below the temple, with the result that his right eye "was blown clean out," and he adds: "The piece travelled from my temple into my mouth, blowing some of my teeth down my throat, and causing me to have a fractured jaw."' Private Topham was with the First Brigade, and was in almost every engagement from Mons to October 27th. Once while at Vendresse, a " Jack Johnson" struck a pine tree at the head i of the trench in which the private lay. Had t not been for that tree, he says, he would have been blown to pieces. At Dixmude Private Topham bad another narrow escape. He was on look-out duty, when he "got bowled over like a log."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>COME ON, YOUNG MEN! Join Up at Once and Help to End the War Boon.</head>
        <p>COME ON, YOUNG MEN! Join Up at Once and Help to End the War Boon.</p>
        <p>"We were over 16 days in the cold, wet trenches at a stretch. If we had more recruits there would be no need for us to stay in for so long at once." Such, is the declaration of a wounded soldier who is now at Raddon Court Hospital, and it is up to the young men of the country to respond to that call from the trenches and join the Forces at once. All branches of the service are now open for ordinary enlist- ment, and recruits will be accepted for cavalry if they have had previous experience in mounted corps. Amongst the men who are especially wanted are motor drivers and wireless opera- tors for the Royal Flying Corps (in which the pay is ss. per day), men for the R.A.M.C. and Foot Guards, and tradesmen and drivers for the Royal Engineers. "The more men we send, the sooner the war will be over," so young men of Warrington, answer your country's call and join any branch of the service at once at the Central Police Buildings or Orford Barracks. FOUR HUNDRED—BUT MORE WANTED!, There are now over nine hundred men en- listed in the 4th Reserve Battalion of the South Lancashire Prince of Wales' Volunteers. More and more, however, are urgently needed. RECRUITS FROM THE WORKS.</p>
        <p>_ During the week another dozen men from the Whiteeross Works. have "joined up," whilst five more have gone from Messrs. Crosfield's.</p>
        <p>"A PERFECT, GENTLE KNIGHT."</p>
        <p>Rector's Panegyric of the Late Major Appleton.</p>
        <p>IMPRESSIVE MEMORIAL SERVICE.</p>
        <p>A knight there was, and that a worthy man;. That from the time that he first began To ride out, he loved chivalry, Truth and honour, freedom and courtesy Full worthy was he.</p>
        <p>..... . . And though he was worthy, he was wise, And of his port as meek as is a maid.</p>
        <p>He was a very perfect, gentle knight.</p>
        <p>In these words, quoted from wise old Chaucer,. father of English poesy, did the Rector of War- rington (the Rev. Canon Willis) pay part of his warm, sympathetic tribute to the memory of a gallant warrior—Major Francis Martin Appleton—at a special memorial service held in the Parish Church on Sunday morning. The Major, it will be remembered, was accidentally shot through the head in Tunbridge Wells, at which place the Warrington Territorials, in, which he was the senior major, are at present btationed. .</p>
        <p>The Rector found his text in St. Luke vii. 4, 5 and 6: "And when they came to Jesus, they besought Him instantly, saying: That he was worthy for whom He should do this: for ho loveth our nation, and he has built us a synw. gogue. Then Jesus went with them. And when He was not far from the house, the centurion sent friends to Him, saying unto Him, Lord trouble not Thyself: for I am not worthy that Thou shouldest enter under my roof."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>OUR WOUNDED GUESTS. Preparing for Christmas at Raddon Court.</head>
        <p>OUR WOUNDED GUESTS. Preparing for Christmas at Raddon Court.</p>
        <p>The wounded soldiers at Raddon Court Tem- porary Hospital are going to have as joyous a time as their hurts and other circumstances permit this Christmas, for concerts have been arranged for them, and, in addition, the nurses are going to organise an entertainment. There have been four new patients admitted during the week, and yesterday morning the only mem- ber of the Liverpool Scottish who has been treated at the hospital was discharged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>SAILORS' MUSIC FOR SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>SAILORS' MUSIC FOR SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday two cadets from H.M.S. Conway paid a visit to the wounded soldiers lying at Racklon Court and ,entertained them with a number of musical selections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <p>LOCAL POLITICS: IS TIIERE PEACE?</p>
        <p>It is to be hoped that we in Warring- ton do ndt stand in danger of reaching that undesirable point at which we shall be crying Peace when there is no peace. Throughout the country, throughout the Empire, throughout the civilised world there has been a strong desire to close up all ranks against the attack that is being made upon the existence of civilisation. Opponents have forgotten their points of difference, controversy has ceased, party leaders have been competing with each other to show the sacrifices they can make in order that the nation's forces may be consolidated, and there may be no loophole provided for the benefit of the enemy. For the most part, these arrangements have been carried out in a thoroughly dis- interested manner; it has been recog- nised that for any one party to push its own welfare under cover of the truce would be as undesirable as for an army in the field to consolidate a posi- tion under cover of an armistice.</p>
        <p>There was a great deal of talk at the HOTTOMLEY recruiting meeting about the • hush of party controversy, Nit why, may we ask, was the MAYon, the KING'S representative in Warrington, not in the chair, and why was Mr. HAROLD SMITH, the Conservative M.P., in the chair? Why, on the huge placards caning the meeting, was the name of Mr. SMITH ill large type air] that of Mr. ARTHUR CROSFIELD in smad characters? Why was Mr. BOTTOMLEV asked to attend the Meeting and why has the meeting which LORD HERM' promised to attend been abandoned? Why-7----but we will not, in the Cir- cumstances, persue our inquiries. We have purposely refrained from askl.ng similar questions for the past few months, hut we think it necessary to sound a warning note in order that those whom it may concern may know that any attempts that may be made to reap, quietly, political capital out of a national trouble will- be noted by the people with practical effect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>NEXT ELECTION.</head>
        <p>THE NEXT ELECTION.</p>
        <p>It is also necessary, even in this time Of truce, to make a point clear with reference to the next General Election. Again we regret to. intervene, but it is essential there should be a clear under- standing, and no possibility of any- thing happening that is, not intended merely because it is expected of us to be quiescent. At the BOTTOMLEY meet- ing, Mr. HAROLD SMITE!, who very pro- perly referred with much satisfaction to the way all parties have combined in face of .the common foe, made use of the following remarkable sentence:</p>
        <p>"Mr. CROSFIELD and he were the representatives of the two greatest Political .parties in. Warrington, and he asked them to believe him when he said that he spoke not only for himself, but also for Mr. CROSFIELD, when he said that no word of party controversy would ever pass their lips until this country's representatives had signed the peace of Europe in Berlin." The life of this Parliament comes to an end next year. The war may last longer than this Parliament. If there is to be no controversy till Peace is signed, and the Election comes before the Peace, what then? Some have interpreted Mr. SMITH'S remarks to mean that if an Election takes place before Peace comes, the truce will cover a walk-over for him into tlie next Parliament. If that is what Mr. SMITH thinks, and intended to imply, he is labouring under a 'misapprehension. , The • Liberal party :will contest War- rington at the next Election with all the will and with all the strength that they possess. To say why would be to enter into party politics, which we refrain from doing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>PROPOSED NEV FREEMEN.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED NEV FREEMEN.</p>
        <p>Last month we asked that an ill- conceived plan which had been placed suddenly before the General Pui poses Committee of making additional Free- men of the Borough upon what we characterised as a • wholesale scale should be left in abeyance until after the war. For a time the plan appeared to have been abandoned, but it seems that the promoters have succeeded in getting a sufficient number of mem- bers of the Council to sign a requisi- tion to the MAYOR to call a special meeting of the Council to consider the conferring of this great honour upon six gentlemen, whose names are set out in the notice which we reproduce to-day. Before the names were made public we protested against this step being taken at the present time, and the official publication of the names now would throw, as it is intended to throw, an unfair task upon an adverse critic. However, the argumenis we used still apply, and there remains nothing more to be said except this: That the proposal has caused a great amount of dissent in the Council, because of the Way in which it _is brought forward and the time at which it is brought forward—an unfortunate state of things when honours are being bestowed.</p>
        <p>THE GERMAN RAID. The German Naval raid on the East Coast towns is a sad affair, judged by the loss of life entailed, but it will be a dear exploit for the apostles of "fright• fulness." Berlin, which speaks of bom- barding "the fortress of Scarborough," has bedecked her streets, so that we what little things bring joy have first-hand knowledge now of to the hearts of our disconsolate enemy. The incident will be the best recruiting §timulus we have yet had, and it will confirm the opinion of America and the world that we are dealing with vulgar murderers and not with warriors. ,</p>
        <p>PERFECTLY healthy children are generally happy; but a very slight stomach trouble is often enough to make them miserable. • That is the time to try a Steedman's Powder. You will find that it acts like magic. Steedman's Powders contain no poison or narcotic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>ESTIMABLE TRAITS IN THE MAJOR'S CHARACTER.</head>
        <p>ESTIMABLE TRAITS IN THE MAJOR'S CHARACTER.</p>
        <p>He was sure, the preacher said, thartfley could all see why he had chosen that text. He wanted' to speak to them of one who was in the minds and hearts of them. all, and he was going to think of him, and ask them to think of him, in three capacities—as a man, as a Churchman, and as a soldier. He had tried to think of the chief characteristics of the late Major; as he had known him for a quarter of a century, and. the first thing that came to his mind was the (lead soldier's purity; nobody could have looked in his face without feeling that he was a pure man—and the word in the Greek of the Scrip- tures, which was translated " pure," was a very striking one, which meant: One who was filled with the love of God." The second feature in the late warrior's character was his sincerity; all who knew him would agree that he was per- fectly sincere and genuine. The third feature was his humility. Another light in which they might regard the Major was that of a Churchman. They had no doubt read in the papers how that he used to attend that church, and it was an true; he loved the Parish Church. But it was not because the Major came to that church that he (the preacher) wanted them to think of him, but because he was an understanding and apprecia- tive Churchman. Tfe knew why he should be a Churchman; he had been thoroughly well- taught, thoroughly well instructed, and his fidelity to the Church was a strongly-mark cd trait in his character. There was nothing cc show or ostentation about his profession as a Chu :(11137111; people could never have said .:het his churchgoing was a piece of form, a cere- mony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>"JUST AS A SOLDIER SHOULD BE."</head>
        <p>"JUST AS A SOLDIER SHOULD BE."</p>
        <p>Think of him as a soldier! He was always so neat and quick and smart; so alert—just as a soldier should be—in the discharge of duty. He was so ready, so punctual, so dependable. He knew bow to obey, and, so, he knew how to command; but he did not command with sever- ity, but always with love. He (the preacher) was in Germany some few years ago. It was at the time of the autumn mameuvres. Crowds were gathered to watch the soldiery, and the Kaiser was expected. Presently he saw an officer go up to a soldier. Apparently he was not satisfied with the man's dress, so be struck him in the face and tore his coat from top to bottom. He (the preacher) recounted this inci- dent at the Barracks, and he ventured the re- mark that he did not think any English soldier would have stood that treatment, whereupon the dead Major remarked, "I don't think an English officer would have done sixch a thing !" No, that was not the way English officers treated their men. They treated -them as com- rades, as men ! They looked after them int' did not march them to battle with revol7rs behind them!</p>
        <p>During the service the hymns "Let saints on earth," "Fight the good fight," and Brief life is here our portion," were sung, and at the close the choir rendered the Russian Contakion of the Departed, and the organist (Mr. F. l. Darby) played the Dead March in "Saul."</p>
        <p>A TRIBUTE FROM MANHOOD AND YOUTH The service was attended by a large number of members of local military bodies and of the boys' organisations, in which the late Major was so warmly interested. Men and boys marched in processional order from the Town Hall—fronting which they wear marshalled—to the Parish Church, the Band of the Blue Coat School (conducted by Mr. Millett) leading the way. Following :came Lieutenant C. L. Wil- liamson, representing the local Battalion of Ter- ritorials, members of the National Reserve Guard (under Lieutenant Taylor), members of the Warrington and District Preparatory Training Corps (under Sergeant-Major Sulli- van), a contingent of ex-Volunteers who served with the late Major in the South African War, a number of representatives of the Warrington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade (under Supt. Baldry), the Warrington Battalion of the Church Lads Brigade (commanded by Major J. A. Brookes, with whom was Cieut. Bunch), the Ist, 2nd and 3rd Warrington Troops of Boy Scouts (under Scoutmasters Bennett, Young and Binks, respectively), the Grappen- hall Scouts (under Scoutmaster Edwards), and a detachment of the Free Church Boys' Brigade (under Lieut. Jones). The congregation included the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Peacock), Alder- man Bennett, Councillors Craik and Locker, Mr. and Airs. A. 11. Crosfield, Lieutenant Wilt- cocks (South Lancashire Regiment), Messrs. J. H. Smethurst and H. Collin (representing the Infirmary), E. Rose -(Blue Coat School), and G. Mather.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the service the proces- sionists were drawn up in line in front of the church and stood at the salute whilst the buglers. • of the Church Lads' Brigade and Boy Scouts sounded the "Last Post."</p>
        <p>AN INSPIRING' PERSONALITY. The procession was then re-formed, and wended its way back to the Town Hall, where, before dispersing, they joined in singing the, National Anthem, to the accompaniment of the Blue Coat School Band. At the invitation of the Mayor, the officers, Scoutmasters, and several other townspeople partook of light refreshment in the Mayor'S Parlour. Addressing the officers and Scout- masters, The Mayor said he was glad of the opportunity' of expressing to them his deep sympathy on the loss of their distinguished officer and dear friend. He ha dl known Major Appleton for many years and could appreciate, to some ex- tent, how great their loss had been. The town also, by the death of Major Appleton, had lost a worthy citizen. He tcusted that this particu- lar work would not be allowed to suffer, brt that others would come forward and help to carry on these excellent movements. Mr. A. H. Crosfic/d said Major Appleton we a man of his own day and generation, and o' dear friend of his. His friendship was an le- spiration, and to know him was to love hiw• It had been very truly said that he was "everr body's friend, and beloved by all who knew , him." Lieutenant Williamson, as a brother officer of Major Appleton, thanked the Mayor for having called them together in that !way, and for ill kind and sympathetic manner he had addresse them. Major Brookes, speaking for the Church La,- , Brigade, said the loss the local Battalion Witt; sustained in the death of their dear friend a'lll chief was inestimable. He desired to thank, officers, Scoutmasters and members of etr.leo organisations for their response to the invitat:t to join the memorial service. It was a ra as. sympathetic and friendly -act, and as such lilt, deeply appreciated by all members ef C.L.B. Mr. J. B. Starkey, for the Scouts, joined, otoa 19° in the thanks to the Mayor. Major Alqeoe was a dear friend wad fellow worker, anu was loss to the youth aril manhood. of the tor% incalculable. • ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S EXAMINER."</p>
        <p>Christmas Number Will Be Published</p>
        <p>On Thursday.</p>
        <p>" The Examiner" next week will be published on Thursday, instead of Saturday, Friday being Christmas Day, and Saturday Bank Holiday. We shall give, in addition to the local news of the week and the usual features, the following special Christmas reading matter:</p>
        <p>"A CHRISTMAS COLT.F.CTION." By G. B. Burgin. "THE GRAYSIIAMS' CHRISTMAS." By May Wynne. "TWO CHRISTMAS EVES." By J. J. BELL And CHRISTMAS CONUNDRUMS.</p>
        <p>"THE EXAMINER" will be obtainable next THURSDAY MORNING, just as if Thurs- day were Saturday; and will provide the best indoor entertainment during the holidays.</p>
        <p>Christmas Toys for Poor "Kiddies." The dolls and toys which have been given for the poor children of Warrington will be on ex- hibition in the Parr Hall on Wednesday morn- ing, and the Mayor will distribute them in the afternoon.</p>
        <p>For Dr. Barnardo's "Family." The recent house-to-house collection on behalf of Dr. Barnardo's Homes realised the sum of £l3 18s., and the directors desire to thank all who so kindly helped to bring about such a satisfactory result.</p>
        <p>Gifts from Needlework Guild. This week we are asked to announce, with thanks, the receipt of parcels of clothing, &amp;c., from the Warrington Needlework Guild by the Matron of the Infirmary; Mr. Laws, the Police Court Missionary; and the Wycliffe Con- gregational Church.</p>
        <p>Musical Service at Bold-street. In our advertisement columns will be 'found the details of the special services to be held in Bold-street Wesleyan Church to-morrow. Several well-known local vocalists will contri- bute items at a musical service to be held in the afternoon.</p>
        <p>"Messiah" at Wycliffe. The " Messiah" will be rendered by an aug- mented choir and orchestra at a special Christ- mas Choral Service to be held in Wycliffe Congregational Church to-morrow evening. On Christmas Day the Rev. T. T. James will preach on "The Message of Incarnation."</p>
        <p>Slav v. Teuton. The Philomathic Society discussion on the above question will appear in next week's "Examiner."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Shorthand Writers.</head>
        <p>Shorthand Writers.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Warrington Short- hand Writers' Association a highly successful social was held on Friday evening of last week. A vote of thanks to Alias Ellison, who had the arrangements in hand for the entertaining of the members and their friends, brought the en- joyable gathering to a close.</p>
        <p>Musical Successes.</p>
        <p>At the recent examinations held in Liverpool, under the auspices of the London College of Music, the following young students were suc- cessful in passing, with honours, the piano playing tests: Eva Whitby (advanced senior), Cissie Leigh, May Ward and Ronald Charnock (elementary). They gained, respectively, 85, 98, 91 and 86 marks.</p>
        <p>Mayor and Mayoress's Christmas</p>
        <p>Wishes.</p>
        <p>The Mayor and Mayoress wish the inhabi- tants of Warrington a very happy Christmas, and extend to all those who have suffered through the war their sincere sympathy, and they confidently hope that the coming year may witness the success of our arms and bring to all the blessings of a lasting peace.</p>
        <p>Rich Harvest from Nightingale Lecture The lecture on Florence Nightingale, so enter- tainingly given last week by Mrs. Ridgway in aid of Haddon Court Hospital, and so success- fully organised by Miss Mary Gornall, resulted in a nett sum of £4l ss. being realised. That amount was, on Wednesday, handed over to the treasurer of the hospital, the Rev. G. C. Briggs. The bouquet presented to Mrs. Ridgway at the Parr Hall by the committee was handed to her by Miss Vera Gornall.</p>
        <p>Increased Infirmary Accommodation. The Board of Management of the Infirmary have decided to furnish and open early in the New Year the top ward in the extension that has, so far, not been used. This has been ren- dered necessary by , the large number of wounded soldiers that are now being received at the Institution. The special war fund that has been opened to raise .R 1,500 a year's extra expenditure, now stands at £731. The list appears in our advertisement columns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Thirty-two Births; Twenty-nine Deaths.</head>
        <p>Thirty-two Births; Twenty-nine Deaths.</p>
        <p>In his weekly report the Medical Officer of Health (Dr. G. W. N. Joseph) states that dur- ing the seven days ended December 12th, 32 births were registered and 29 deaths, as against 28 births and 24 deaths in the corresponding period of last year. Of the deaths, 13 were those of persons between the ages of 25 and 65. The patients in the Borough Hospital num- bered 127. four of whom were suffering from enteric fever and six from diphtheria.</p>
        <p>Wanted: Workers in the Mission Field At the Warrington Parish Church on Wed- nesday evening, the Rev. Edwin H. Day (organ- ising secretary of the Society for the Propaga- tion of the Gospel in the Dioceses of Chester, Liverpool, and St. Asaph) delivered the third of a series of missionary addresses. Taking for his text Acts xvii. 27: " That they should seek the Lord, if haply they might feel after Him, and find Him, though He be not far from every one of us," the preacher said that though in a large number of the Mission Fields there was good work going on, there were still a large number of workers wanted.</p>
        <p>Local Pastor's Farewell. There was a large congregation at the Latch- ford Baptist Church on Sunday morning, when the Warrington Battalion of the Free Church Boys' Brigade held their quarterly rally. The Rev. J. S. Chedburn (pastor) occupied the pul- pit; and Private C. D. Smith, of "A" Company, ably rendered the solo "While the Days are Going By."—Referring to his approaching de- parture from the town, the preacher said he Wished to take that opportunity of saying good- bye tdthe members of the Warrington Battalion of the Boys' Brigade. To the officers his last words were "Don't give in !" He knew only too well the difficulties which confronted them in work of the character they were doing; but no good influence, which was brought to bear Upon a young life, would be wasted. The boys themselves must. realise that, by doing their duty to the Brigade, they would derive health, benefit and blessing from their association with it. The movement had Come to effect a large Purpose, and to stay.</p>
        <p>The Leather Trade. " There seemed," says the "Leather Trades Review," "to be a slackening of interest in business last week, and inquiry was hardly as brisk as usual, but all indications are towards a strong renewal of business immediately after the holidays and stocktaking. In the mean- time there is no diminution in the volume of output except such as is imposed by the Weather, as deliveries of all kinds are very Urgently required, the only complaint met with nowadays being of non-deliveries. It would be difficult to imagine the position if the home loarket were active, for it seems likely that so long as the military demand lasts the supply Will lag far behind requirements. Prices are fairly steady, chiefly because very little forward business is being done, but there is little doubt that any increase of inquiry will cause a fur- ther advance, and the general opinion seems to bo that the New Year will furnish some serious "1-prises in this direction Raw sup- are rather more plentiful, tho virtual lolpoval of the various continental embargoes 'lrtging larger supplies on the market, but %lac witlintramokegtict QA priceti.".</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Pastor's Farewell.</head>
        <p>A Pastor's Farewell.</p>
        <p>Farewell sermons will be preached at the Latchford Baptist Church to-morrow by the Rev. J. S. Chedburn.</p>
        <p>National Reservists Sup Together. At the Winmarleigh Cafe on Tuesday even- ing, Lieutenant Taylor gave a greatly appre- ciated hot-pot supper to the members of his company of the National Reserve.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Carol Service.</head>
        <p>Carol Service.</p>
        <p>A carol service will be held in the Legh- street Primitive Methodist Church to-morrow eveaing, when the soloists will be Miss Jessie Woodland and Mr. J. Dakin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Scarlet Fever Still Abroad.</head>
        <p>Scarlet Fever Still Abroad.</p>
        <p>During the week ended December 12th, 34 fresh cases of scarlet fever were notified, mak- ing a total of 96 cases under treatment. Six cases of phthisis were also reported in the same period.</p>
        <p>Band of Hope Concert. In connection with the Christ Church, Latch- ford, Band of Hope a capital concert was given on Friday evening of last week.—Mr. W. R. Lowes (superintendent) presided, and several entertaining items were given by the members. Mr. Mockett made an efficient accompanist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Lieutenant-Colonel Green's Will.</head>
        <p>Lieutenant-Colonel Green's Will.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant-Colonel Malcolm C. A. Green, of the South Lancashire Regiment, and formerly of The Glen, Latchford, who was killed in action at Ypres on November 17th last, left unsettled personal estate of the net value of .C 2,382.</p>
        <p>Priory School Music Success. In the R.A.M. and R.C.M. examinations re- cently held at Liverpool, Vera Nicholls, a pupil at the Priory School, Warrington, was success- ful in passing the primary division for piano- forte playing.</p>
        <p>A Deserter's Explanation. John Horan, of the 273rd Battery, Royal Field Artillery, was charged—at the Borough Police Court on Thursday—with deserting on Decem- ber 9th. He said he had never meant to desert, but was very ill at the time.—He was remanded to await an escort.</p>
        <p>£5O Bequest for Nursing Association. The Honorary Treasurer (Mr. H. Roberts) gratefully acknowledges the sum of £5O from Mr. J. W. Knowles, solicitor to the executors the same being a bequest by the late Mr. Ed- ward Baker, a former respected workman of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons. Ltd., in appreciation of the services of the Nursing Staff rendered during his last illness.</p>
        <p>Forthcoming Marriage. A marriage has been arranged, and will take place early in the New Year, between Mr. Donald Gladding, 1.C.5., Bengal, eldest son of the Rev. Thomas Gladding, of Ashton-under- Lyne, and formerly of Padgate, and Miss Evelyn Avis McArthur, youngest daughter of the late Colonel A. D. McArthur, of the Royal Engineers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Mr. Harold Smith Leaves the Press Bureau.</head>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith Leaves the Press Bureau.</p>
        <p>Sir Frank Swettenham, G.C.M.G., and Sir E. T. Cook have been appointed assistant directors of the Official Press Bureau, and Mr. R. P. Hills, assistant secretary, has been ap- pointed secretary of the Bureau in succession to Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., who has resigned in order to take up his duties under the Ad- miralty.</p>
        <p>Christmas Gifts for Young Co-operators. No fewer than 3,500 children of local co- operators have had an enjoyable time at the Co-operative Hall this week, for on Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday evenings a cinematograph exhibition was given, and each child who has attended received a present from a huge Christmas tree.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>" Messiah " at Legh-street.</head>
        <p>" Messiah " at Legh-street.</p>
        <p>At the Legh-atreet Pri—mitive Methodist Church on Sunday excellent performances of Handel's " Messiah " were given. The Pastor (the Rev. A. J. Chessum) occupied the pulpit, and delivered brief and suitable addresses. Mr. F. H. Banner presided at the organ, and Mr. A. Spruce conducted. The soloists were : Mesdames C. Worthington and A. E. Hoyle, Misses Jessie Woodland and Maud Jones, and Messrs. W. J. Booth and E. H. Pearce.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>AVarrington Market.</head>
        <p>AVarrington Market.</p>
        <p>The prices ruling In the Warrington Market on Wednesday were :—Wheat, 6s. to 6s. 3d. per 7011 i.; oats, 3s. 6d. to 3s. Bd. per 451 b. Wheat markets are firm and further advances are probable. Oats are slightly easier in price, and the demand is not so brisk. Wheat and oat straw remain normal, at prices ranging from 21s. to 225. 6d. per ton f.o.r. Flour is firmer; an advance of Is. per sack of 280Ib. being put on; whilst there are indications of further advance. Meadow hay fetched .X 2 10s. per ton, and clover hay £3 to .E 3 ss. per ton, f.o.r. For War Sufferers. Very successful entertainments were given, in the Cairo-street Schoolroom on Tuesday and Wednesday evenings, by the girls of the Priory School, the proceeds being in aid of the local Relief and Belgian Refugee Funds. On each night the hall was well filled, and the substan- tial amount of £l3 ss. has been forwarded to the funds mentioned. The programme in- cluded a little French play, "Le Petit Chaperon Rouge" ("Red Riding Hood"), and the operetta, "Robin Hood and Maid Marian." The limelight was kindly supplied by Mr. Hill. The Belgian refugees from Penketh attended the first performance, and those from St. Aus- tin's the second. The Misses C. Hitchens and M. Woodward were mainly responsible for training the children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Holiday Arrangements.</head>
        <p>Holiday Arrangements.</p>
        <p>Next Wednesday and Thursday the final clear- ance at the Post Office will be at 9.30 p.m., and the town letter boxes at 6 p.m. Christmas Day the hours at the Post Office will be as on Sun- days.</p>
        <p>On Christmas Eve the tram and 'bus service will be as on Saturdays, and on Christmas Day will commence at 2 p.m. and finish at the same time as on Sundays. The early work- men's cars will not run on Boxing Day.</p>
        <p>A special market will be held -on Christmas Eve, in addition to that usually held on Wednes- days. The markets will, however, be closed all day on Boxing Day. The members of the local Grocers' and Provi- sion Dealers' Association have decided to close their shops all Christmas Day and Boxing Day, and also all New Year's Day. They will, how- ever, remain open all day on Thursdays, Decem- ber 24th and 31st,</p>
        <p>Special Constables and Escaped Lunatic. It was reported to Mr. Haslam (who is the head of the special 'constables in the Helsby district), at 8.15 on Tuesday evening, that a lunatic had escaped from Chester, and might be passing through Helsby on his way to Kingsley. Mr. Haslam at once gave instruc- tions for all the men of No. 2 Section to be immediately called out, and, at the same time, personally called out as many men of Section No. 1 as he could get in touch with, and in- structed them to watch the upper roads. It reflects great credit on the special constables that within half an hour 30 men were patrol- ling the roads and the lunatic was found and captured, before 9.30 p.m., near the pumping station. Mr. Haslam also notified Mr. Bowers, of Frodsham, and Mr. Greenway, of Alvanley, in case the man should have passed Helsby. Sergeant Postons, of the Cheshire Constabulary, who had been cycling along the roads in search of the man, was present, and assisted in the capture, and conveyed the lunatic to Frodsham.</p>
        <p>• The man who has a grudge against the uni- verse always finds all the cactus bunches there are</p>
      </div>
