      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 19, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 19, 1914.</p>
        <p>trio of part songs by the Male Voice Choir came</p>
        <p>next. First, they gave Walford Davies' moving "Hymn before action," following this with two Elgarian trifles—both of them daintily conceived and delightfully renderd—" It's 0! to be a wild wind," and "Feasting, I watch."</p>
        <p>Crosfield's splendid Band, under the baton of Mr. J. C. Taylor, brought the programme to a close with "The Dance of the IIDOMP8" from Grieg's brilliant "Peer Gynt " suite, and a popular fantasia, "Memories of Britain "—an attractive arrangement Jf airs from th•a four united kingdoms. The Grieg selection, with its weird suggestion of the v.rorld of the impish " little men," proved specially attractive, and it was given most effectively. It should be mentioned that "The Dance of the Gnomes" was arranged by Mr. W. Belli- xell, who is so closely associated -with al band's successes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>CROSFIELD-BELGIAN CONCERT.</head>
        <p>CROSFIELD-BELGIAN CONCERT.</p>
        <p>Great Audience Assists the Distress Funds.</p>
        <p>MRS. CROSFIELD'S TRIUMPH.</p>
        <p>The reverberating boom—b-o-o-o-m of huge guns, awakening devilish echoes and setting them a-clattering with a myriad tongues the tale of woe and destruction—the sharp, venom- ous, spiteful c-r-r-r-ack of the death-dealing rifles—the harsh, metallic, hoof-beats of hosts of galloping horsemen, with a softer accompani- ment of jingling spurs and accoutrements—the persistent, rythmic tramp, tramp, tramp of thousands of feet—the pitiful cries of hordes of hungry, homeless, innocent women and children, fleeing hither, fleeing thither, fleeing anywhere, everywhere, out of reach of the Unspeakable Terror pressing, ever pressing, on from behind, by day warming its blood-guilty hands at blazing fires that once were happy, smiling homesteads; at night lighting its foul, unholy way with gigantic pyres that before the present man-made and man-fed holocaust of rapine, incendiarism and horror were prosper- ous towns—some hoary with age and world- famous for the architectural beauties wrought in them by the wonderful craftsmen of the golden age of the guilds, the age when men worked with a pride rather than with a gold lust—all this—maybe, in nebulous form, but still there !—was at the back of the thoughts of the thronged audience who assembled in the Parr Hall on Saturday evening to pay a meed of their tribute of appreciation to the heroism of the tiny Belgian nation, who have borne so much of the brunt—the rude, relentless shock, the crushing horror, the stress, the uni3peakable misery, the diabolically-inflicted suffering inci- dental to this world-shaking war. The occasion which brought this big, sympa- thetic crowd together was a grand patriotic concert organised by Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Cros- , field, under the auspices and with the invalu- able assistance of the Infirmary Working Men's Committee, in aid of the Belgian Refugees' Fund, a concert that, both from the artistic and financial points of view, was an unqualified success, the promoters having called to their aid the services of the best local talent avail- able—vocal and instrumental—whilst also con- tributing to the programme themselves, in addi- tion to defraying the whole of the expenses. The audience was one of the largest that ever thronged Parr Hall, but it was not a bit bigger than the excellence of the programme, and the splendid cause in which it was given, war- ranted.</p>
        <p>Most appropriate was the first item in the long programme—an orchestral arrangement of the national anthems of the Allies, including the quaintly fascinating song of that sturdy little Eastern race, the Japanese, and the war song of those dauntless fighters from west of the Balkans—the Servians—who had hardly extri- cated themselves, victoriously, from one devas- tating war ere they found themselves plunged into another, and one even more terrible. Par- enthetically, it may be noted that the Japanese and the Servian National Anthems had been specially scored for this occasion by Mr. Arthur Down. After the national songs came a spirited rendering of Suppe's well-known overture, "Light Cavalry "—a composition electric with the suggestion of armed horsemen; to which the Musical Society's Orchestra did ample justice, well meriting the outburst of applause which was their reward at the hands of the audience. Mr. J. B. Poole followed with the prologue to Leoncavallo's tragic opera "I Pagliacci." His treatment of the classic recitative was in every respect admirable, and fully merited the hearty reception accorded it. Miss Marie Houghton next gave the audience a rich vocal treat— " Lusinghe pin care," from Handel's "Ales- sandro." Her splendid voice, allied to her cul- tured treatment, brought out to the full the seductiveness of the passionate theme, and to those who only know Handel as the austere I composer of oratorio, this number was something in the nature of a revelation. An encore was inevitable, and in response to the vociferous demands, Miss Houghton gave the audience music made in our own homeland—" The Rosary," with its ear-haunting rythym, redolent of the suggestion of cloistered convent and the life of pious devotion. In quite another vein was Mr. J. Babbitt's "Song of Britain," with its lilting choruses—to which, as also to the verses, the singer did ample justice, catching completely the infectious spirit of the words: Scotland, Ireland, Wales and Old England, , These be your homeland, oh, sons of the main; Staunch is your story, and Great is your glory, and</p>
        <p>Hold fast that glory, your honour maintain! Again, an encore was inevitable, and Mr. Rabbitt obliged with "An English Rose," a charming little ballad from Edward Ger- man's " Merrie England." The next item proved one of the biggest of the many big treats of the evening—a rendition of Elgar's setting of "The Reveille," by the Warrington Male Voice Choir, a composition of which it is true to say they have now made themselves masters. Under the baton of Mr. J. H. Brazendale, and with voices that blended to perfection, the choir brought out to the full the merits of this well-known composition of one of Britain's master musi- cians.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Crosfield's appearance upon the platform was the signal for a spontaneous outburst of welcoming applause and of recognition of the important part she was to take in the concert. Her performance of Chopin's E flat Polonaise, for piano and orchestra, with its touching intro- ductory slow movement, may be fairly regarded as the outstanding feature of the programme. In its interpretation one saw that the tempera- mental qualities of vivaciousness, allied with a high degree of emotionalism, which Mrs. Cros- field possesses in full measure, enabled her to deal in an exceptionally able manner with the contrasted effects of these two movements. As an example of finished technique, and of a style ih which the personal equation of charm and ingenuous ardour was largely In evidence, Mrs. Crosfield's playing is entitled to high praise, and a discriminating audience was not slow to pass upon it the commentary of enthusiastic ap- plause. A word of appreciation should be said of the unobtrusive way in which the orchestral parts to the polonaise were performed under the guidance of Mr. Crossley's baton. In response to a vociferous recall, Mrs. Crosfield gave with facile grace and expression a further example of her skill as a piano exponent. The Musical Society's Orchestra, under the able conductorship of Mr. Frank H. Crossley, opened the second half of the programme with a pot-pourri of nautical airs, ranging from the rollicking "Life on the Ocean Wave" to the sombrely effective "Death of Nelson." The selection proved very popui.tr, and deservedly so, for not only did it embrace all the ear- haunting sea ditties, so clamant in their appeal to the sons and daughters of the Mistress of the Seas, but it was also splendidly played. Mr. J. Rabbitt then sang " The Trumpeter," in which his fine voice was heard, as it ever is, to the fullest advantage, the general effect of a most effective song being heightened by the trumpet obligato, played by Mr. Blackburne.</p>
        <p>Mae ElEach treated" the aid ence to a most sympathetic rendering of Beethoven's "Romance in 17," which afforded her splendid scope for dis- playing her powers as a violin executant, and so far delighted the audience that they could not be denied some further exhibition of her skill. "Land of Hope and Glory" (Eiger), as sung by Miss Marie Houghton—her glorious vocal powers exercised to the full—was a sure winner, and the singer had to appear again. Mr. Crosfield's willingness to assist the good cause by personal service as well as in other ways was acknowledged to the full when he appeared in the role of artist 9, hearty applause greeting him as he came upon the stage. "Pianoforte selections, Mr. A. H. Crosfield," read the programme. Commencing with some excerpts from Chopin's works, Mr. Crosfield concluded with one of the more popular of Mendelssolin's "Lieder ohn Worte," which, it is pleasing to say, he played in the light and blithesome manner which is so essential to its due interpretation. Mr. J. B. Poole roused the audience to a great pitch of enthusiasm with a charming West Country ditty, "Up from Somerset," in which the composer has so happily caught the spirit of the words:</p>
        <p>For we'm come up from Zumerzet, Where the cider apples grow, For we're all King's men in Zumerzet, As they were long, long ago. And when you're wanting sojer boys, An' there's fighting for to do, You just send word to Zumerzet, And we'll all be up for you! To reiterated demands for an encore, Mr. J. rl. Poole responded with °ft delicious. little cheimoaette. "Tin Coin flf a Dream." A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>THANKING THE WORKERS.</head>
        <p>THANKING THE WORKERS.</p>
        <p>11 At the interval, Mr. GEORGE WALEER, deputy-chairman of the Working Men's Infirmary Commiitee, in the absence of Alderman Burton, the chairman, said their thanks were due to Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield for providing that concert. The con- cert was originally intended for the benefit of the Infirmary, but the Working Men's Com- mittee thought, in the new circumstances, the proceeds should, be devoted to the Belgian Relief Fund. (Applause.) The committee ap- proached Mr. Crosfield, who at once fell in with the idea, and further suggested that if they were willing to allocate the whole of the takings to the Belgian Fund he would relieve them of any financial responsibility whatever. (Ap- plause.) He, moreover, said that the Infirmary should not be the loser as, in a month or two, he would repeat the concert for the Infirmary on similar lines. (Applause.) In the absence of the esteemed chairman, he would ask the Mayor to thank Mr. Crosfield. The MAYOR said Mr. Walker had thanked Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield, and he would thank more especiallf Mr. Crossley, the Musical Society, Mr. Payton, the Male Voice Choir, and all those who had assisted at the concert, and the people who had come forward in such large numbers in support of a good cause. He would also like to thank the Working Men's Committee for mak- ing the arrangements that had prove a-To suc- cessful—although, as their honorary president, it might seem out of place he should do so. The thanks having been accorded by acclama- tion,</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSFIELD, who was received with ap- plause, said on Mrs. Crosfield's behalf and his own he wished to thank most sincerely Mr. Crossley and Mr. Payton for their quite invalu- able assistance at every point in the preparation of the concert. He wished also to thank his old friends, Alderman Burton and Mr. George Walker, and he was greatly indebted to Mr. Hampson for having so admirably assistal in , organising the concert. Finally, he thanked them all most heartily for coming to enjoy what he ventured to think had been a most delight- ful musical treat, for which they were greatly indebted to the members of the orchestra, of the Musical Society, the Male Voice Choir, Crosfield's Band, and the artistes who individu- ally had given them exceptionally delightful music. (Applause.) Mr. Crosfield concluded by addressing a few words of welcome in French to the Belgians present, a welcome which was heartily seconded by the audience.</p>
        <p>Nous sommes heureux, said Mr. Crosfield, d'avoir ici ce soir la presence de nos amis Beiges et tree flattes de pouvoir offrir Phos- pitalite de notre chere vine de Warrington aux representants de la vaillante et beronifle _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>PROCEEDS.</head>
        <p>THE PROCEEDS.</p>
        <p>The accounts of the concert have not yet been made up, but it is expected that the proceeds will be about £lOO. Mr. Parker gave a subscription of £l, and Mrs. R. Pemberton gave £1 135., the proceeds of the sale of Belgian favours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>R. CROSFIRLD AT THE INFIRMARY.</head>
        <p>R. CROSFIRLD AT THE INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>Plays for the Wounded.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfield visited the Infirmary this week, and after having had the pleasure of inspecting the whole institution, had a chat with the wounded men. He spoke to them individually and collectively, and entertaineC item by play- ing pianoforte selections. In his address to them he described•thern as heroes, whose deeds in support of civilisation against barharism would live in history and rival those which had come down to us through the ages. We were, he said, in a strong position owing to the fight such men as those he was addressing had made; they were strong and fortunate in their Allies, includ- ing not only the brave and gallant Belgians, Frenchmen, Russians, Japanese and Servians, but Time, Justice and Hope. Mr. Crosfield, who was accompanied by the Mayoress and Mrs. Osborne, was conducted over the institution by Mr. J. H. Smethurst, chairman of the Board of Management. GIFT FROM THE KING.</p>
        <p>Yesterday a consignment was received at the Infirmary from the King at Sandringham, con- sisting of 20 pheasants and six hares " for wounded soldiers and sailors."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>FOR THE WAR FUNDS. Musical Society's Successful Performance of "Elijah."</head>
        <p>FOR THE WAR FUNDS. Musical Society's Successful Performance of "Elijah."</p>
        <p>Theirs was a double delight who attended the Warrington Musical Society's performance of "Elijah" in the Parish Church on Thursday evening, for, not only had they the pleasure of hearing the superb oratorio most creditably rendered, but they were also, through the medium of a silver collection, enabled to assist the War Funds. "Creditably rendered," we have said. It was all that, and more, especially when the limitations imposed upon the excellent principals and the well-balanced and carefully- trained choir, by the nature of the building in which they were called upon to sing, were taken into consideration. The treatment accorded !.Z.Zendelssohn's im- mortal music was-such as to reflect high credit upon all concerned, the many choruses being most effectively given by the large body of vocalists, whilst the soloists and orchestra were fully alive to what was required of them. In a word, the performance lacked little in finish and ranked among the most creditable of the many that the society have given. The music accredited to the part of Elijah was well sung by Mr. J. B. Poole, his fine voice being heard to great advantage in the numerous recitatives and arias falling to his lot. Mr. J. Babbitt had been selected to sing the Obadiah music, but circumstances prevented him doing so, and at quite short notice Mr. J. H. Draper undertook the onerous task, and, what is more, discharged it in a manner calling for warm praise. Among the ladies, Mrs. Smallshaw, Mrs. Dutton and Miss Hewitt specially distinguished themselves, all three being in fine voice, and singing sympathetically and with a full sense of the devotional character of the work. Mrs. Dutton was conspicuously successful in the air "Hear ye! Israel," with which the second part of the oratorio opens. Miss Wilkinson and Miss Hipkiss also rendered valuable service in the course of the oratorio.. _ . .</p>
        <p>The chorus, responsive to the skilled baton of Mr. Frank H. Crossley, missed few of the opportunities afforded them, their rendering of the sequence of Baal choruses and of the num- ber descriptive of Jehovah's watch over Israel being particularly effective, as also was their treatment of the magnificent and descriptive chorus, Behold!" God the Lord passed by !" The orchestra, under the leadership of Mr. R. H Pearson, did its work most thoroughly, alt hough somewhat handicapped by the position in which it was placed, and Mr. W. H. Ps 3 ton rendered invaluable service at the organ. During the interval between the first ana second parts of the oratorio, Mr. F. M. Darby played a short voluntary on the organ, and at the close performed with noteworthy skill Men. delssohn's Sonata No. 3</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>P.C. GRUBB KILLED.</head>
        <p>P.C. GRUBB KILLED.</p>
        <p>Yesterday news was received in Warrington that Lance-Corporal Herbert H. Grubb, of the Royal Berkshire Regiment, who, from 1911, up to the outbreak of war, was a constable in the Warrington Borough Police Force, had been killed in action on November 3rd. Mrs. Grubb, who is residing with her parents in Thelwall- lane, got the official notification from the Mili- tary Authorities yesterday morning. She has received many expressions of sympathy, includ- ing one from the Chief Constable, and others from various nembers of the Force.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>YET MORE HELP!</head>
        <p>YET MORE HELP!</p>
        <p>Belgian Artistes Meg for Belgian Wounded.</p>
        <p>SPLENDID CONCERT AT ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>On Sunday was music—" that gentle goddess who has bridled the soft-soughing zephyrs to her will, rivalled the songs of the fir-is, and has found harmony even in the warring elements" —called to the aid of the brave, patient Belgians. On Saturday night the tender Muse was wooed on behalf of the despoiled people who have fled from the war- blasted country-side and shell-wrecked towns to our hospitable shores—and never, amid the countless acts of compassion and friendship she has perfornied, has England done greater honour to herself than in her solicitude for the victimised women and children of her *Rant and ever-gloriously-to-be-remembered little Ally—whilst on Sunday she was, in all the radiance of her beauty, called to the ser- vice of those gallant warriors who have fallen wounded whilst fighting amid the ruins of their once-beautiful, now sadly war-scarred, towns— fighting on so pluckily, so unyieldingly to pre- serve that which of all their possessions is among the few that still remains intact to them—the honour of their nation. Yes, Sat- urday's concert was for the old-men victims, the hapless and homeless women and bairns; Sunday's was to help to bring succour to the young men :fallen by the way in the glorious fight "for King, for country, and for liberty" —it was to help on the noble work of succour being done by the Belgian Red Cross Society. And Warringtonians felt that their good name was at stake; that it was up to them to do their " bit " to help little Piou-piou on his bed of pain—for is not his fight our fight also?--- and so they rallied in their hundreds to the Royal Court Theatre, where were appearing those sweet singers from the Belgian State Opera-houses, who have come across at the request of their Government to support and emphasise with their voices the Belgian Red Cross Society's appeal for aid. And, in making their appeal the Belgian State Operatic artistes were aided by some of the best of our popular local singers; as, for instance, Mrs. Sanders, Miss Sara Silcoek, and Miss Elsie Wilkinson; whilst Mr. V. Garrod, the fine actor whose company is this week appearing at the Royal Court, also lent his aid; as likewise did a specially augmented orches- tra,, under the baton of Mr. James Roy (well known to patrons of the popular theatre as the regular " Master of the Musick"). Sir Joseph Beecham had kindly granted the free use of the beautiful theatre, and Mr. A. M. Loader, his general manager, had heartily supported and co-operated with Mr. H. L. Warburton, the acting-manager, in doing all that a management can do to ensure complete success. The members of the theatre staff gave their services willingly and gratuitously, as also did the local artistes and the members of the special orchestra.</p>
        <p>The programme had been arranged with much taste, and, although it was something of a novelty to hear the Belgian artistes sing in their own tongue, the effect was decidedly pleasing. Mme. Berth° Savoir, however, sang in a most charming way and with equal effect, both in her own language and in English. The National Anthems of the Allies formed the first item, and in the rendering of them the orchestra acquitted themselves with credit, their performance meriting, and meeting with, warm applause. "There's a land" (Frances Allitsen) was rendered by Mrs. Sanders, in her usual finished style, and Miss Silcock had to respond to an encore for her sympathetic treat- ment of "Forget-me-not." In most effective fashion Mr. Garrod recited "The Day," and such was the dramatic energy with which he declaimed this forceful piece that the audience would not let him go until he had given "A Scrap of Paper," and a recitation suggested by "Punch's" striking cartoon—a reprint of which appeared in a recent issue of "The Examiner" —in which King Albert, Belgium's warrior- king, is represented as replying to the Kaiser's taunt, "Well, you have lost everything," with the words "Not my soul!" The orchestra gave a delightful interpretation of some ballet music, entitled "Egyptian" (Luigini), and a none-the-less effective one of the overture to " Mirella." (Gounod). The voices of Mrs. Sanders and Miss Silcock blended beautifully in the old-fashioned yet still acceptable duet, "The Elfin Call" (Glover), and later Miss Wil- kinson was encored for her rendering of "Land of Hope and Glory." Madame Savoir fairly won the appreciation of the audience with her first number, but her two English songs proved even more popular, the audience being quite carried away with enthusiasm when she sang, with a naivete that was peculiarly winning, " Your King and Country Need You," and joined in the chorus lustily. M. Jules Colbert, of the Grand Theatre of Verviers, and Mons. Ant. De Vally, first tenor from the Theatre Royal, Antwerp, also delighted in solos and duets, and it is safe to say that a Warrington audience has not been privileged to hear such perfectly trained artistes for a long time. Each had to respond to the repeated calls of the audience, and with each succeeding number they enhanced their soon-made popularity. By way of conclusion the orchestra played and the two Belgian male vocalists sang "La Brabanconne" (the Belgian National Anthem), followed by our own National Anthem. Mr. A. Trow discharged the duties of accom- panist, which in this case were peculiarly arduous, with unerring judgment and artistic skill.</p>
        <p>Copies of "The Day" and Belgian souvenirs wore sold to the audience in aid of the fund.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR PROUD.</p>
        <p>During an interval in the concert thanks were accorded all who had assisted. _ _</p>
        <p>His Worshi.p—tie7-iefayor, who, along with the Mayoress and Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield, occupied a box, said that, on behalf of the Bel- gian Red Cross Society, he offered those present his sincere thanks for attending in such large numbers. When they considered that there had been three similar efforts during the past week, it was surprising to find such a large band of citizens attending that night. He was ex- ceedingly proud to be Mayor cf a borough with such citizens. He thanked the artistes who had given their services, the proprietor for the use of the hall, Mr. Loader and his staff for their services, and everyone who had assisted in making the concert a success. He was proud of the citizens who could put their hands down so handsomely for so worthy an etort. (Applause.) . .</p>
        <p>.Loader thanked the Mayor for his sup- port, and said that Lady Greenall had been called away, but had sent a cheque for £lO, which was a very useful contribution. (Ap• plause.) Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., who would have liked to be present, was detained at the Press Bureau, but they hoped he would have some good news for them. (Hear, hear.) Be thanked Mr. Warburton, who, ne said, had organised the concert almost by himself. It was his own idea, and he (the speaker) gave him his hearty co-operation from the mou.ent it was asked. (Hear, hear.) The Belgian artistes stayed at the Lion Hotel and before returning to London on Monday they visited Raddon Court Hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
