      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>AFTER BOMBARDMENT.</head>
        <p>AFTER BOMBARDMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Box, commercial traveller for the Rubber Company of Scotland, Stirling, a well- , known Warrington man, was s.tunderland on , the day the East Coast was bombarded by the Germans. Business came to an end, and he and , other gentlemen of the road went on to Hartle- pool to see the results of war first hand. Mr. Box assures as that the state of devasta- tionat Hartlepool was much worse than has been described in the newspapers and he tx- pects the list of casualties to grov'Considerably. The same shells had gone through the Carnegie Library and the station and wiecked both; and the gasometer was made into scrap-iron. Candles were in great demand, even for the nee of the booking clerks at the station. It was curious, says Mr. Box, to see taese officials look- ing for tickets with candles stuck in beer bottles. The Norwegian Church, which re- sembles St. Mary's, Buttermarket-street, had its roof removed wholesale, and for a distance equal to that between Market Gate and the Swing Bridge there was not a window left in any of the houses. Mr. Box brought back several souvenirs," including portions of shells.</p>
        <p>111 R</p>
      </div>
