      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>AT NURSING ASSOCIATION'S MEETING.</head>
        <p>AT NURSING ASSOCIATION'S MEETING.</p>
        <p>The claims upon all our people which are Incidental to the War are very numerous and urgent, and demand and deserve the most prompt and generous recognition, yet I venture to hope and believe that our Nursing Associa- tion will be adequately supported during the current year, as its record of unostentatious, sympathetic and practical work has begotten for our Superintendent and her Nursing Staff a most appreciative regard for themselves and for the skill and devotion which characterise the manner in which their duties are fulfilled.</p>
        <p>The foregoing extract from the report of the Hon. Treasurer of the Warrington District Nursing Association (Councillor 11. Roberts), which was read at the 17th annual meeting of that body, held in the Council Chamber of the Town Hall on 'Wednesday- evening, formed the text of more than one of the several speeches</p>
        <p>made. The Mayor (Alderman Peacock) was in the chair, and a small attendance included the Deputy-Mayor (Councillor Joseph), the Revs. G. C. Briggs and W. Thompson Derry, and Messrs. John Fairclough, 11. Hope, and F. A. Cross, together with the Hon. Treasurer.</p>
        <p>LOSS IN SUBSCRIPTIONS WIPED OUT. In the course of his annual report the Treas- urer said it was gratifying to be able to report that the loss in subscriptions during the pre- vious year had been wiped out by 14 new sub- scribers. Thanks to the splendid work of those who organised the Alexandra Day collection, the Association had received the handsome donation of £7O 16s. 3d. from the funds so con- tributed. The employes of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., Ltd., in addition to the presentation of the beautiful memorial tablet, affixed to the front of the Nurses' Home in Arpley-street, further emphasised their desire to perpetuate the memory of the late Mr. Frederick Monks, by collecting the sum of £6O, which had been added to the amount already invested, together with the proceeds of the "Alexandra Day" collection, which latter bid been made up to ..tlOO. It was difficult to give adequate expres- sion to the debt which the Association owed to those friends who had from time to time initi- ated and carried through special efforts result- ing in substantial additions to the income. In that connection the concert given by the "Per- fection" Soap Work's Band in the Hippodrome, which was kindly lent by the proprietors free of charge, and also the concert promoted by the pupils of the People's College, should be specially noticed. The efforts of the Working Men's Committee had again been highly suc- cessful.</p>
        <p>A Windfall and a Bequest. Supplementing his printed report, Councillor Roberts referred to "the somewhat ambitious scheme" promoted a few years ago to amass the sum of £2,000 in invested securities, with the object of securing a certain amount of reliable income, which was now very near being accomplished. The sum of .tlOO which would figure in next year's accounts received as the result of the Old English Fair, and a further item of £50— which would also be shown in the next report— from a bequest left by a patient nursed by one of the Association's nurses, had been invested in the War Loan; the committee feeling that whilst they had a great regard for the Warring- ton Corporation, they thought they could get a little more than 31 per cent.</p>
        <p>INCOME INCREASING IN SPITE OF WAR.</p>
        <p>The Mayor, in proposing that the treasurer's report and balance sheet—a summary of which has already appeared in our columns—remarked that it was very gratifying to find that in spite of the times they were passing through and had passed through—in connection with the Insur- ance Act and other matters which operated against such institutions as that—the income of that Association was increasing. He only hoped that the Association would continue to prosper, and that the £2,000 Mr. Roberts had spoken of would be obtained before long. Mr. Hope seconded the proposition, which was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>NURSES' 16,000 VISITS LAST YEAR.</head>
        <p>NURSES' 16,000 VISITS LAST YEAR.</p>
        <p>The Lady Superintendent's annual report stated that 773 cases had been dealt with during the year (41 of them having been transferred from the previous year), whilst 16,226 visits were paid by the nurses. Forty-one of last year's cases remained on the books, whilst 484 of the patients were nursed to convalescence, 60 of them were transferred to hospitals, and 29 were " removed for other causes." One hundred and eighteen died. Of the patients nursed, 353 were housewives, 131 were children, 44 were labourers, 34 were forgemen, 37 were old-age pensioners, and 28 were wiredrawers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>FIFTY-TWO MORE PATIENTS THAN LAST YEAR.</head>
        <p>FIFTY-TWO MORE PATIENTS THAN LAST YEAR.</p>
        <p>In their annual report, the committee set forth the fact that the Superintendent (Miss Milne) and the nursing staff had each of them carried out their work in a manner which had earned the gratitude of the patients and their friends, and the committee desired to add their testimony to a like effect. During the year 16,226 visits were paid to 732 cases, the increase in the number of patients being 52. The com- mittee gratefully acknowledged the financial support received during the past year, and also desired to express their thanks to Drs. Langdale and Fox for their professional attendances upon the nursing staff. Their report concluded with the following appeal: "Will all subscribers kindly bring the claims of our Assocation before the notice of their friends?"</p>
        <p>GIFTS THAT WILL BE WELCOME. The ladies, who form the House Committee of the District Nurses' Home, and who meet once a month to receive the Superintendent's reports and to examine the household books, reported that they desired to express their un- bounded appreciation of the work of the nurses and the management of the home. There had been several changes on the nursing staff during the year—Miss Tweedy, Miss Evans and Miss Fildes having each left to take up posts nearer their own homes, and their places had been fined by Miss Paynton, Miss Hewton, and Miss Miles, and in August last, the work having in- creased so much, a sixth candidate, Miss Halli- gan, was added to the staff. The committee were, however, most fortunate in retaining the services of Miss Milne, the valued superintend- ent, and her excellent senior nurse, Miss Trin- ham. They heartily thanked all who had sent gifts to the home during the year, and trusted their many friends would still remembec the Association's need of old linen, bed jackets, &amp;c. The foregoing reports were received. NEARLY £3OO FROM WORKING MEN'S COMMITTEE. Mr. F. A. Cross proposed that the thanks of the meeting be given to the Working men..Bl Committee for the handsome donation et „c297' obtained by them, pointing out that it was ,eB6 more than last year. He also called attention to the items of £l6 us. 3d. raised by Croa. • field's "Perfection" Band, £7 by the concert given by the pupils of the People's College, and .R. 60 donated by the employes of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., Ltd. In addition, it was worth noting that the working man himself had paid • over ..t23 more in subscriptions than he did last year—a very highly satisfactory state of affairs, and one which showed that the real British - workman, and especially the Warrington work- man, was a philanthropist at heart, and not only at heart, but also in practice. (I-rear, hear.) " The Rev. W. Thompson Derry seconded, and it was envied.</p>
        <p>WHAT EVERY CHURCH SHOULD DO. The Rev. G. C. Briggs proposed best thanks to the Ladies' Committee, the Superintendent (MI" Mil"), the Nursing Staff. the jinn. Tress- ----</p>
        <p>toidintied in next column.</p>
      </div>
