      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>PARISH CHURCH.</head>
        <p>THE PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Bishop's Appreciation of the Work</p>
        <p>It Does. TRIBUTE TO THE RECTOR.</p>
        <p>"The Church of England is doing as great a work of education in this parish as in any parish in the Diocese."</p>
        <p>In these words the Bishop of Liverpool (the Right Rev. Dr. Chavasse), preaching at the Parish Church on Monday evening, commended to a large congregation one of the most impor- tant phases of Church work. On week-days, he said, something like 2,100 children were gath- ered to receive Scriptural training on the lines of the English Church. On Sunday, the num- bers were even larger, the total number on the books being not less than 2,300. At the Smith- street schools—which, owing to circumstances which he regretted to say they could not pre- vent, must before long pass out of their hands— he saw upwards of 200 children taught with the greatest care by one who had given nine years of the best part of her life to that great work, and who had won the regard and love of the children and the respect of their parents:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>One of the Largest Schools in the Diocese.</head>
        <p>One of the Largest Schools in the Diocese.</p>
        <p>He also visited Ellesmere-street school, where 220 or 230- infants were excellently taught by their admirable headmistress and her assist- ants. He then passed into the great boys' school—one of the largest in the diocese—where upwards of 700 boys were on the books—and where Mr. Brown was doing excellent work training on the very best lines the material which gave those splendid soldiers who to-day were keeping back the tide of invasion from these shores. He was greatly struck both by the teachers and scholars and the methods adopted. Lastly, he had the privilege of visit- ing Fairfield School, where even a larger num- ber of girls were being most admirably trained, in one of the best schools he knew, by Mrs. Stansfield and Miss Gorst.</p>
        <p>His Lordship's Tribute to the Rector. The Bishop had been making his annual visit of inspection to the various centres of parochial activity, and his sermon; in the evening was in the nature of a review of the impressions he had gathered from what he had seen. He began with a tribute to the services of the Rector (Canon Willis) and his wife, "ho, he pointed out, had now worked amongst the parishioners for '26 years. "You know," he said, "how your Rector has devoted body, soul and spirit to your pastoral care; how he has sought to shepherd you as souls for whom Christ died, and how he has left, everywhere in the parish, the marks of his ministry. I have but to look round this, your beautiful church, to see in the walls stripped of their old plaster, in the removal of the organ from the west end, in the two beautiful screens which have been put up, the building of the new choir vestry on the north sde of the church, and the renovation of the AI vestry on the south side, some of the marks of his ministry."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Praise for Parochial Activity.</head>
        <p>Praise for Parochial Activity.</p>
        <p>His Lordship went on to refer to his visit to the Fairfield and Church-street Schools, and added that in those places, as also in the growth of the congregations and the number of com- municants and confirmees, they had further marks of the Rector's ministry; whilst other and even greater fruits were to be found in the spirit of concord and peace which prevailed not only in that congregation, but in the town and in the slow, but sure, progress of the spiritual life of Warrington. They had also to thank God for the assistant clergy. His Lordship went on to pay a tribute to the work the Revs. Buckmaster and Vickers are doing at St. Clement's and St. George's Missions, and said that the congregation had to thank God for the spirit and energy of the churchwardens and sidesmen, the Sunday school teachers, the mem- bers of the choir, and all others who had helped to witness for God in that place.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Needs of the Parish.</head>
        <p>The Needs of the Parish.</p>
        <p>llis Lordship also dealt with the needs of the parish, the first of which, he said, was a new Mission Church in Beresford-street, and the second of which was a fourth assistant curate. If in the next year or two the congregation could see their way to raise £l5O or £l6O a year for the support of such fourth curate, they would be doing an immense service to the spiritual work of the parish. Touching upon the Diocesan Finance Scheme, the Bishop remarked that the allotment the parish was asked to contribute was ..t6O a year. The churchwardens informed him that that sum would have been easily raised had not the war interfered and dislocated their arrange- ments, as it had dislocated so many others. They, however, assured him that, although the parish could only provide perhaps half the amount at present, they trusted to right the matter when the war was over.</p>
        <p>Episcopal Handshake. ' As the congregation dispersed the Bishop shook hands with each member of it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>NATIONAL CHRISTMAS CARD.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL CHRISTMAS CARD.</p>
        <p>Admiral Sir John Jellicoe has written to Messrs. Raphael Tuck and Sons, thanking them for the advance copies of the National Christ- mas Card which they are issuing by special request in aid of His Royal Highness the Prince of Wales' National Relief Fund. The Admiral much admires the design and the manner in which it has been reproduced. This National Card, "Defenders of the Empire," representative of both the Army and the Navy, has received the commendation of many leaders of both Forces. It is a splendid 1 card to send out to our soldiers at the Front 1 and to our sailors afloat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>PHILANTHROPIST IN PRACTICEcontinued.</head>
        <p>PHILANTHROPIST IN PRACTICE— continued.</p>
        <p>- 7-"'•</p>
        <p>urer, and the lion. Secretary (Mr. J. Dutton Fairclough, who was prevented from attending by military duties, he having offered his ser- vices to King and country). Mr. Briggs testi- fied to the usefulness of the work of the Associa- tion, and expressed his belief that, with the possible exception of the Infirmary, there was no more charitable work going on in the town, nor, he ventured to add, more Christlike work. He had come across dozens of cases in his own parish in which the visits of the nurses had been God-sends to poor people, and, despite the many patriotic appeals made upon them, they must not allow the work of that Association to be lost sight of. When looking through the report he had counted the number of places of worship which gave contributions of offertories, and noticed that they were very few indeed. He knew that there were in many cases prac- tical difficulties in the way of giving Sunday offertories, for the churches had many calls upon their collections, but he did think that every place of worship in the town -might give something, however small, to the really noble work of that Association, which, in time of war, no less than in time of peace, was being effi- ciently and sympathetically carried on. His own church gave the Good Friday collection, work funds. and also allocated something from their sale of The Deputy-May or seconded.</p>
        <p>OFFICERS ELECTED. On the proposition of Mr. Trope the President (the Right Hon. Lord Lilford), the Vice-Presi- dents, the committee (with the addition of Mr. Upson), ,the Hon. Treasurer (Councillor Roberts). and the Hon. Secretary (Mr. Dutton Fairclongh) were re-elected, and the meeting concluded with, a vote of thanks to the, Mayor and for lending the Council for presiding Cham- ber for the purposes of the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 19, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 19, 1914.</p>
        <p>r DISTRICT rIEWS</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>Christmas Tree and Sale of Work.—The annual Christmas tree and sale of work organised by the Vicar and church-workers of St. Thomas' Church, in aid of the S.P.G. and the Waifs' and Strays' Fund, was held on Saturday in the Church Room. There was a good attendance throughout the afternoon and evening, and the huge Christmas tree in the centre of the room, laden with all kinds of dainty presents for the young, was the joy of the children.—The Rev. J. Collins (vicar) opened the sale, and the fol- . . . .</p>
        <p>lowing stall-holders had a busy time :—Work Stall, Mesdames Wood, Worrall and Smith; G.F.S. Stall, Miss Moores, Miss Garnett and Miss Robbins; Bran Tub, The Misses Ethel and Edith Melbourne, and Miss M. Cookson; For- tune-telling Witch, Miss Heath; Christmas Tree, Sunday School teachers; and Refreshment Stall, Mrs. H. Woods. . .</p>
        <p>The proceeds amounted to about</p>
        <p>Funeral of Well-known Local “Character."— At Hill Cliffe graveyard on Saturday the mortal remains of Harry Shaw (better known as "British ") were interred, the Rev. George Curtis conducting the funeral service. The mourners included, in addition to the relatives, the following: Messrs. Joseph Moss, Albert Broady, John Gosling, T. R. Westbrook, Alfred Cook, William Cook, Henry Musson, T. Wells, T. White, Thomas Broady, William Toft, S. Stringer, John Fairhurst, and Alfred Maddock (representing Mr. F. Houghton, of The Dingle). Wreaths were received from Mr. and Mrs. Thos. Shaw (Warrington), "Fred and Janey ' Shaw," "Hilda, Lily and Harry," Mr. and Mrs. George Shaw, jun., Mr. and Mrs. F. Jackson, Mr. and Mrs. William Toft, Mr. Thomas Broady and family. Mr. and Mrs. Alfred Cook, Mr. Henry Musson, Mr. and Mrs. F. Houghton.</p>
        <p>Young Rechabites' " Pound Social."—The members of the "Pioneer" Tent—the juvenile section of the "Rechab " Tent of the Indepen- dent Order of Rdchabites—held their first "pound social" of the season in the Indepen- dent Methodist Schoolroom, Chapel-lane, on</p>
        <p>Monday evening, the function proving highly successful. Seven new members were placed on the books—six of them being initiated there and then, the seventh having as a good excuse for non-attendance the fact that he only hap- pened to be born the previous day. One tiny new member—Miss Dorothy Berry—caused much merriment by insisting upon taking her seat alongside the Deputy-Ruler. Games were afterwards indulged in, and at intervals the ever-welcome trays of biscuits and sweets were brought round. Roars of laughter were caused by Bro. Watkin's dancing doll, and at eight o'clock the children dispersed homewards, apples, oranges and nuts—for the gift of some of which thanks are due to parents, friends and adult members Of the tent—being distributed as they Went. The adult members afterwards held their annual meeting, and the brightness which prevailed in the juvenile meeting spread to this gathering also, tasking it one to be re- membered with pleasure, and a fitting close to a year of hard and willing service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>PAD GATE.</head>
        <p>PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>A Broken Bye-law.—At the County Petty Sessions, held at Warrington on Wednesday, before Messrs. R. C. Parr, J. Rymer Young, W. Furness and William Fairclough, William Bradley, of Boothbank, Allostock, near Knuth- ford, was fined 10s. and costs for allowing a heavy motor vehicle to remain stationary in Manchester-road, Woolston, so that a wheel of the same rested on Spittle Bridge, in contra- vention of the County Council bye-law.—De- fendant did not appear, but was represented by Mr. Thomas Fyane, of Widnes, who, on his client's behalf, admitted a technical offence. Wesley Guild.—A very interesting lecture on "Ignatius Loyola," the founder of Jesuitism, was given ot the Wesley Guild on Monday evening by Mr. J. Williams, of Widnes. He explained die inner workings of this society, contending that it was full of secrecy and vice, and that it did harm to those who became en- tangled with it.—The mover and seconder of the vote of thanks, viz., Mr. England and Mr. Lon- don, respectively, had both made a study of the subject, and a breezy debate which took place between them did much to interest those pres- ent at the meeting.—The Rev. A. J. Morton presided. WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>Concert for War Funds.—A highly successful concert—the first of a series organised by a local committee in aid of various War Funds— was held in the Winwick Village Schoolroom 1 on Friday evening of last week. The pro- gramme was delightfully varied, songs, pgtriotic and pathetic and grave and gay, and high-class music by Verdi and Elgar being blended with the latest popular songs. The artistes were: Miss Ina Jenks (soprano), Mrs. E. A. Sanders (contralto), Mr. Ditchburn, of Manchester Cathedral (bass), Mr. A. Roberts (tenor), Messrs. Richards and. Rigby (baritones), Mr. Williams (solo cornet), Mr. R. Bloomfield (concertinist). The humour was supplied by Mr. E. A. Sanders, in amusing sketches, and by Mr. R. Chandler in funny songs. Mr. Quinton IT. G. Bow was at the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>LY3IIII.</head>
        <p>LY3IIII.</p>
        <p>On the Sessions' Bench.—At the Lymm Sessions, held on Wednesday, there were pre- sent on the bench: Messrs. J. W. Sidebotham (in the chair), J. Coxon, S. W. Gould and T. J. Ridgway.</p>
        <p>Christmas Shopping.—Most of the shops in the village have by now been gaily decorated for the Christmas shopping. Lymm, evidently does not mean to be behind with the "business as usual" programme !</p>
        <p>Christmas Football for Red Cross.—The ar- rangements for the grand football match—to be played on the Pepper-street ground on Christ- mas afternoon, in aid of the Lymm Red Cross Hospital—are rapidly being completed. The teams will be those of the Wednesday and Saturday Clubs, and Mr. Alfred Watkin has kindly consented to kick off at half-past two.</p>
        <p>Respite for Lymm " Tommies."—A second batch of Kitchener's Army and Territorial units have been home to Lymm during this week on three days' Christmas leave. For the next week or two it is expected that different lots will be coming and going, although none are expected to be at home on Christmas Day itself. They, however, do not mind that very much, as elabor- ate preparations have been made for a day of true .British cheer at their respective training , depots! Unpaid Contributions.--Samuel Stanbank, of Reddish-lane, Lymm, was summoned at the Petty Sessions on Wednesday, on a charge of not having kept up Payment of a weekly con- tribution of one shilling—towards the mainten- ance of his father—as promised to the Bucklow Guardiansfendant's wife, who appeared on his behalf, said he was not in a position to pay. His wage was Lils. per week, the rent of the house was 4s. 6d., and there were five children.— The money was ordered to be paid.—Defendant's wife: "1 won't pay !"</p>
        <p>To Help the Queen's Fund.--On Saturday Miss Mary Brundrett and friends gave a matinee concert in the Higher-lane Baptist Schoolroom, at which a silver collection was made on behalf of the Queen's Fund, for women thrown out of employment by the War. The following ladies and gentlemen kindly gave their services: Mrs. Wilson, Mrs. Ridgway, 11Iiss Mary Brundrett, Mr. Harold Wilson and Mn Fred Randall. Mrs. Slee and Mr. Bert Randall acted as accompan- ists. There was a very good attendance, and is Brundrett is to be congratulated and warmly thanked for the very enjoyable after- noon spent by those present.</p>
        <p>Social for Soldiers' Comforts.—A very success- ful social gathering of the Parishioners and members of the. congregation of St. Peter's Church, Oughtrington, was held on Wednesday evening of last week in the large school, the occasion providing an excellent opportunity for heartily welcoming the new Rector (the Rev. G. S. Tudor-Evans) and his wife and family, whilst it also enabled the rev, gentleman and his wife to become acquainted with those they had not already met. After the Rector had re- plied to suitable speeches from Messrs. G. Tel- fer and 0. G. Smith, an enjoyable entertain- ment was provided. by the following artistes: Mrs. Wyatt, Misses Armstrong and A. Arm- strong, atd Messrs. H. Thomason, T. Peters, E. BrOgdale, L. B. Barnes, A. E. Donaldson and F. Rutter. About 220 people attended the social, and out of a profit of £2 6s. 4d.. A:1 6s. 4d. has been given to the Higher-lane (Lvmm) Ladies' Sewing Class, who are making useful articles of clothing for the soldiers and sailors. The .balance of £1 goes to the Lymm Red Cross Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>NEW APPOINTAIENT.</head>
        <p>A NEW APPOINTAIENT.</p>
        <p>Lymm Agency tor Unemployment - Insurance.</p>
        <p>To Mr. Arthur Sutton, of Bridgewater-street, Lymm, the Board of Trade have this week en- trusted the local agency of • the unemployment insurance, in succession to Mr. J. Niddrie, who has been promoted to a similar pbAition at Run- corn. Mr. Sutton, who is well known in Lymm, had been acting as deputy to Mr. Niddrie for the last three months, and is therefore already quite conversant with the duties upon which he now enters. For the last fifteen years he has held the position of bookkeeper to Mr. J. H. Holt, builder, who receives his resignation with considerable regret.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>FATAL ACCIDENT. mr. Greenmail Succumbs After Cycling Mishap.</head>
        <p>FATAL ACCIDENT. mr. Greenmail Succumbs After Cycling Mishap.</p>
        <p>With regret we record the demise of Mr. Greenman, of Booths Hill, Lymm, which took place at his home on Thursday evening, after a cycling mishap. On the morning of the day in question, Mr. Greenman was cycling slowly along Station Brow, and presumably, the bicycle skidded, for the rider was thrown off and ren- dered unconscious—remaining so for some hours. He was conveyed home, suffering from severe in- juries, to which Doctors Burrowes and Thorpe devoted skilled attention. The patient, how- ever, passed away in the evening. Mr. Greenman, whose age was 56 years, occu- pied the position of timekeeper on the Lymm section of the Manchester Ship Canal—duties which he had satisfactorily performed for twenty-six years. He had a large circle of friends and acquaintances, by whom he will be particularly missed. Ile leaves a widow and daughter to mourn their sudden loss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>LICENSEE FINED.</head>
        <p>A LICENSEE FINED.</p>
        <p>Child and Soldier Illegally on His Premises.</p>
        <p>Tom Charles Heaven, licensee, of Heatley, was summoned, at the Lymin Sessions on Wed- nesday, for having allowed a child under the age of 14; to be on his licensed premises on the night of Saturday, November 7th.—Mr., Percy Jordan, of Manchester, appeared on behalf of the defendant, and Mr. Leicester Culdecott pro- I secuted for the police. Mr. Jordan intimated that his client pleaded guilty. Mr. Culdecott explained that the house was visited, at a quarter-past ten on the night in question, by Sergeant Davies and Constable Allcock. Entering the bar-parlour, the officers found, amongst several men and women, a child of about five and a half years. There was also a soldier in uniform there, which was not legal at that late hour. Defendant was called from another room, and these facts were pointed out to him. When the constable endeavoured to get particulars from the soldier, however, defend- ant became angry and said: "Don't answer him! I'm master here! Let him mind his own business!" He "carried on" to such an ex- tent that it was almost impossible for the con- stable to obtain any information; and, as he left and walked away, defendant followed him to • the door and called out after him very I loudly: "You're very clever and very smart, aren't you? I suppose you watched him come in!" Defendant would not give the police any information, and. was very excited.—This state- ment was borne out by Sergeant Davies. Mr. Jordan pointed out that defendant had been licensee of the house for about seven years, and had never had anything against him be- fore. Until recently he had had an assistant named Shaw. Shaw, however, had gone to the Front; and at that time defendant had a friend and his wife—named Swift—helping him; he himself did not know that the child—or the soldier, for that matter—had entered the pre- mises. It was this that excited him; and hear- ing, a day or two after, that lie had annoyed the officer, he went and apologised. A fine of 10s. and costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>One To Our Allies. Those who at this time of year are so fond of insisting on the borrowed nature of our annual feast are just now with something like malicious glee " rubbing in" the fact that the Christmas tree is essentially a German institu- tion. Some of them, indeed, would have it supposed that even Santa Claus is of Teutonic origin; but that heresy is easily refuted, Saint Nicholas being the patron saint of Russia. Then what about the Christmas cracker? Well, long before the war I met with this explanation by an unquestionable authority—The cosaque, as it was originally called, was introduced into Europe by Cossack officers, who, while stationed' in Paris, sent their billet-doux delicately ?wrapped up in tinted and coloured paper. With true British ,acqUisitiveueSs we annex pt this cosaque and converted it into our Christznas' tracksr• •</p>
        <p>-ç -- - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild Concert for Soldiers' Comforts. —Under the auspices of the above Guild, the Wesleyan Choir gave a concert on Wednesday evening. This concert is now an annual insti- tution, and the proceeds each pear have been given to some worthy cause. This year's pro- ceeds will be devoted to finding comforts for the soldiers. The programme which was long and varied, was provided entirely by members of the choir, viz., the Misses M. Theodore, E. Tanner, A. Millington, M. Distance, V. de Prez, A. Lawton, B. Tanner, G. Millington, R. Tanner and A. Dutton, and Messrs. W. M. Cart- wright, J. H. Cartwright, F. Wilkinson and S. T. de Prez. The items were all of a high order, , the part-songs and "Le Marseillaise," given by the choir, being especially well received. Mr. , J. Evans presided, and Miss A. Lawton and Messrs. R. Wallington and J. IL, Cartwright were the accompanists: There was a very good attendance and the proceeds amounted lo 4 59.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>CONSUMPTION.</head>
        <p>CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>. Marvellous Recoveries. The following 'are names and addresses of people who have been in the grip of the great White Plague, and generally considered to be far-advanced, hopeless cases:— Mr. F. C. Monk, 72, Fenwick-road, Peckham Rye. Mr. Edward Spencer, Cropstone-road, Anstm near Leicester. Miss A. Keene, 101, Church-road, Hove. Mrs. Julia Jones, 39, Salisbury-road, Cape Hill, Smethwick, near Birmingham. Mr. B. H. Ketley, 75, Rochester-ay., Upton Park, E. Miss Whitehead, 50, Moorcroft-street, Hollins-road, Hollinwood, near Oldham. G Mr. George eary, Station-road, Ratby, Leicester- shire. Mr.W.Watts, 79, Glenthorne-rd., New Southgate,N. Son of Mr. E. Larcombe, 68, Cambridge-street, Chard, Somerset. Mr. A. Williams, 61, Limes-road, West Croydon, Surrey. Mr. J. J. Skipworth, 7, Riches-rd., Harringay. N. Mrs. F. Wood, 106, Brayard's-rd., Peckham, S.E. Mr. W. J. Stephens, 111, Kimberley-rd., Cardiff. Son of Mr. E. A. Dawbarn, 243, St. Anne's-street, Birkenhead, Cheshire, Mr. G. Green. 6, St. Peter's-rd., Petersfield, Hants. If you doubt it. write yourself to one, any, or all of them for direct evidence. They, among hundreds of others, have been given a new lease of life by taking Stevens' Consumption Cure. If you are suffering from this scourge of the human race, send to-day for a FREE SAMPLE SUPPLY of this self-same life-giving substance. The personal advice of Mr. Stevens wh iimt hsoeul ft isu ayl so at your command. A postcard or letter to 204, Worple-road, Wimbledon, Will bring you the free sample and full particulars • hrgç . Idateter.—.A.drt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>SOLDIER'S WIFE IN TROUBLE. What "A Jealous Disposition" Led To.</p>
        <p>Strong criticism was offered by Councillor R. Roberts, at the Borough Police Court on Tues- day, when Jane Cornwell, a married woman, of 36, Birchall-street, Warrington, was summoned by Mary Jane Williams, of 15, Clare-street, for using bad language on December sth.—Defend- ant pleaded guilty to using One objectionable • phrase..</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Browne, who appeared on behalf of the complainant, said that during the last month or two defendant, who was of a highly jealous disposition, had been charging different women with being "teo familiar" with her husband. The husband had gone to the Frottt with the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment; and - was now a prisoner in Germany. Mr. Browne repeated the language alleged to have been used by defendant; who, he said, could not prove her allegations. Complainant did not I even know the husband by sight. A Prisoner ,of the Germans. Another woman, vi'ho lived in Clare-street, and whose husband was also imprisoned in Germany, bad been wrongfully charged in the same manner. On Friday of the previous week • defendant had been granted a separation from her husband—with his consent.</p>
        <p>Cornwell said her husband had admitted being friendly with some women, though not "that one."</p>
        <p>The Chairman : You are now trying to justify yourself by making a charge against your own husband. Don't you realise what a serious thing it is, when a man is away fighting for his country, to make reflections upon his wife? . Hasn't she enough to think about in the dan- gers to which his life is exposed? It is dis- graceful for any woman—especially for one well-placed, as you are, with a husband—at least, until you were separated from him! If you are Prepared to apologise, the charge will be withdrawn on payment of costs. Defendant said she had no money, and would not apologise.</p>
        <p>The Cha-irman : You evidently need teaching a lesson. You will be fined 10s. and costs, with fourteen days' imprisonment as the alternative. We consider your conduct in this matter is a disgrace and a discredit to you, in every way. Defendant: I can't help that!</p>
        <p>BAD BOY'S "JOY-RIDE." Got a Job as Butcher's Boy—And What He Did ! Sitting as a Juvenile Court on Thursday morning, Mr. J. Rymer Young and other mag- istrates bad before them a fifteen-year-old boy, „ who was charged with stealing various small v, articles, to the value of 2s. 9d., from stalls in •L the Market, on Wednesday; and also a ring ' from a lady there. The Chief Constable said the boy admitted the offence, and the police strongly urged that he should be sent to a reformatory. He had been a source of trouble at a Manchester Indus- trial School, which he had entered in 1909; and at home they had absolutely no control ov him. Only the previous day he had obtained employment with a local butcher by saying he could drive a trap; and, after a joy-ride, had ,t returned and left the loaded cart in the shop- yard. The case was adjourned until next Wednes- day.</p>
        <p>t, 11</p>
        <p>y n</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY. Before Mr. J. Upson (in the chair), Alderman Tinnion, Mr. A. Jolley, and Mr. T. Pemberton. Drink, the Excuse!—Sarah Jane Kirk, 313, Winwick-road, was charged with stealing a shawl, value 6s. 11d., the property of Messrs. G. and H. Jackson, 6, Winwick-street.—The Chief Constable said that, the shawl was taken from outside the shop, and a woman who had seen the prisoner with the shawl gave information, which led to the arrest of Kirk.—Prisoner I am very sorry. I had no need to do it. It was the drink, and I will sign the pledge if You will give me a chance.—The Chief Constable said prisoner was making her tenth appearance. and she was sent to prison for one month. Thought He Was In Liverpool.—James Burke, no fixed abode, was fined. ss. for having been drunk and disorderly in Parker-street.— Prisoner said he had been bringing horses over • from Canada to Liverpool. He did not know he was in Warrington until that morning. He 1: thought he was in Liverpool. (Laughter.) MONDAY. j Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen j Bolton and James Evans, Messrs. J. T. Locker, W. Furness, S. Wright and G. Pilling. "More Like a Madman."—Thomas Dagnall, i;* 131, Bostock-street, was charged with havinv, been drunk and disorderly and with refusing to quit the Coopers' Arms on Saturday.—lnspector:" Forster said that the prisoner was being ejected from the public-house by a barmaid, but he would not go. When he was eventually got outside he threw/ himself down and struggled J. more like a madman than a sane individual.— A fine of ss. and costs was imposed. TUESDAY. Before Councillor H. RolJerts (in the chair), and Messrs. J. Hepherd, C. Jackson Holmes, and I'. -Locker. ",Going On Very Well."—Annie Mary Kier- man, a married woman, of 115, Mersey-street, Warrington, again appeared to answer a charge of obtaining, by false pretences, groceries to the value of ..t2 14s. from Sarah Miller, of 35, Thelwall-lane, between November 14th and December 3rd.—The Chief Constable said that the Police Court Missioner and the N.S.P.C.C. Inspector had given their assistance in the case, and during the week over which the case ' had been adjourned the woman had been going.' on very well. He asked for a fortnight's Bd-.1 journment, that the supervision might continue, —The adjournment was granted, bail being again allowed, with the woman's, husband a. surety. A Scene In Gas-street.—Joseph Green, of 13. Gas-street, Warrington, was charged with assaulting a police-constable, in Gas-street, 09. Monday night; and Beatrice Green, his wife; was also charged with being drunk and dis- orderly at the same time and place.—lt stated that a constable saw the woman lying co. the ground, bleeding. When he went up to he a crowd of about twenty gathered, becomin very hostile; and the woman's husband cam' up and struck the constable.—The woman wqi fined 10s., and her husband ss. THURSDAY. Before Messrs. J. Rymer Young (in the chair) A. J. Jolley, and W. T. Williams; and Dr J. B. Naden. The Punctured Tyre.—George Douthwaite., cab-driver, was summoned for using obseefl. language in Winwick-street on Saturday niglit".. —Douthwaite admitted the offence.—Thoina: O'Toole, of 20, Wellington-street, Warringt°,f a taxi-driver, in the employ of Mr. J. W. Lakes of the Lion Hotel, said that a lady and gentman engaged his taxi at the Central Stati°"i Douthwaite wanted the fares; and, as the was starting, starting, a tyre suddenly went down, %Is. defendant started using very objections, 0. language. He (witness), however, drove on eos distance so that Douthwaite would not get 1,4 job.—Defendant said he had been driving 1, Warrington for 35 years, but the Chief coil_</p>
