      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>GUARDIANS AND PEW RENTS AT PADGATE.</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS AND PEW RENTS AT PADGATE.</p>
        <p>"TOLL ON YOUNG CHILDREN."</p>
        <p>No Payment to Be Made.</p>
        <p>The question as to whether the Warrington Board of Guardians should pay pew rents to the Padgate Parish Church in respect of the chil- dren from the Cottage Homes attending ser- vices therein, was again discussed at the fort- nightly meeting of the Board on Wednesday, when Mr. A. J. .T.olley presided. The Clerk read the minutes of the previous meeting, which contained the item: "Mr. Mather gave notice that at the next meeting he would move that a sum of £l5 be paid to the Padgate Parish Church as pew rents.'" Mr. Mather: That is wrong. My notice was altogether different. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: Is there any record of it in writing?</p>
        <p>A QUESTION OF MEMORY. The Chairman: It is a question of memory. Mr. Mather said he gave notice "that the present arrangement under Which the Chaplain is paid a salary of .t 25 a year be rescinded, and that the Board revert to the old method of pay- ing ~tl.O salary and £l5 for the use of the church." He expressed the hope that notices of Motion would be taken down in writing in future. The Clerk mentioned that the Chairman had ruled that the notice of motion was not neces- sary. He hoped, for his own protection, that notices would be given in writing in future. Subsequently the minutes were confirmed by eight votes to three. When another matter was being discussed, and Mr. Mather asked if he should give notice of a motion in writing, the Chairman explained that it was not necessary at a Board meeting, but it should be done if notice. were given to the Clerk at his office.</p>
        <p>Mr. McKinley: Mr. Mather is in an antagbn- istie mood this morning. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>VACANT CHAPLAINCY AT THE PADGATE HOMES.</head>
        <p>THE VACANT CHAPLAINCY AT THE PADGATE HOMES.</p>
        <p>The Clerk reported that at a meeting of the I Padgate Homes Committee it was moved by Mr. Mather and seconded by Mr. Bent, "that the Bishop of Liverpool be asked to nominate clergymen for the chaplaincy at the Cottage Homes." The Rev. A. M. Mitchell proposed and Mr. Brooks seconded the amendment "that the Clerk approach the incumbents of Warring- ton to ascertain when they or their curates could undertake the duties at the Homes at a salary of ..C25 per year." Three voted for the amendment and six against. A further amend- ment was proposed by Mr. Brooks and seconded by Miss Broadbent, "that the Bishop of Liver- pool be asked to nominate clergymen for the position of chaplain, and that his Lordship be informed that the stipend attached to the office is ..t25 per year." On this amendment being put to the meeting, as a motion, five voted for and four against, and it was recommended ac- cordingly. It, was also recommended that the Rev. ,H. Wadeson be appointed temporary chaplain at the Cottage Homes at a salary after the rate of £25 per annum until "a permanent chaplain is appointed."</p>
        <p>After disCussion the minutes were confirmed, and the recommendation,s adopted. Mr. Mather being the only one to vote against them.</p>
        <p>A ROSE BY ANY OTHER NAME</p>
        <p>When Mr. Mather's I-oiiol;.erm-e.-on he said</p>
        <p>, he would move that a sum of ,Rl5 be paid to the Padgate Parish Church, not as pew rents, , but for heating, cleaning, lighting and repair- ing. Some people, he said, objected to the term "pew rents," but it did not matter to him What the donation was called. The Chairman: In the form in which you now submit your motion we shall have to get the sanction of the Local Government Board. Mr. Mather: If the Board wishes it to go as it was I am satisfied. The Chairman mentioned that pew rents were paid by the Board at Martinscroft. The original motion was then) discussed, Mr. Seaton having seconded it. The Rev, AS-M. Mitchell said the best reason for opposing the motion was that the payment of pew rents was illegal. The Chairman: If the Board choose to make the payment in this form they can do so. The Rev., A. M. Mitchell: You have to go deeper than that. The Chairman: We don't need to go, any deeper._ .</p>
        <p>_ . The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: The Board has no right to vote anything for a .purpose NVhich illegal. They have no right to exclude the children in the Homes from the Churca.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mather: There is no question of exclu- sion.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. 31. Mitchell: You will be com- pelled to admit these children. Mr. Mather: There is no question about that. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell You demand that which is not right. If the churchwardens put any difficulties in the way a complaint will be made to, the Bishop. I hope the Board will give a direct negative to Mr. Mather's motion. "MORALLY AND RELIGIOUSLY ROTTEN." The Chairman: If the Board choose to pay this there is no illegality whatever.</p>
        <p>In answer. to Mr. McKinley, the Chairman said the .tl5 then proposed would be in addi- tion to the other</p>
        <p>Mr. .Dale opposed the idea of placing a toll on the young children attending -the Rouse of God. It was bad in principle, whether right, legally, or not. Morally and religiously it was rotten. Most of the churches were anxious to get the young people into their places of wor- ship, and it was wrong that the children should have to pay a toll in any shape or form. "Whilst they are children," said the speaker, "deal with Ahem as children, and when they become adults allow them to please themselves as to whether they pay for •the senrice and ac- commodation or not. I go to a clinrch some- times and do not pay pew rents." Mr. Brooks agreed with the Rev. A. M. Mit- chell and Mr. Dale. He was of the opinion that they ought not to pay at Martinscroft. CHAIRMAN'S VIEW OF THE ISSUE. The Chairman said the children could not give any money of their own. The question was as to whether the Guardians , should make a voluntary contribution to the church on behalf of the children attending. On a vote being taken, five voted for Mr. Mather's motion and eight against.</p>
        <p>IN THE COURTS—continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WELCO</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>" Call Him Louder." Thursday's "Elijah" performance brought to my mind a somewhat amusing correspond- ence occurring many years ago in a paper in which I was interested at the time. One of those 'superior persons who would have it sup- posed that they have far outgrown Mendelssohn opened the controversy, and showed at the very outset that he totally misapprehended that whereof he professed to write, instructively. • Among other strange objection, he declared that the music to the words "Baal, we cry to thee," only resembles an impatient mob in its noise. This brilliant bit of Criticism was promptly met by the comment, "winch is exactly what any sane person would imagine it was intended to represent. If the whole multi- tude of Israel did not call upon Baal, certainly the prophets did, and we are told that there were four hundred., and fifty of them. If the independent shouting of four hundred and fifty frenzied heathen prophets did not resemble 'an impatient mob' in its noise, what would?" Another hastened to point out that once the chorus have risen to give the invocation to Baal they do not resume their seats until the whole is finished; so that when Elijah says " Call him louder," the chorus cannot get up, because they are up already! Yet one more, following the same line, observed—" Mendelssohn uses all his force in the first 'Baal we cry to thee,' so that when Elijah • says Call hum louder,' the oblig- ing chorus gets up and calls him, certainly not louder and probably not quite so loud." Apart from the fact that Mendelssohn does not use all orchestral resources in the first chorus, an effective rejoinder was easily forthcoming. It was something like this: When Elijah said "Call him louder" did he mean it in the same spirit as one gives an order to an inferior? As- suredly not. It was spoken (fancy having to Point out so patent a fact !) in bitter mockery, —the acme of derisive sarcasm! How could they make more row, when they had already yelled to the full capacity of lungs and throat?</p>
        <p>" Good King Wenceslas." . The incidental allusion to the fine old carol in the introduction to last week's special article in view of Christmas is my excuse for a rather more detailed. reference which may at least interest some of " Uncle Ned's" nephews and nieces. As a matter of fact, we do not hear so much of tigs carol as we might and ought. The vicar of ale parish in whiefn I lived years ago trained a number of his Sunday school children to sing this and several others, and the whole were given in church one memorable Sunday afternoon, in the Christmas season.. I. went and was much impressed, and was never able to understand why the admirable experi-, ment did not lead to more services of the kind. But to the story. It is to the effect that old King Wenceslas left his own comfortable abode during a snowstorm to cheer the heart of a poor woodcutter with substantial and season- able creature comforts; and that, wild as the night was, the page who accompanied, him found life and warmth in the footsteps of the good king. Of course the moral of this delight- ful fancy is that true pleasure and happiness may be found in treading in the footsteps of one whose soul overflows with brotherly love, and whose life abounds in good deeds. While it is impossible for anyone to succour all the afflicted and cheer all the downcast, each can do a share in his or her own sphere. I rejoice in the thought that, while the need, was per- haps never so great, our people, irrespective of creed and minor considerations, are at present wonderfully united by bonds of practi- cal sympathy 'for the wounded, sick, bereaved and 'distressed.</p>
        <p>Dickens and Christmas. • This is what one understands as the Dickens' spirit, of which " Examiner " readers were so forcibly reminded in last Saturday's' issue— though, to be sure, it is much older than the master-novelist; he simply re-discovered the joyousness of the season, depicting it and en- forcing its lessons in his own inimitable and irresistible way. Eight through the ages the Christmas spirit has caught men in its toils, • and filled them- with goodwill to their fellows, particularly the poor and unfortunate. In the Middle Ages—which I am always careful not to describe as " dark " ages—f here was no doubt a good deal of rough and boisterous mer- riment, what with their munamings and mask- ings and hobby-horses, and their gargantuan feasts of boar's head and capons, mince pies and plum porridge, and other things calculated to shock the modern vegetarian and turn the twentieth century representative of the Fat Boy olive green with envy and regret. How the village squire of even less than a century ago loved to dispense bounty to the poor and hos- pitality to all comers at this season especially ! It is good to turn up one's " Pickwick " and " Bracebridge Hall," and to read again and again how the Hall was the pivot round which the Christmas festivities turned. By-the-bye, a dip into "Scarsdale" or "Mistress Barbara Cunliffe" assures the reader that the Wash- ington Irving tradition is likely enough to be perpetuated. Of all Christmas stories, how- ever, Dickens' immortal " Carol " is easily first. I remember reading somewhere that the conception of this classic, which has been well described its the finest charity sermon ever preached, came as a flash to the master as he was hurrying through the grimy, streets of Manchester in 1843. Six thousand collies were sold on the day of publication, and 2,000 more 'were demanded immediately. Dickens himself had anticipated great popularity for the work, and counted on clearing £l,OOO by it. The total profits amounted to £726. When, just after his thirty-second birthday, the account showed that the sale of the first 6,000 had yielded a surplus of only .4:230, he impulsively . determined to break with his publishers, and leave London for Some cheap seaside place; but he was soon induced to resume the old relations with Messrs. Chapman and Hall, with mutually- beneficial results -known and read of all men.</p>
        <p>Not An Intentional Killjoy, But— Every now and again some individual arises to tell us Why We Are, and Whit We At and How We Know Why We Are, and How We Are What We Know, and What We Would be If We Weren't, and What, Precisely, Areness Is, also Wereness, and Why We Aren't What We Mightn't Have Been If We Weren't, and other simple and entrancing facts. Such an indivi- dual is called a philosopher." So humorously remarked the "Sydney Bulletin" a while ago. I somehow thought of it as I read the doughty Vicar's cheerful Christmas contribution in the current number of the Burtonwood • Parish Magazine. How the reverend gentleman does like to make the flesh-eater's flesh creep, to be sure; but why not so the whole hog—tut! tut that will never do: I must try again—why, in the name of consistency and logic, when lie advocates the banishment of beer from the festive board at the Workhouse, does he not include in his protest at the Guardians' meet- ing the "charred corpse" which, along with 0.8.5. the great, confessedly fills him—again, ho, no: dear me, how very awkward—excites ibis horror and indignation? I have a great deal of respect for-the plucky Vicar of Burton- wood; he has plekty of backbone--do:es to be a Daniel, and all that sort of thing, you know; hut really, I think he might be a leetle :incre Charitable in his dealings with his w..e,ker brethren. It is not given to all to be Spartans. Extreme asceticism does not come natural to many, and the peculiar form of austerity in favour at Burtonivood—l bear in mio that Mr. Mitchell is, among other things, 4. no-break- fitsteris unlikely to appeal with any success to the majority of those so vigorously denounced. 'Or, look you, the manner of the advocate in inviting us, at least by implication, to the unusual mortification of a Barmecide feast by Way of celebrating Christmas, • is repellant rather than persuasive. Would it not be as well to try some other tack? I make the sug- gestion in all humility and diffidence—and as genuine, admirer and well-wisher ivlui has on rtain occasion taken the unpopular side along ith the good Vicar. As to this latest and per- ;laps hottest bit of argument, its weaknesses av'e been effectively exposed by anticipation lt,l these columns—l do not mean by mYseif. "lough I could add materially to 'what I have I)revionsly written on the subject from personal 7,nowledge and experience, but in an article 'ttatled "Common-sense in eating" and in a tonious extract from an eminent doctor's eon- gibution on "Where vegetarianism fails." I Zttl give the references more particularly if ,teeseary, but for the present my space is quite "4,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Rousing Call to the Young Men of Warrington.</head>
        <p>A Rousing Call to the Young Men of Warrington.</p>
        <p>MUCH AS YOU HAVE DONE, MORE REMAINS I</p>
        <p>"The honour of the mightiest Empire the world has over known depends on the young men of England to-day."</p>
        <p>upon—and properly spat upon—by every French- man and everyone. w .o appreciated the treaties of nations and the obligations, they imposed? We did what all of us knew England was bound to do. We stood up for the honourable obliga- tions of nations and for the freedom and rights of, and of justice to, small communities. (Cheers.) MARTYRED, DEVASTATED BELGIUM.</p>
        <p>Thus read the motto suspended across the back of the spacious platform at the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening, when was held a great public meeting, the primary ~idea of which was to stimulate recruiting and which was addressed by Mr. Horatio Bottomley—financieg, politician, sportsman and journalist; a man who by sheer force of his remarkable personality, allied, shall we say, with a trenchant pen and with a certain unorthodoxy that adds a piquancy to most of his utterances and to much that he has done and does in the sphere of public life, has secured for himself a big following in the country. Until comparatively recently Mr. Bottomley sat in the House of Commons as the representative of South Hackney. Originally returned as a Liberal, it was not long before he found himself at variance with his party, and for some time his position in the Mother of Parliaments might be likened to that of a stormy petrel, or of one, Rosebery-like, " ploughing his lonely furrow."</p>
        <p>, We have seen the result up to now! We have seen the ghastly spectacle of martyred, devas- tated Belgium, the handiwork of an Imperial criminal. There is not; I suppose, one of us in this hall to-night who has .:•ot relatives an friends .fighting in France or Belgium to-day, and I believe no eloquence—certainly none to which I can lay claim--coald do justice to the horrors through which lkose countries ate pass- ing. In conclusion, Mr. Smith introduced Mr. Bot- tomley to the audience, describing him as "the most distinguished advocate of a business Gov- ernment," and as one who came "with no ulterior motive, but to preach that gospel which was the gospel of the whole United Kingdom to-day," and adding that to-day they had for- gotten all political controversy. Mr. Arthur Crosfield—whom they welcomed on the plat- form as one of the most distinguished citizens of Warrington—(applause)—had, during his political career, said very many unkind things about him (the speaker), and, might he add, more untrue things—(laughter)—but they had forgotten all that. (Applause, in which Mr. Crosfield heartily joined.) Mr. Crosfield and he were the representatives of the two greatest political parties in Warrington, and he asked them to believe him when he said that he spoke not only for himself, but also for Mr. Crosfield, when he said that no word of party controversy would ever \ .IaSS their lips unlit this .country's representatives had signed the peace of Europe in Berlin. (Cheers.) •</p>
        <p>Mr. Bottomley, Mr. Harold Smith and Mr. Crosfield Address Huge Recruiting Meetings.</p>
        <p>had they been guilty of such outrages upon humanity as those which had blackened the history of this great war? (Cheers.) Could they hesitate longer in deciding, if one or other of the races was to rule the destinies of the world, which was the more fitted—and with- out cant—and he said it with all reverence—to receive the trust at the hands of God? (Cheers.) UNPREPARED—BUT SOON PREPARING!</p>
        <p>The outbreak of war, the speaker proceeded, found this country to some extent unprepared, but they were rapidly making up for that un- preparedness, though it took time, and a longer time than might have been necessary, because certain neutral nations had been blind to their own interests and were standing aside. He could not help thinking that with the spectacle of outraged Belgium before her eyes, Italy might have considered. what her position would have been in similar circumstances. "When," said Mr. Bottomley, "Lord Kitch- ener announced to the House of Lords that a million and a quarter men were in training a cold shudder went down the backs of many German officers, and when they learn that that million and a quarter is being made into two millions it will not be long before Germany is swing for peace." (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>OUR ARMY MAY BE LITTLE-BUT IT GOES A - LONG WAY!</p>
        <p>INe all know, we all feel, that two million Brit- ish soldiers, with all the martial traditions and instincts of their race, are unbeatable by any of the armies of the world. (Cheers.) At pres- ent we have a mere handful out at the front, and yet there is not a French officer who does not tell you that they have more than- once saved the situation. (Cheers.) They don't know fear; they don't know defeat, and they are giv- ing the Germans a practical lesson in the truth of that old Jingo ditty, if you care to call it so— Our army may be little, But they've learned before to-day That a little British Army Goes a damn long way. (Cheers) But it is not quite fair to that little handful of fellows out there to leave them alone. (Hear, hear.) They are confronted with a task such as no British Army ever had to face before, and it is the bounden duty of every citizen of this Empire who prizes his Empire and his citizenship, who values his means of livelihood and all that is dear to him in this ,life, to go and give a hand at the earliest pos- sible moment. NO DOUBT ABOUT THE RESULT.</p>
        <p>With. that done, there is no doubt about the result. We are still Great Britain—(cheers)— still old England—(cheers)—still the British Empire; we have still 400,000,000 of a population to draw upon; we have still all the glorious records of our martial race behind us, and don't let us for a moment hesitate about what is going to happen in the end. If we were alone with Germany, the result would be the same. (Cheers.) But as an island race, dependent on our Navy, and with a cowardly enemy that dare not come out in the open and fight. it takes time to complete our preparations. But they are being completed-! .(Cheers.) The war, Mr. Bottomley contended, was not going to be the long-drawn-out business that some of the professional politicians seemed to think, because they could not keep ten million men fighting for more than a month or two— human nature could not endure it; equipment, food, and a thousand other considerations came into play. WHEN IT COMES TO 'PEACE TERMS!</p>
        <p>I • Mr. Bottoniley went on to talk of the terms of peace, and suggested that first of all this country should require that the German Navy, at present bottled up in the Kiel Canal, should come out—and be added to their own ! (Cheers.) They had more legitimate use for it than 'Ger- many had. 'Then they wanted , something in the shape of indemnity. The war had cost a lot of money, and they wanted some money not only to bear the cost, but to look after •those poor devils who had suffered in the war—(ap- plause)—and the dependents of those who fell. They did not want that military menace to exist in Europe any longer, and they should see to it that Genmany was split up into the independent States that existed before 1870. (Hear, hear.) Heligoland must be given back to this country. The Kiel Canal would have to be talked about, and various other matters Of that kind. And last, but not least, what were we going to do with the Kaiser? (Hear, hear, and laughter.) "I would not," said .Mr. Bottomley, "insult the memory of Napoleon by sending him to St. Helena. Build him a hut on the Manchester Ship Canal—(laughter)—and by the time he gets there he will be sufficiently demented to mistake it for Kiel! (Laughter.) lam quite serious, althOugh jocular in a sense, perhaps, when I say ' the time is drawing near when our responsible I statesmen will have to consider what they are going to do. (Hear, hear.) If "Germany does not realise the hopelessness of her position, and begin to discuss terms on which we are pre- Tared to put an'end to the fight, the alternative is simply—ghastly though it may be—to send our million and a quarter men now ready. to take the field and add 750,000 more, and fight our way, hack our way, to Berlin—but by the rules of war. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>REPRESENTATIVE PLATFORM.</head>
        <p>A REPRESENTATIVE PLATFORM.</p>
        <p>Tuesday night's meeting in the Parr Hall was presided over by the Borough Member (Mr. Harold Smith)—who wore the uniform of a Naval Lieutenant—and supporting him on the platform, among a remarkably representative company of ladies and gentlemen, were the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman Peter and Mrs. Peacock), Si4,Gilbert and Lady Greenall, Mrs. Harold Smith; Mr. and Mrs. A. 11. Crosfield, Mrs. W. Peter 113'lands, the Rev. F. E. Evans, Colonel Moggridge, Major Tyler (recruiting officer), Alderman Smethurst, Mr. C. E. Parker, Dr. Fox, and Messrs. Henry Greenall, H. It. Armitage, J. Fulton-Smith and E. A. White. In addition to the one quoted above, other striking mottoes were displayed in the hall; including the following :—" We are unworthy of our birthright if we fail Lord Kitchener now !"; " Here's to the Day' when we enter Berlin !" and "It's got to be done. Come on and do it!" Prior to the speeches, Crosfield's Prize Band played a number of appropriate selections—and Played them in their usual excellent manner— and Messrs. J. B. Poole and J. 11. Draper sang suitable songs, with Mr. Percy Smith as accom- panist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>TilE MEMBER AM) OUR ONE COMMON BOND.</head>
        <p>TilE MEMBER AM) OUR ONE COMMON BOND.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN began by saying that he had received many letters of apology for non-attend- ance, among them being one from Mr. Henry Roberts—whom they, and he, knew well, and who was absent from reasons they all regretted. In the course of his letter Mr. Roberts said "There never has been a time in our nation's history which has demanded and deserved such strenuous effort and patriotic sacrifice as the present European conflict has forced upon us.. " Warrington has already and in many ways given of her best in support of our great and rightepus cause, but the end is not yet, and every yOung ynan who, obedient to the call of duty and the Empire's needs, enrols himself amongst those who are giving all for England, will be giving substantial help in bringing the war to a speedier and successful termination, and will be helping to lay the foundations of a permanent and world-wide peace. "May I, as a life-long Liberal, express my great admiration of the true patriotism which the leaders of the Conservative party are show- ing by their unstinted support of the Govern- ment in the present -crisis. I cannot help thinking that when the,war is over, that better feeling, which the sense of our common danger has begotten, will so beneficently affect the whole field of political controversy that the march of national and Imperial progress, now temporarily checked, will be resumed with accelerated force."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>POLITICAL CONTROVERSY HUSHED. _</head>
        <p>POLITICAL CONTROVERSY HUSHED. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Smith; eiTtiio" say that no one could preside at a meeting so densely packed as was that, at a meeting so representative of all parties, all creeds, all sects, without feeling within his bosom a thrill of pride, not only in the position which he happened to hold that evening, but also at the fact that he had some association with the town of Warrington, which had for years past fulfilled such glorious tra- ditions, and which had at the present moment maintained its loyal and patriotic state. They : had on the platform many geritThmen, mid, lie might add, many ladies, who were bitter poli- tical opponents of his—they were in Warring- ton bitter politically as, perhaps, no other town was bitter—but that night those ladies and gentlemen came with no political views. (Ap- plause.) Hushed was the voice of political con- troversy. They came there a,s Britons, with one common bond—the love of their country and their determination to stand by her.</p>
        <p>ONE OF THE GREATEST CRIMES EVER PERPETRATED. They came there regardless of politics, or Of creed, with three great objects—to protest against one of the greatest crimes that had ever been perpetrated against huttanity; to reiterate their unwavering and unalterable de- termination to see the war through to the bitter and successful end; and to appeal to the youth of Warrington—the young men, who felt within their own hearts and conscienees that bhey had not those family ties which others had, and were able to do it—to give some sort of service in this time of England's need. With those three objects before them, they forgot their trum- pery, political differences, and thought only of the country which gave them birth; they thought only of the great traditions of the past; and they looked into the future with hearts full of hope and confidence. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S NOBLE PART.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S NOBLE PART.</p>
        <p>He hoped no one would imagine that by hold- ing that meeting anyone was desirous of re- proaching Warrington for the part she had played—aye, nobly played—in this great war, for there was no one, whether a native or by happy accident associated with it, who was not proud of the town's -record, and they were mak- ing that appeal that night because they did not want to leave any stone unturned in their en- deavour to get :still more Warringtonians to wear His Majesty's uniform and play their part in the present stupendous national crisis. (Hear, hear.)- They had to fight the strongest and the most dangerous enemy in the whole world. (Hear, hear.) They had to fight a nation that, had dishonoured itself; that had laid its plans: deep and long;. that hoped. and, foolishly, believed that it could seduce England and that it could terrify Belgium into a dishonourable acquiescence; a nation that, having seduced England and terrified Belgium, proposed to crush France and to bribe Russia in order that her infamous ends might be accomplished.</p>
        <p>NAPOLEONIC PLOT-BUT NO NAPOLEON!</p>
        <p>" The plot," continued Mr. Smith, "was Napoleonic, but there was no Napoleon!" (Hear, hear-) They have not seduced England, they did not terrify hat gnllant country, Bel- gium. (Applause.) They have not and will not crush France. (Applause.) And they will never EllSSifi, for Russia knows what the re- sult of this war would have been if either they or we or both of us had stood aside when honour was at Stake. Think for a moment what would have happened if this country of ours had successfully stood aside. Does anyone in She British Isles believe for one moment that the German nation would have ceased to I ave toasted The Day" P—the day when we were to be subjugated and insultod, and our country devastated. Does anyone believe that if we had stood aside, any Englishman dare show his face upon the Continent without risk of being spat</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>BOTTOMLEI"S ADDRESS. "A Plain, Blunt Man, Who Calls a Spade a Spade."</head>
        <p>BOTTOMLEI"S ADDRESS. "A Plain, Blunt Man, Who Calls a Spade a Spade."</p>
        <p>Mr. BOTTOMLEY, who was greeted with un- bounded enthusiasm, and in whose honour the great audience :sang " For he's a jolly good fellow !" began by saying that that was by no means the first occasion upon which he had listened to that welcome vocal compliment, but, almost invariably it had been upon occasions when he had paid for the beer—(loud laughter) —and went on to add: "I am Bottomley, and I daresay that most of you are surprised to observe that apparently I have no cloven hoof or horns in my forehead. I come,before you as a plain, blunt man, 'who calls a spade a spade, and, where absolutely necessary, a blasted shovel. (Laughter and loud cheers.) The pres- ence of distinguished Indies on the platform olaces limitations to one's speech." (More laughter.) Continuing, Mr. Bottomley spoke briefly of himself, asserting with a naivete that delighted the audience that he had been through prac- tically every court in the land except the Divorce Court, adding that there was a time when he was dubbed " The Napoleon of the ,ity," and that he took an interest in "the sport of kings",--not that he had any inclination for it, more than any other man, but because as the owner of on or two horses it was a. fine experience to be beaten by a short head, and then go up to the owner of the successful horse' and tell him that One was pleased he had won. (Laughter.) MEMBER AND M. CROSFIELD AT THE</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>"PENITENT FORM" (?)</head>
        <p>"PENITENT FORM" (?)</p>
        <p>Briefly, Mr. Bottomley touched upon what he dubbed his " irresponsible activities in the Douse of Commons," and went on to say that it was not as litigant, financier, racehorse owner, or politician, that he came before them that night; nor was it as the advocate of a business Government, for that was the time of political truce, and even such an obviously correct and sensible proposition as a business Government mist be put on one side until after the termination of the war, though every day of their lives they were receiving splendid les- !ions in the true principles of -business govern- ment, such as the placing at the head of every department of the State a man with special qualifications, as, for instance, the appointment of .Lord Kitchener at the head of the War Office.</p>
        <p>It was, continued Mr. Bottomley, a great pleasure to him that night to see their Member and their late Member coming to the penitent form of a business Government and admitting they had been quarrelling in he past about nothing—(laughter)—and so long as anything serious was going on they would stop their non- sense. (Laughter.) He was happy to think that if they believed in -his doctrine of politics they would not have had to make those confessions (Laughter.) MR. BOTTOMLEY EXPLAINS HIS MISSION</p>
        <p>They might ask, seeing that he came in none of the capacities, what he had come for? and his reply was that in his own unofficial manner lie was going about from one part of the coun- try to another addressing gatherings similar to that, because he felt it was a time when the representatives of every party and no party, of every sect and no sect, should do their little bit towards the solution of the grave crisis in which the country found itself. He would to heaven that he were fit to go out and shoulder a rifle, but they could not all go out to the trenches, but there was not a man in the country, whatever his age or his circumstances, who could nordo something, however. little it might be, so that iiinn the great fight was over he could look his fellow men in the face and say, "However small it was, I did my bit according to my op- portunities .and 'means." (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>CRISIS THAT IS SHAKING CIVILISATION. We are, continued the speaker,. in a crisis which is shaking the foundations of civilisa- tion. It is h crisis which is reminding us all of the very thin line which divides civilisation ,from barbarism. It is a revelation to most of us, especially to the younger men of this genera- tion, what a thin line it is Which separates savagery from what we call civilisation, and it teaches us that ,in the last resort in national crises it is the man who goes out to fight who matters, and that very little matters about any- body else. This crisis has come Upon us. lam not here to say—heaven knows any man who did would ill conceive the gravity of the situa- tion—" I told you so," but .1 am at least en- titled to say that, with my eyes unfettered r,y tne spectacles of party, I have done some little in the way of warning the country for many years past of the danger which was threatening us. (Hear, hear.) I have wondered that dur- ing the past ten years our responsible states- men did not see the gathering cloud. Gmnany has been busy preparing for years for this trial of strength. But I will. tell you of one who checked her and kept her back. We had on the throne a great diplomatist, a great ambassador of peace—l refer to King Edward the Seventh. (Applause.) Be knew his mad nephew, and he had the sufficient authority of age, influence—and experience to keep him in check.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>KAISER A BOGUS NAPOLEON.</head>
        <p>THE KAISER A BOGUS NAPOLEON.</p>
        <p>From the moment that great guarantee for the peace of the world left us, when the hand of death removed him from the throne, this am- bitious, this bogus Napoleon., broke out in his true colours, and it was only the wilfully blind who did not see that which was coming upon us. Mr. Bottomley detailed the circumstances that preceded the outbreak of war, and went on to add that if the great question now before the world was, as he took it to be, Which was to be the 'ruling race in the counSels of Civilisation, Teuton or Anglo-Saxon? which of the two races was the more fitted for the trust? In all the annals of British warfare, even when this coun- try had been dealing with the most savage tribes of the world, and had been subjected to temptations snob. as Germany had never known,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>SOCIAL REFORM INSTEAD OF ARMAMENTS.</head>
        <p>SOCIAL REFORM INSTEAD OF ARMAMENTS.</p>
        <p>When this German menace had been laid we should be able to save £40,000,000 or £60,000,000 of the .6100,000,000 we now spent annually on armaments. That was where the working man's interest in the war presented itself, Any sum up to .660,000,000 would be freed from waste to be applied to the many objects of social reform that were awaiting treatment. Hundreds of thousands of pounds at present thrown to the winds might be liberated for the benefit of the honest British working man, and instead of old- age pensions beginning at 70, they could begin at 60, 10s. a week could re paid instead of .55.; a fund could be provided for the benefit of the unemployed, and they could build healthy atv.l decent homes for the people, instead of herding them together in slums. (Applause.) No sick and helpless citizen need then be unrelieved, and there could be a great and national scheme of insurance for the benefit of the deserving sick and weak, absolutely free and national in the fullest sense. When they had got this business over, and liberated these vast sums which were at present spent upon armaments, they could build mer- chantmen instead of men-of-war. They could make ploughs insthad of cannon, and could give that help to the farming industry of England which it sorely wanted—help that vould sow, restore it to its old power and prosperity. .</p>
        <p>THE GULF BETWEEN RICH AND POOR.</p>
        <p>They could restore the old Yeomanry Regi- ments, which were the backbone of England in the days gone by, and provide work for willing workers for at least fifty years to come. That would do a great deal to remove that great gulf between rich and poor, which was the conun- drum of this country. That gulf was greater in England than in any other country, and when the cause was traced back it would be found that it was caused by that naval and military burden, the removal of which would mean much. When that burden Went many social problems would go. They would not talk about Labour versus Capital, but Labour and Capital, the two things, mutually respecting each (Aber, and working properly together. They coul I have an tinchallenged sea supremacy. By every dictate of Nature, by every historical require- ment, there should be no question in the mind of any nation as to which should be the mis- tress of the sea. They were an island nation; they were a sea power, and there must be no further challenge of their right to rule the waves in the truest 'sense of the phrase. (Cheers.) ONE THING WE MUST NOT FORGET. There was one -thing they must not overlook, whether the war lasted a long time or a short time, and that was that the time would come</p>
        <p>when such of the bpave men at the Front as were spared would come back, and in that con- nection he urged them to -see to it that the message went to the remotest trench that not only had their brave soldiers their hopes, their wishes, and their prayers, but that they a ere also prepared to pay the price for the work which they were rendering for the Empire. (Cheers.) "We don't want our young men who are fighting in the trenches at this moment to have their hearts made heavy and their minds unhinged by the thought that if they fall or become incapacitated, they will have a moment's want so long as England has a sover- eign in its coffers. Those who cannot fight should see that justice and fair treatment are secured for those who can." (Renewed cheers.) YOUNG MAN, WHY ARE yeru NOT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 19, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>DOING YOUR DUTY?</head>
        <p>DOING YOUR DUTY?</p>
        <p>' The MAYOR then proposed a hearty vote of thanks to Mr. Bottomley for his address. lii doing so he mentioned that he had several near relatives in the Army doing their duty. He was sorry to find that one of his sons, when he wanted to enlist, was rejected, but a youngor son had been accepter], and he hoped would do his best that he might be proud of him. (Hear, hear.) He noticed, night after night, in War- rington, hundreds of young men, and when he looked at them he thought of the call of the South Lancashire lads. He felt inclined to say, "Young fellows, why are you not doing your duty?" He wanted to appeal most strongly to every one not debarred by family ties to go into training, so that this country might show two or three million men to the Germans, so that they ..might see that the game was up, and that they could not and would not beat us. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>BRITISII CHIVALRY AND COURAGE</head>
        <p>BRITISII CHIVALRY AND COURAGE</p>
        <p>Grosfield and the Immortal Glory</p>
        <p>That Is " Tommy's "1</p>
        <p>Mr. CROSFIELD, in seconding, said he was right glad to follow his friend the Mayor, and en- dorse the appeal which, in his strong and manly way, he had made to the young men of War- ringthn. And yet, he had a feeling that it was hardly necessary to make any appeal in War- rington, or in England either; because he felt strongly that throughout the land the young men had a pretty good notion where their duty rested at this present juncture. (Cheers.) The people of England had done Magnificently. It must strike a chill, like the cold of death, into the heart of the Kaiser as he realised the enormous numbers that were training, and who, in a very short time, would be taking the field against him. THE ALL-IMPORTANT FACT.</p>
        <p>But at the same time it was impossible to over-emphasise the fact—the all-important fact —that it was only by amassing greater and ever greater forces that we should bring home to the Germans at the earliest possible moment the futility and, from their own point of view, the overivhelming and abysmal disaster which must be the inevitable consequence to them of continuing the war, upon which they had em- barked, if they persisted in going on to the bitter end. (Hear, hear.) It was an easy task in England to-day for any man to stand on a platform and persuade the youth of the country to go and take their share in the fighting—to face the hardships—aye, and the risks, too! It was not on one ground only that they made an appeal to the young and the strong of Britain; but on many grounds. Duty had been spoken of that night, and there was no nobler duty than fighting for one's country. And then there was the question of chivalry, and he, for one, was glad to think that if they had no share in what was called the new Kultur, they had still got a good share of chivalry left in Old England. It was to-the chivalry of Britain that the appeal came.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>CRY OF THE WOMEN AND CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>THE CRY OF THE WOMEN AND CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The moment they thought of the fearful scenes of human misery extending oVer so large a part of the great central plain of Europe, where the most awful tragedy that had ever been known to human history was being enacted; the moment they thought of the appeal that came from the women and children, who were suffering untold agony and despair in the war-torn countries of Europe, their chivalry and courage were aroused—they were ready to respond to that appeal, which, he supposed, was about as strong an appeal as could well be made, viz., the appeal to honour. Aye, a won- derful appeal was being made to them on the score of honour and immortal glory. Take any family that any one of them knew, in which a grandfather, great , grandfather, or a great uncle, or any other relative had the deathless privilege of taking part in the memorable con- test at Waterloo! Was there a single incident in the history of that family that was referred to with such pride as the fact that an ancestor took part in that historic contest? They could go a great deal further back than Waterloo— they could go back 2,300 or 2,400 years! Was it not true that the civilised world to-day still rung with the traditions of the literally immor- tal glory of the hetbes who fought at Then- mopylve, Marathon and Salamis? He had the privilege, thanks to his friends, Mrs. and Miss Broadbent, of talking the other afternoon to some of those heroes who were at Raddon Court, And he had never in his life spent a more absorb- ingly interesting couple of hours than he did in, listening to the vivid and graphic accounts they gave him of the struggle, and of the kind of work which their men had to face in the terrific conflict. As he ventured to say to the men: " Well may the world ring with the name and fame of those heroes of antiquity, who fought 2,400 years ago, but I am very certain that 2,400 years hence the world will still be ringing with your name and faille!" BRITISH COURAGE, ENDURANCE AND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>DETERMINATION.</head>
        <p>DETERMINATION.</p>
        <p>When one got at first-hand evidence of the courage, endurance and determination that the British soldiers had shown and were showing in this present war, it made one feel that every man Jack of them deserved the Victoria Cross. (Cheers.) In the ages long ahead their name and fame would stand high; would occupy the highest place in the role of history; would stand out brightly in human memory, when many a monarch, many a king had been for- gotten. When history looked back upon these days, they would be written down as memorable for the really magnificent courage displayer by the British troops—courage which had never been surpassed in the history of the world. (Cheers.) But these days, and the events of them, would be looked back upon by generations yet to come for other reasons, too. They would be for ever memorable because of the magni- tude of the issues, and because of tife marvel- -101114 results of the peace which would be achieved .at the end of this war—by far the greatest of all wars, greatest in its magnitude, greatest in reSpect of the numbers engaged in it, and greatest, above all, in the results that would be achieved in this country, and the immeasurable and illimitable advantages to humanity that would result from it. (Ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>AN EPITAPH FOR WILLIAM 11.</head>
        <p>AN EPITAPH FOR WILLIAM 11.</p>
        <p>In conclusion, Mr. Crosfield asked: "What sort of picture do you think will be drawn when Germany ceases from this struggle? I won't follow Mr. Bottomley in the most ingenions-aild entertaining suggestion he made as to what we should do with the Kaiser, but I will suggest what might be written about him—" the man whose game was empire and whose dice were human bones." (Cheers.) I think when his end comes, if they want an epitaph to put on his tombstone, they might do worse than just have these lines :</p>
        <p>Since he miscalled the Morning Star, Nor man nor fiend has fallen so far.</p>
        <p>(Cheers.) That couplet was written about a much bigger man than the Kaiser; it was written a century ago, of Napoleon. The Kaiser was a smaller man than Napoleon, hut the couplet fitted him well, all the same. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>GERMANY'S NOTE ALREADY CHANGED. Continuing, Mr. Crosfield said he thought it was very interesting and very significant, how the note had already changed in Germany. They did not now hear quite so much about the snaashing up of every neighbour that hap- pened to stand in the path of the Germans; they did not hear so much of "blows from the mailed fist." No, the Germans were content, nowadays, to say that they were " going to hold on."</p>
        <p>The meeting terminated with a vote of thanks to Mr. Harold Smith (for presiding at the .opening of the meeting) and the Mayor (who presided over the latter part), the compliment being accorded on the proposition of Sir Gilbert Greenall, and being aCknowleaged by the Mayor. The singing of the National Anthem 'brought a memorable meeting to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>BIG OVERFLOW MEETING.</head>
        <p>BIG OVERFLOW MEETING.</p>
        <p>MORE ROUSING SPEECHES AT THE ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>So big was the demand for tickets for the Parr Hall meeting that early after their issue it became evident that the Parr Hall could net hold one-half of those whcL were desirous of hearing Mr. Bottomley, and so the Royal Court Theatre was booked for an overflow meeting, and that large building, too, was packed, the audience including many ladies, to whom, save for the few privileged guests on the platform, admission was denied at the Parr Hall. WARRINGTON'S EXAMPLE.</p>
        <p>Mr. PETER RTLANDS, who presided over the overflow meeting, said that he believed that the percentage a men who had joined the colours from Warrington would compare favourably with the percentage from any other town in Great Britain. He did not want to say that the time had come when Warrington could say " Hold! Enough!" and leave others to do the work, but he did say that in Warrington they had the satisfaction of knowing that in the matter of recruiting, as in many other things, they had set an example to the rest of the coun- try. (Cheers.) MANY GONE—STILL MORE WANTED.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT spoke at length on the solidarity displayed throughout the Empire on the declaration of war, and said that, whilst Warrington had already done remarkably well in the matter of recruiting, he .would still urge every young man in the town who was physi- cally fit, and who had not already offered him- self, to go and fight for his country. The more men that went the sooner the greatest horror of all ages would be at an end. (Hear, hear.) This would be a war to end war. MORALS v. MATERIALISM.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. WYNNE JONES said that we could not have kept out of the war without eternal dis- honour to ourselves. (Hear, hear.) There had been a ready and noble response to the appeal for recruits, but more men were needed to add to the nation's glory by adding to the nation's victories. (Hear, hear.) There was a great deal at stake in the war. There were millions of pounds at ptake, and the trade of the country, as well as the very living of some people, was affected. But there was something more than those things at stake. The war in which they were engaged was a war of moral ideals against materialistic ideals. (Hear, hear.) They were not going to exchange their social order, which was based on justice, for that which was based on tyranny, draped with I,Cultur, or for that which was based on barbarism draped in the garb of civilisation. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Peace and Truth Instead of Militarism.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jones related many stories of bravery and courage on the part of the British soldiers, and said that Kitchener of Khartoum, who had been re-named Kicker of Kaisers--(laughter)--had ap- pealed for more recruits, and he was sure that Warrington would make still greater efforts and send all the men she' could. They had great obligations to fulfil, and they should concen- trate all their powers and resources, so that when the end came, it would bring victory to the Allies, and the terrible force of militarism would be beaten down, and righteousness, peace and truth would reign in its place. (Applause.) A TASK FOR MOTHERS, SISTERS AND</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>SWEETHEAATS.</head>
        <p>SWEETHEAATS.</p>
        <p>Mr. CHARLES PLINSTON made";:stirring appeal for recruits, and asked the mothers, sisters, and sweethearts to encourage the menfolk to enlist so that the gallant men in the cold and dreary trenches would be spurred on by the thought that comrades were coming to help them. They should not let one of the soldiers be able to say, "Where is my friend Bill? Ile is not the man I thought him." "Young men," concluded Mr. Plinston, "show that you are Prepared to take your part in the great war, and if you have to face dangers remember that—</p>
        <p>A man cannot die better than facing fearful odds, For the ashes of his fathers and the Temple of his Gods." (Applause.)</p>
        <p>RUSSIA'S SHARE.</p>
        <p>Mr. RYLANDS then detailed the activities of the Russian troops, who, he said, did not come as much before our notice as the French and Belgians. and spoke of the great part they had played in the war in occupying German -troops which would otherwise have been in Belgium and France.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>NOW, THEN, MEN!</head>
        <p>NOW, THEN, MEN!</p>
        <p>Mr. norromm, whose arrival had been eagerly awaited, then made his appearance, and re- peated, in essence, the speech he had delivered in the Parr Hall, adding that they should let the message go forward that there was not an able-bodied man in Warrington and in Eng- land who was not prepared to do his share in the great 'battle. (Applause.) Mr. HAROLD ,SMITU, M.P., who also had found his way across from the Parr Hall, proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Bottomley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>MOST MERCIFUL_ THING.</head>
        <p>THE MOST MERCIFUL_ THING.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD seconded, and said Mr. B2ttomley had made two magnificently inspir- ing speeches. He (the speaker) was not sure that, at the present time, any appeal was. espe- cially needed to convince the young fellows of the seriousness and the gigantic issues that were at stake, and to make them realiiie where their duty lay. (Hear, hear.) He hoped the recruiting would go on, even better than it had i been going on. If t did, it would be the most merciful thing that could be done for Europe, in helping to bring the war to an earlier end; in showing Germany the overwhelming blunder she had made, and also in making her realise that, overwhelming as would be the catastrophe for the Fatherland, she had befter cut her losses, face the inevitable, and endeavour to make such reparation as she could, and en- deavour partially to expiate that which it was</p>
        <p>impossible wholly to expiate, viz., the colossal and unprecedented crime against all civilisa- tion. (Hear, hear.) He did not know whether anyone felt inclined to show much mercy to the Germans, but he felt sure the most merciful thing they could do would be to put them out of their misery as quickly as possible. (Hear, hear.) THAT BUSINESS GOVERNMENT The vote of thanks having been carried,</p>
        <p>Mr. BOTTOMLEY, in reply, remarked, in answer to %hat Mr. Crostield said about•not being able to End another Bottomley in the House, that</p>
        <p>he had never .seen his Way to join either of the Ministries in the past, and he did not pro- pose to take a seat on the Treasury Bench until he formed the first business Government. (Hear, hear.) He proposed a vote'of thanks to the Chairman, and "being an advocate of a business Government" he would second it, and ask them to help him to carry it. The audience did.</p>
        <p>During the evening Mrs. E. A. Sanders and Mr. J. Draper rendered two songs in Pleasing and acceptable manner, and the orchestra, led by Mr. J. Roy, played several patriotic selec- tions. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <p>symptoths of kidney- patient suffers from them other symptoms are :—Backaelie, Urinary )sy, Rheumatism,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>HOW THE KAISER HAS BENEFITED</head>
        <p>HOW THE KAISER HAS BENEFITED</p>
        <p>BRITAIN!</p>
        <p>After the meetings a big torchlight proces- sion—headed by Crostield's Band—wended its • way through thronged streets to the Town Hall, where the National Anthem was simg before the crowd dispersed. .• Afterwards several prominent gentlemen Ad- journed .to the Mayor's Parlour, where His Worship thanked those who had helped to make the meetings successful. Mr. HAROLD, SMITH acknowledged the courtesy t and kindness of His Worship. After emphasis- ing the fact that at the present time party differences were entirely in abeyance, and that men of all parties had co-operated for the good of the country, Mr. Smith said that a seat in the House of Commons counted as nothing be- side the great issue with which the country was face to face to-day, and it mattered nothing whether he or Mr. Crosfield represented War- rington.</p>
        <p>In the course of a brief speech, Mr. CROSFIELD referred to Mr. Thu-old Smith's timely and ap- propriate remarks, and said it was difficult for the German Emperor at the present time to awaken in the breast of a single Britisher any feelings of gratitude to himself. Still, as it was . an ill-wind than blew nobody good, so it was certain that in two respects even the Raiser had benefited this country. He had in the first instance done us a good turn by creating that very unity of opinion and of action throughout the country to which Mr. Harold Smith had referred, and the like of which had never been seen before in the whole course of our political history. The Kaiser had also evoked, from end to end of the British Empire, feelings of genuine, heartfelt and enthusiastic loyalty. coupled with a spirit of willing eo-operation and self-sacrifice such as he (Mr. Crosfield) had no doubt had struck a chill cold as death into the heart of the Emperor and his reckless and mis- guided advisers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>MOTHER OF LARGE FAMILY</head>
        <p>MOTHER OF LARGE FAMILY</p>
        <p>Tells How She Keeps Her Health—Happiness For Those Who Take Her Advice.</p>
        <p>t to tell you how much good -m's Vegetable Compound and Sanative Wash have done me. lam forty-five years old, and am the mother of thirteen children. Many people think it strange that I am not broken down with hard work and the care of my family, but I tell them of my good friend, Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable 'Compound, and that there will be no backache and bearing-down pains for them if they will take it as I have. I am scarcely ever without it in the house. "I will say also that I think there is 130 better medicine to be found for young girls. My eldest daughter has taken Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound and it has helped her development. , "I am always ready and willing to speak a good word for Lydia, E. Pinkham's Vegetable Compound. I tell every one I meet that I owe my health and happiness to your wonderful medicine." (Name and address on request). Lydia E. Pinkham's Vegetable Com- pound, made from simple roots and herbs, contains no narcotics or harmful drugs, and is now acknowledged to be the most sucrescful remedy for woman's ills known. Write to-day for a free private book (to women only) to the Lady Manager, the Lydia E. Pink-ham Medicine Co., 42a Ilampstea,d Road, London, N.W. a For 2s. 9d. she will send you a bottle of the Lydia E. Pinkbam Vegetable Compound, or for 4s. 6d. a much larger bottle. Any chemist will supply it at these prices.. -</p>
        <p>11111</p>
        <p>want Ly,?.ta E. Pinkha. •gz. , . . iig:7::', . N</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
