      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>TEE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEiVIBER 19, 1914</head>
        <p>TEE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEiVIBER 19, 1914</p>
        <p>410 0) • 400 41&gt; 0 0 101410 040 IP 44/ i&gt; 044 I) 4. 1014 P .000 0 0 0 0 00.0 0 0. 0 0 40:•• 4* •:e 4* • 44. 09 II:11 •:i. G 40 II) •:. 40 44 ..,./ 41 0,/ 011 •* 41 41)* ..), 44. 4. 4. 4* .&gt; 4. 4* 4. .3 40 4..3 4. 4. +444.* 4.4. 04, 0 4.4-0 4. 4' 4. 4. 4* .:. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4.. 4. 4. 4. +4. •••• 4. 4. . 4. •.•, ..:* • .0. ele • ** WOMAN'S .:..:. .:.., 4.:..:..), s.D ID 4* 0) 0 0 * • ' I 4. 1 lig I ...-.A. ++ 0 • 0 •</p>
        <p>t -- CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>The Good Ship "Loyalty." Most of the Royal Houses of India have sent sons, brothers, or other near relatives to France, to fight for the Empire, and there has been no more striking outcome of the war than the many manifestations of loyalty on the part of the Indian Princes. Among the States which assisted and protected Europeans at the time of the Mutiny was that of Bhopal, now governed by a remarkable Indian Princess, the Begum. She has, in conjunction with another great Indian Princess, the Maharanee of Scindia, fitted out a hospital ship to accommo- date five hundred wounded soldiers. It is aptly named the "Loyalty," and has been Sent to Europe, where it is to be hoped it will arrive in safety, and do the good work its generous donors have planned.</p>
        <p>Cambridge Certificates, for Women. A propositior will be brought forward at Cambridge University in the near future to consider the desirability of conferring a certi- ficate on women who have distinguished them- selves in the various fields of research, and there is a likelihood that some kind of recognition will be granted. Perhaps some day British Uni- versities will have the courage to confer honor- ary degrees on distinguished women. The Rus- sian Puma has passed a Bill admitting women to posts as lecturers and professors at Univer- siti s, at the same salaries as men, and women with degrees are permitted to teach boys in schools and colleges. The Revival of the Petticoat.</p>
        <p>The under skirt has brought back the petti- coat, which is now comparatively voluminous, and very much longer than of late. Some models have deep accordion-pleated frills, which fall from the knees, where they appear to be held by a band of ribbon. These petticoats, although made of the thinnest silk, are warm, because sometimes two or three thicknesses of the material are compressed in the pleating. Yet they are not in the least bulky, for not only is the silk thin, but shaped bands are used, which fit quite flat to the figure. Much ribbon is used in the adornment of these truly feminine garments. A great deal more depends on the cut of the corset than of the petticoat, if the new silhouette is to be successfully adopted.</p>
        <p>It is quite possible to greatly reduce the ap- parent size of the hips, by the choice of suit- able designs in corsets. Waistcoats in Renovations. • Tho,ornate waistcoat, cleverly used, may be depended upon to bring a last year's coat and skirt quite up to date. A crossover waistcoat of striped Roman satin, for instance, finishing in a roll-over collar, with cuffs to match, quite transforms a tailored siiit. Fur is often used for a small waistcoat, and for such an acces- sory a short-haired pelt, such as ermine, broad- tail, caracul, or Persian rand), should be chosen. These garments in fur cloth are almost equally good. A tweed model in a beautiful shade of biscuit, flecked with white, had a fur waistcoat of a good imitation ermine. With it was worn a little toque, trimmed also with fur and a necklet that matched greatly aided the good effect. The Ali-British Umbrella.</p>
        <p>Umbrellas entirely of British make are a feature of this winter's displays in the more expensive shops. The silk for these umbrellas is made in an Eastern county, on hand looms, and the covers are entirely hand-made; in fact, no machine work is used at all. The carving also on the handsome handles is the work of Brifish artists. Heads of animals form a favourite finish to a cane that fits on the arm, and the fox terrier and French bulldog, mon- key and frog, are faithfully reproduced. A novelty is a cane, provided with a lead pencil, which comes out when a small spring is touched. The more ornate handles are beauti- ful specimens of the jewelkes' craft, and many are studded with pearls and sapphires, set in gold. Enamel finds a place also. Chinese jade, tortoise-shell and gold are pressed into the ser- vice of the all-British umbrella.</p>
        <p>The Evolution of the Fox Terrier. The fox terrier is quite a modern dog, and has been with us no longer than fifty years. When first he Made his appearance lie was black and tan in colour. Originally lie was used en- tirely for kennel work, but at the end of a long fun he was not easily distinguished from the fox, therefore, several sportsmen combined to evolve the animal we know to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>By MARJORIE.</head>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>The Prince of Wales at the Front.</p>
        <p>It is interesting to note that no heir-apparent to the Throne has been on active service for five hundred years, not since the days of the Black Prince, in fact. The Prince of Wales, who has quickly attained the rank of lieuten- ant, takes the work assigned him very seriously, and is determined to do his best to help lathe great cause of the Allies. His brother, Prince Albert, who is now nearly nineteen, is most anxious to rejoin his ship, as soon as his health permits. He is at present a midship- man, but will shortly be promoted to a-lieuten- ancy.</p>
        <p>The French in the Stone Age. The War, that engrosses the thoughts of every man and woman, bids fair to transform modern warfare, as well as many other things. Military science appears to be going backward instead of forward, in spite of the inventions and discoveries of modern engineers. Trenches were the only fortifications known to man in the stone age, and they are playing a most import- ant part in this, the greatest war the world has known. The bayonet, too, and the hand grenade have both been revived, the latter after the lapse Of at least a century. For the first time, however, women have taken an active part in the care of the wounded, as doctors. Never before has a hospital been equipped entirely by women. Doctors and nurses, chauffeurs (driv- ing the motor ambulances), and the orderlies of the Scottish Women's Hospital, organised by Dr. Elsie Inglis, are all women. Several units from this hospital, which has its headquarters in France, have been sent so far as Serbia. In dealing with the wreckage of warfare, pro-, gress has certainly been made.</p>
        <p>r.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH. Useful Information in Popular Langualle.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH. Useful Information in Popular Langualle.</p>
        <p>• trlin</p>
        <p>HOME NOTES. Profitable Recipes and Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>An advocate of the vegetarian diet has stated that the nut trees of the world are capable of supplying food all the year to the population of the globe.</p>
        <p>Dry Skin. To improve a dry skin I)athe the skin in warm water and immediately after wiping rub • well in the skin glycerine and rose water. Do not use face powders. Wear a veil in the wind.</p>
        <p>Ink In Carpets. Put the stained parts in a quantity of ben- zine, then use a fine, rather stiff brush, with fresh benzine. Dry and rub bright with warm water and curd soap. Or dissolve one ounce cyanide of potassium in four ounces water; this' mixture is very poisonous and should therefore be used with great caution. Moisten the stained part with this solution by dipping it into it, or by means of a small brush, and in a few hours the stains should be obliterated.</p>
        <p>Sixty Kinds of Bananas. To most persons in the temperate zones a banana is a banana. But the truth is that there are over sixty known varieties of the fruit, with as great, or greater, variation in character as in the different kinds of apples. Hawaii is said to have something over 40 distinct varieties of the fruit, most of which have been introduced by the whites. Some of these are of extremely delicate and delicious flavour, while other kinds are used, if at.all, only when cooked in various ways. There is scarcely a city house lot, or country " kuleana " or homestead which does not have a dumb or two of bananas, NI !licit grow with practically no care, new I lants, suckers, shooting up to replace the ones which have fruited and been removed.</p>
        <p>Beetroot. To cook beetroots, they should be soaked in lukewarm water, and lightly scrubbed with a soft brush, taking care not to injure the skin.</p>
        <p>„sher (to uninvited guest): _ —'s friends to the right Uninvited Guest: "I'm afraid I'm a neutral!”</p>
        <p>and games; a nice long letter from me (if I can manage to write one); and—last but not least— the result of our Christmas Competition, together with the best essays. Now, isn't that jolly? I am hoping we shall have some more new members to welcome, also. -</p>
        <p>Your loving UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>FAITHFUL TO HIS PROMISE.</p>
        <p>-- . - A captive was brought before an Asiatic prince, and was ordered to be put to death. The scimitar (sword) was already raised above his head; when, oppressed by unbearable thirst, he asked for water. A cup was handed to him, but he held it as if afraid that the scimitar would fall while he was in the act of drinking. " Take courage," said the Prince; "Your life will be spared till you have drunk this water." Instantly the captive dashed the cup to the ground. His good faith in the Prince saved the word had passed and it was enough. The captive went on his way rejoicing. ,</p>
        <p>THE CROWS AND THE ANKLET. This is a third story which used to be told in India over three thousand years ago; and after this I shall only have one more of the series left for you to hear. A pair of crows lived in a hollow tree; and there also lived in the bottom of this tree a fierce snake, that used to eat the young of. the crows as soon as they were hatched. "What can we do?" said the hen-crow; " we can never bring up a family here." "We must get rid of the snake," replied her mate. "But," said the hen, "that is impossible, for he is too strong for. uli."</p>
        <p>Ili next day, when the eog of a king vaine</p>
        <p>Aids to Beauty. Every woman wants to be beautiful, yet how few will take the trouble to follow just a few , rules, especially in winter time, when rooms are cold, and courage is required to persist in the daily sponge clown with lukewarm water. Plenty of fresh air, plenty '-of exercise, and sleep, are. three great factors toward beauty. Unless you can retire early to bed, and then obtain at least eight hours' sleep, you cannot expect to feel " fit " during the day; and run- ning about the house, even if doing necessary work, isn't exercise and fresh air. A good walk in the open air must be taken daily. Be, regular in your hours of retiring and rising, eat good plain food, knock off some tea-drinking, and you will soon note a marked difference in your ap- pearance and health. Use of Drugs. • _</p>
        <p>The growing use of drugs so noticeable in our day, may properly be held responsible, in part at least, for the enormous increase of disease which has occurred within recent times. To the use of drugs as much as to any other cause, per- haps more than any other, if we include alcohol, opium, and tobacco, may be charged the enor- mous increase in the insane—a class of defec- tives which has multiplied 300 per cent. in fifty years; in epileptics, who have also increased 300 per cent.; and in various other classes of defectives.</p>
        <p>The saddest feature of the whole business, however, is feeding the dope to infants by ignorant mothers. To quote Dr. E. Mather Sill, Lecturer in Diseases of Children at the New York Polyclinic Medical, School and Hospital, " it cannot be too emphatically stated that the giving of patent medicines, cough and soothing syrups, patent diarrhcoa mixtures, teas, and so- called blood medicines or tonics by a mother or nurse to a young child, on her own responsi- bility, is most reprehensible and dangerous. Most of these preparations contain opium or alcohol in some form, drugs which are badly borne by infants and young children. ,</p>
        <p>down to the river close by to bathe, the male crow flew down and seized a golden anklet which the Prince had laid upon the bank. Then he hurried back to the nest, and dropped the anklet inside the hollow tree. Of course, as soon as the king's son came to the bank again after bathing, he missed the golden anklet; and every place was searched for it. At last it was found in the hollow tree; and, the serpent being also found by the Prince's attendants, it was killed. Thus were the crow's family able to live in the tree, in perfect peace and safety. Skill will make up for lack of strength.</p>
        <p>. CHRISTMAS IN WAR-TIME. (By JEssrs Sam) "Yuletide dear now is here; Silent snow lies o'er the ground. Still once more, as of yore, Happy hearths We gather round. Troubles sleep. Concord deep, Joy and mirth, peace on earth, Tenderness and love abound." Thus was the song we sang But that War's crashing clang Out rang. 'Tis long. How long? When shall we sing the song Again, Full free from pain? "Peace on earth." Ah! its birth Is not given of mortal man. "Love towards men." Yet, again, Ne'er 'twill come, for ne'er it can. Stay! At last, peril past, To the skies we shall rise; Ended then Life's sorry span. Song never known before Sung shall be evermore-- "Rejoice !" • Heed Heaven's voice.</p>
        <p>I</p>
