      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER. :11 Conducted by UNCLE NED. -,-4 A lIEARTY INVITATION to join this Corner is i extended to all children who read it regu- • larly. name, Applie ati on for membership, stating address d age, should be made to "UNCLE Nan, : ' Examiner" Office, Bewsey_ st Warrington." Every girl or boy, whose name e name is in the Big Book, is entitled to . time, ttohethecomwin)entietrisonosf lli‘e,hh ldicfrbomtimerizteos seful p le are awarded.</p>
        <p>OUR CHRISTMAS CORNER. MY DEAR CIIILDREY,— It was with pleasure that I received this week a neatly-written letter from - DOROTHY BENNETT, 42, Warburton-street, whom I welcome to our Corner very gladly. Dorothy says: "I expect all the children are looking forward to Christmas." I should think so! When I was young, I used to like it best of all the year; and, weeks , before it came along, I began to mark off the days on my calendar as they passed by. Even now,- although my hair is grey, Christmas has not lost its charm for me in the least; I am looking forward to it eagerly. But there is a special reason why the.mem- bers of our Corner should look forward to it; and now I am going to tell it to you—after keeping it a secret all this time, so as to give YOU a nice Christmas surprise ! It is this: Next week we shall have a special CHRISTMAS Cousins, twice as big as we usually have. It will contain stories and poetry; riddles, jokes</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>BANKING "AS USUAL."</head>
        <p>BANKING "AS USUAL."</p>
        <p>Warringtonians' Savings Not Lessened By War. ABNORMAL TIMES: NORMAL THRIFT.</p>
        <p>"In view of the abnormal times . . . the progress made during the past year is eminently Satisfactory !" This encouraging phrase is ex- tracted from the report on the past year's work- ing, presented to the Trustees and managers of the Warrington, Runcorn and District Savings Bank at the 97th annual meeting, which was held at the Bank, in Buttermarket-street, War- rington, on Tuesday morning, and was attended by the following : Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes (vice-chairman, who presided), W. Tinsley, Josiah Longland, G. W. Joseph, Roger C. Parr, S. C. Beckett, John Pritchard; G. Summerfield and Thomas Wright (trustees), Rev. 11. V. Pigot, and Messrs. W. Clegg, r. G. Craik, A. Down, J. G. Bill, F. W. 'Hutton, J. E. Richard- son, J. E. Seeker, and J. H. Smethurst (mana- gers), with Messrs. W. A. Proud (chief actuary), and Arthur Bennett (auditor), and other officials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>UNABLE TO ATTEND.</head>
        <p>UNABLE TO ATTEND.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. A. Frost wrote regretting that another engagement prevented him from attending, add- ing : In view of the facts of the war and of all things in connection with finance, the Bank has done very well, and has retained the confi- dence of the thrifty public, which it has striven to deserve."</p>
        <p>Mr. George Dakin also regretted that his recent serious accident would keep him away, and wrote that as his doctor had told him that he must suspend his active work for some little time to come, he felt that the moment had arrived for resigning his post as a trustee. He added that he had been connected with the Bank no fewer than fifty years. The Vice-Chairman said that the trustees had received Mr. Dakin's letter with much regret. 110 (the speaker) had talked the matter over with some of his colleagues, and they were all unanimously of the opinion that they should send a letter of sympathy to Mr. Dakin, to- gether with an expression of the hope that he would soon be restored to health, and That he would allow his name to remain on the list of trustees.</p>
        <p>The meeting concurred with the Vice-Chair- man's proposal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>"BUSINESS AS USUAL," DESPITE THE</head>
        <p>"BUSINESS AS USUAL," DESPITE THE</p>
        <p>WAR.</p>
        <p>..... In moving that the statement of accounts prepared by the actuary and examined and approved by the auditor be passed, The Vice-Chairman regretted that the Chair- man (Mr. William Long) was unable to be pres- ent that day. He was present at the last meet- ing of the Committee of Management, and the wish was then expressed that he would be able to attend the annual meeting, but he said he Was afraid it would be more than he could stand. Mr. Long still continued to take a great interest in the working of the Bank, and was well posted up in everything that went on. Continuing, the Vice-Chairman said that some of them rather thought that, in view of the extraordinary time they were now passing through, they would have had to report a con- siderable decrease this year, but he was glad to say that, so far as Warrington and. Runcorn were concerned, that was not the case. Tratie in those towns had been very good, and the thrifty people had not Yet had occasion to lay a finger on their deposits to help them out. When the question of amalgamation with the Run- corn Bank was before thetn be ventured to state that, as amalgamation had proved very successful in all the districts where it had been carried out, he thought it would also prove a good thing for the Warrington Bank, and he was new glad to be able to tell them that that prediction had been fulfilled, and they had a very nice increase of £19,000 odd on the year's working.</p>
        <p>As Safe as tbie—Warrington Savings Bank.</p>
        <p>It seemed hardly' necessary that he should say anything at all about the security of the Bank, or about the absolute safety of the de- posits which were entrusted to them, hut it waft; perhaps, as well that he should reiterate, as em- phatically as he possibly could, that the money deposited in that Bank was absolutely safe. There was not, nor could there be, any question whatever, but that any depositor who placed his sovereign with th6ra Would get it back again. Some people were rather afraid that, owing to the war, England might become bankrupt, and so on, but that, of course, was simply impos- sible, and every deposit that was placed with that Bank was under the charge of the National Debt Commissioners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Half of the Staff Volunteer for the Front.</head>
        <p>Half of the Staff Volunteer for the Front.</p>
        <p>Continuing, the Vice-Chairman said that it might be interesting to the public to know that, although they had not a very large staff at that Bank, still, half of them had volunteered for active service. (Applause.) They had under- taken to keep open the posts of such men, and to pay them salaries at the usual rate during their absence. (Hear, hear.) The committee had been able to nlake some temporary arrange- ment to meet the case, by slightly curtailing the hours at the branches and by engaging some additional assistance in the Bank. By such means they hoped to be able to maintain the high standard of efficiency that had always characterised the working of the Bank. It might he of interest to the meeting to know that during the past year, as usual, not a single account had been transferred to the Post Office, whilst, on the other hand, several accounts had been transferred from the P.O. to the Bank. (Hear, hear.) During the whole time Mr. Proud had been with the Bank lie could not recollect more than two accounts going to the Post Office.</p>
        <p>THE PANIC THAT DID NOT COME OFF. Mr. Beckett seconded, remarking that he was very pleased to be there as representing the Run- corn Bank, and more particularly so in view of the fact that the past year had been a very successful one, considering the times the coun- try was passing through, and had passed through, more particularly at the coinmence- ment of the war, when there was likely to be a serious panic. Like Warrington, they in Run- corn had not suffered to the extent that might have been reasonably expected, and he was very pleased, both for their bank and for his own, that the amalgamation took place when it did. For the first twelvemonths under the new ar- rangement, it might be of interest To them to know, over £lO,OOO had been received on account of the Special Investment Department, which. he thought, was exceedingly good for a small town like Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The Vice-Chairman's proposition was then adopted., as also was the auditor's report, in which Mr. Bennett made 'mention of the fact that every facility had been offered him by the staff, and that the books were well and carefully kept.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>97th ANNUAL REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE 97th ANNUAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>Amalgamation with Runcorn " Fully Justified." The 97th annual report of the Committee of Management stated that in view of the abnormal times the progress made during the past year was eminently satisfactory. The amalgamation of the Runcorn Bank had been . fully justified by the excellent results obtained by the opening of an investment department at Runcorn, as 60,923 had been received in this department, and there was an increase of 6424,170 tor the year in the amount due to the Runcorn depositors, who had much appreciated the in- creased facilities for saving now open to them, and had taken full advantage, thus securing the higher rate of interest allowed in this de- partment. There had been an increase of 397 in the number of accOunts, making a grand total of 19,419. The amount received from depositors is £118,306 6S. id., re'presenting 25,585 transac- tions; £120.786 2s. 2d. has been paid to deposi- tors in 13,162 transactions. The total number of transactions was 38,747, with a turnover of £239,092 Bs. 3d.</p>
        <p>Funds of the Bank. The funds of the Bank are as follows: Assets: Ordinary department, £395,342 9s. 6d.; Government Stock, £22,316 is. 7d.; „Investment department, £133,255 14s. 2d.; total, £550,914 58. 3d. Liabilities, £541,639 7s. Leaving a cash surplus of £9,274 18s. 3d. The surplus assets over liabilities inclusive of the value of the Bank premises, d:c., amount to £10,610 18s. 3d, an increase of £552 10s. 6d. for the year. The summary of deposits and payments is as follows :—Deposits, ordinary department .C 93,634 Os. 6d., investment department £24,672 ss. 7d.; purchases of stock, £420; interest added, ordin- ary department £9,274 125., investment depart- ment £3,633 50. 5d.; total, £131,534 33, ed, ray.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <p>ments, ordinary department £110,842 Os. 6d., in- vestment department .t. 9,944 Is. Bd.; sales of stock, £750; total, £121,536 2s. 2d. Increase for the year, .810,098 Is. 4d. The business transacted at the branches again showed good .I:progress. At Bank Quay, where 481,112 had been received for Head Office ac- counts, there were 539 open accounts, with £7,714 due to them; whilst at Earlestown, 391 depositors had the larger sum of £14,818 to their credit. _ . . _ . . _ _</p>
        <p>The special investment department had been very successful, showing an increase of £18,361 for the year. The total amount now due to de- positors was .R 131,031. School banks now number 42, and have stand- ing to their credit £1,866 14s. 4d. The success of these banks is duo to the ability and effi- ciency with which they are conducted by teachers in the schools, and the committee take this opportunity to renew their thanks to those ladies and gentlemen. The staff have readily responded to their country's call at this time, Messrs. Hallwood. Gillies and Darbyshire having joined Lord Kitchener's Army in September last.</p>
        <p>Mr. Seeker Then moved that the report of the Conimittee of Management be approved, and that the meeting tender the members of that body their best thanks for the able way in which they managed the affairs of the Bank. It was very creditable to them that they erere able to report such a successful year, especially in view of the war. • 'f!is was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>CHAIRMAN AND VICE-CHAIRMAN RE.</head>
        <p>CHAIRMAN AND VICE-CHAIRMAN RE.</p>
        <p>ELECTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Longland then pi:o—po—s.ed that Mr. 'Wm. Long be re-elected chairman and Mr. C. Jack- Holmes vice-chairman. He need not, he said, dwell upon the services which Mr. Long had tendered, mid, so faas he was able, con- tinued to render. No man had done more for the Warrington Savings Bank, or had taken a greater interest in it, and they only regretted that his health (lid not permit of him taking so active a part in the management as he was once able to do. Still, they had the benefit of his name and of his advice whenever it was re- quired. As to Mr. Jackson Holmes's services, be thought the present position of the Bank testi- fi to their value. Upon him had fallen the greater share of the burden of the work, owing to the Chairman's unfortunate indisposition. Mr. Summerfield, in seconding, said he was sure that the Bank was safe whilst in such hands as those of Messrs. Long and Holmes. The proposition having been carried with ap- plause, the Vice-Chairman briefly returned thanks, both on his own behalf and also on that of Mr. Long.</p>
        <p>ELECTION AND RE-ELECTIONS. Mr. Roger Parr then proposed, Mr. Richard- son seconded, and the meeting approved, the re- election of Mr. Wm. Skinner as treasurer and of Mr. Arthur Bennett as auditor. Mr. Bennett, in acknowledgment, said it was satisfactory to note that the stream of gold which began to flow, through the medium of that bank, nearly a hundred years ago, grew steadily broader.</p>
        <p>On the propositimf of Mr. Pritchard, seconded by Mr. Tinsley, Messrs. J. S. Bill, J. G. Craig, j. E. Richardson, J. 11. Smethurst and the Revs. j. Yonge and W. Braeecamp were appointed to in the trustees on the Committee of Manage- ment for the ensuing year. On the proposition of the Vice-Chairman, Mr. L. R. Rowson was elected a trustee. VOTES OF THANKS. A vote of thanks having been accorded the staff and briefly acknowledged by the Actuary —who remarked that the members of tile staff remaining behind feel it a great honour that so many of their colleagues should have volun- teered for the front--a similar compliment was paid to the Vice-ChairMan, on the propOsition of Dr. Joseph, seconded by Mr. Beckett, Mr. Jackson Holmes briefly acknowledging the com- pliment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>WYCLIFFE BROTHERHOOD.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE BROTHERHOOD.</p>
        <p>The lecturette on Sunday in connection with the Brotherhood attached to lie Bewsey-street Church was delivered by Mr. R. E. O'Callaghan, of the Warrington Anti-Vivisection Society. His subject was. " The Lesson of Great Re- forms," and in the course of his- address he sought to show that the chitf impediment to all improvements in social or political ques- tions arose from the innate conservatism of the supposed ,experts, or scientific bodies. the hostility of vested interests coupled with the indifference of the mass of the community. Instancing the experienv of Wilberforce with Negro emancipation, John Howard with prison reform, and the late Lord Shaftesbury with the reform of the lunatic asylums, the speaker showed how the opposition to these, ieforms was identical with the. existing bitter antagonism to a more just and humane treatment of ani- mals, and especially to the appeals of the anti, vivisectionist body. Now, as then, the selfish interests or mistaken beliefs of a few, com- bined with the apathy of the many, condoned a state of things more befitting savagedom than a Christian or theughtful people.—Mr. William Beswick, who presided, in conveying the thanks of the Brotherhood, commented on the . highly interesting and instructive address delivered by the Lecturer..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>RUNCORN GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday, Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., presiding. APPOINTMENTS APPROVED—</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote approv- ing`of the appointment of Messrs. James Pear- son and W. IT. Warburton as vaccination officers for the Budworth and Frodsham sub- districts, respectively, at a fee of fivepence in respect of each child entered on the births' list; a fee of is. 3d. in respect of each registration in the vaccination register of the successful vac- cination of any child born in their districts; and at a fee of is. 3d. in respect of the trans- mission by either officer to the vaccination offi- cer of the district where the birth was regis- tered of a copy, certified by him, of the certifi- cate of successful vaccination in his district of any child not born in his district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>AND SALARIES FIXED.</head>
        <p>AND SALARIES FIXED.</p>
        <p>The Local. Government Board -1-;;;ing sug- gested that a fixed salary might be paid to vaccination officers in lieu of fees, the Finance Committee recommended that the follpwing be such salaries for 1915 :—Mr. Cook (Runcorn district), .t 37 Mr. Warburton (Frodsham), ..C18; and Mr. Pearson (Budworth), £22.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>GRUEL" THE INMATES REFUSED. _</head>
        <p>THE GRUEL" THE INMATES REFUSED. _</p>
        <p>The Master reported that the inmates had refused to eat the gruel, alleging that the meal was not wholesome. The Visiting Committee decided the meal was of good quality, and he (the Master) did not make any change. The action of the Master was confirmed, Mr. Morris ttating that he had found the meal wholesome, as, also, had the Medical Officer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>FORTHCOMING CONFERENCE. _</head>
        <p>FORTHCOMING CONFERENCE. _</p>
        <p>The Deimty-Clerk read a letter from the Secre- tary of the Central Poor Law Conference, giving notice that the conference would take place on February 9th and 10th next. The Right Hon. Herbert Samuel, M.P., President of the Local Government Board, was expected to give an address, and the subjects for discussion would include: "The Poor Law and Special War Relief," "Physical Defectives and the Poor Law," " The Local Government Board Circular on the Relief of Widows, Rze." Mr. Woodward gave notice that he would move the appointment of delegates at the next meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>THANKS FOR GOOD WISHES. _ _</head>
        <p>THANKS FOR GOOD WISHES. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. W. R. 1e Prez, of Stockton heath, wrote acknowledging receipt of a cop.f of a resolution passed by the Board at their last meeting, re- cording appreciation of his services as relieving officer, registrar of births and deaths, and vaccination officer in the Budworth district. He was grateful for the good wishes so kindly expressed for his future health. He cherished the kindliest feelings towards, and remember- ances of, all his late colleagues still in the service of the Board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>DECLINED WITH THANKS.</head>
        <p>DECLINED WITH THANKS.</p>
        <p>The Deputy-Clerk reail"a:let.t.e";K;;;;.the War Office, stating that whilst thanking the Guar- dians for their kind offer of accommodation for wounded soldiers they regretted that the General Officer Commanding-in-Chief of the Western Command was unable to utilise the accommodation the Guardians sb generously placed at their disposal. . _</p>
        <p>The. .Chairman said it was a good job they got that letter before they went on with the idea. He thought the reason for the refusal was that the accommodation was not sufficiently large, looking at the fact that they were so remote from—</p>
        <p>Mr. Mclntyre: Everywhere. (Laughter.) The Chairman: If the recommendation had been in Frodsham or Runcorn, it would have been accepted, but seeing that it is five or six miles from everywhere, it is too far for us to run a small hospital! (liear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>SLAUGHTER OF ROBINS.</head>
        <p>SLAUGHTER OF ROBINS.</p>
        <p>Virginia, North Carolina and Tennessee have the undesirable distinction of 4%eing the only States in the Union in, which the slaughter of robins is permitted by law, and recent investi- gations show that not less than 9,000,000 robins are killed by some 20,000 pot-hunters in these three States during the winter months. Patient investigation by ornithologists has proved that a robin in a year earns one dollar in the de- struction of insects injurious to crops. The pothunters sell them for five cents, a dozen. This wanton waste of millions of dollars. is something which should appeal to the hard common sense of every southern fanner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>ATTACKS CHRISTMAS TREES.</head>
        <p>ATTACKS CHRISTMAS TREES.</p>
        <p>Dr. R. S. McArthur, of New York, is opposed to Christmas trees on the ground that their use tends to the deforestation.</p>
        <p>lie says Christmas trees were adopted from the heathen any way. There will be no evergreens in his church this year.</p>
        <p>\</p>
