      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>FIELD OF</head>
        <p>FIELD OF</p>
        <p>BARROW GET THEIR REVENGE</p>
        <p>House Themselves in the Second Half and Beat Warrington 9-3.</p>
        <p>DEPRESSING PLAY IN DEPRESSING WEATHER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>Notes by "Centre."</head>
        <p>Notes by "Centre."</p>
        <p>Barrow had their revenge on Warrington on Saturday. The first trick of this season's rubber fell to the Wilderspool men, but the return game went against them, to the tune of 9 points to 1. The game was played away—on Barrow's bleak, exposed ground at Roose, over three miles out of the busy town. Considering that the ground is devoid of shelter and that the weather on Saturday afternoon was the reverse of ideal— wind, with persistent cold rain, being the order —it speaks well for the pride and interest that Barrovians take in their Northern Union repre- sentatives that a crowd of just over three thousand turned up. It cannot be said that theirs was the reward of witnessing a game that was brimful of excitement; indeed, truth to tell, the match—more especially in the first half—was -singularly barren of those displays so dear to the heart of the spectatorate; even if it could not conscientiously be classified amongst the desperately uninteresting. True, the 'players did buck up a bit in the second moiety, but by then the keen wind and the dismal wet- ness had taken away the appetites of the on- lookers and their enthusiasm was but luke- warm.</p>
        <p>was nearly always there when wanted, and twice he had to receive the attention of the men with the sponge and embrocation, but, fortunately, neither of his injuries was serious enough to necessitate his leaving the field.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>SCORELESS FIRST HALF.</head>
        <p>A SCORELESS FIRST HALF.</p>
        <p>The first half was as barren of score as it was of excitement, neither side being greatly daring or vastly enterprising. A few runs there were that aroused something approaching enthusi- asm, but as a whole the moiety was a long, and</p>
        <p>somewhat tedious, succession of backward and forward play, with the defenders on each side more resourceful than the attackers. When the teams crossed over the game was anybody's, as the phrase goes. Neither side had given evi- deuce of superiority over the other, and the blank score sheet just about adequately and accurately represented the run of play. Once the Barrovians had got fairly settled in their new territory, however, they displayed latent powers, the existence of which they had not given detectable signs in the Arst portion. They not only proceeded to monopolise the attacking, but the whole of their play livened up and they were more than six points better than their opponents, though, as is so often the case in this world, they did not get their deserts. The im- provement was Mainly noticeable in their van- guard, who combined better than in the first half and played with greatel• confidence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>WILDERSPOOL LADS' SOLITARY TRY.</head>
        <p>WILDERSPOOL LADS' SOLITARY TRY.</p>
        <p>Barrow were the first to score, and Warring- ton only managed their sOlitary.try in the last minute or two. Corns put up the opening points, when the second half was growing fairly old. In doing so he had the assistance of Doyle, who broke away on his wing, and after thread- ing his way through the opposing ranks, passed into the centre. Corns picked up and, adroitly eluding the several pairs of hands stretched out to grasp him, crossed over. The goal-kick was a failure; indeed all four. tries scored in the match went unconverted. Their success em- boldened the Barrow men and their attacks were persistent, but several mistakes prevented them from reaping the full harvest of their persistency. At length, however, came a second success. Mount received the ball when well up the field, and transferred it to Dobson. The latter cut off at top speed, and when em- barrassed by the too close attentions of the War-</p>
        <p>rington thi7eequarters, passed to ifillia-MS:v;h-o dashed over in swinging fashion. It was the neatest bit of work of the afternoon, and the bail was grassed just near the corner flag. Dob- son put on Barrow's third and last try, and barely had the echoes of the applause which greeted it died away, when Taberner put the imprimatur of success on a Warrington attack by crossing the line at the corner. Nothing else was score&amp; and the result therefore was : Bar- row, 3 tries (9 points); Warrington, 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S WEAKNESSES.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S WEAKNESSES.</p>
        <p>Warrington's defeat was due to the fact that their forwards did not show a second half im- provement corresponding with that exhibited by the opposing front rankers. Whilst the Bar- row men "bucked up" and rounded off the ragged edges in their play, the Warrington men continued to content themselves with work of unwonted mediocrity and slipshodness, good openings being allowed to go a-begging in exas- perating fashion, and with disheartening fre- quency. Had it not been that the visitors' de- fenders maintained their standard of reliability to. the bitter end, the adverse balance of points must have been a much larger one. Jolley's was the outstanding work done for Warrington. Plenty of work—and ticklish work. too—was found for him, but he kept his head, and more than once saved a tight situation by his level- headedness, allied to his robust kicking. He</p>
        <p>EMotor-cycles and Cars.ammow cceesorlee. eclairs by competent men. Petrol end tyres. W. KNIGHT 9 STOCKTON HEATH GARAGE, LONDON ROAD. Telephone 63 Stockton Meath.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>BARROW'S SUPERIOR FORWARDS.</head>
        <p>BARROW'S SUPERIOR FORWARDS.</p>
        <p>Brookes was the best of the Warrington three- quarters, and Tranter gave him skilled assist- ance, and plenty of it. Cox and Fairhurst were the. most conspicuous of the visiting forwards and ran strongly whenever one of the few chances of the game fell to their lot, but they were not supported as well as they might have been. Young and Dainteth scarcely took full advantage of the few opportunities afforded them, leaving it to the threequarters to make much of their ,own work. On the whole, War- rington gave a disappointing display and their defeat was not so much due to the fact that their opponents had improved, as that they them- selves had deteriorated. After the manner in which they accounted for Barrow a few short weeks since, it was pretty confidently antici- pated that they would bring home the points. But it was not to be! Much of the credit of Barrow's victory was due to their forward line, who, in the second half, at any rate, played with rare determination and understanding. Spencer, their full-back, was a positive tower of strength to his side, and the home half-backs were a resourceful and reliable pair, whilst the sextette in front, once they got in their stride, took a deal of holding and proved dangerously speedy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT 'LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT 'LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECS. V. PENDLETON.</p>
        <p>This match, in the first division of the above League, was played at Pendleton on Saturday, before a record/ attendance. Both sides were well represented. Forster kicked off for Pendle- ton and Brandwood returned. After exchange kicking, Ryder made a mark, from which Lambert kicked a splendid goal. Both sides were playing a strenuous game, and Povey, breaking through, seemed to have a clear field when he lost the ball. Warren got his backs going, but the defence held good. Crabtree. Ryder and Leah, combining well, took the 'ball down-field, where Horrocks successfully tackled Leah. Both sides indulged in spells of attack- ing, and when half-time arrived the score stood: Pendleton, 1 goal (2 points); Rylands', nil. _ _</p>
        <p>Brandwood commenced the second half, and Lambert returned into touch. The " Wire- men,".getting their backs going, made things warm, but the defence was sound. Warren had to be carried off the field with his shoulder put out. Jackson, feeding Crabtree, that player made good headway, but was well tackled. Pendleton scored at the corner, Lambert failing at goal. A passing, bout between Horrocks and the Brothers Povey ended in C. Povey being obstructed at the corner and Pendleton were Penalised. Rylands' pressed, but could not pene- trate the defence. Gallop, dropping at goal, hit the posts. The game ended in favour of Pendle- ton by 1 goal 1 try (5 points) to nil. Horrocks, Povey, Gallop and Warren played well for the " Wiremen."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Big Game.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. BATLEY. For the first time this season Warrington meet Batley to-day, journeying to the Yorkshire town in order to do so. The Warrington team was selected on Wed- nesday night as follows:— Jolley. Waywell, Brookes, Tranter, Bradshaw. Young, Dainteth. W. Cunliffe, T. Cunliffe, Cox, Mather, Green, Critchley. _ A FIRST APPEARANCE.</p>
        <p>It will be. nOit.sithr;i T. Cunliffe, brother of the popular forward who has figured in the Warrington ranks so frequently this season, is due to make his first appearance in the premier team, his trials with the Combination team having proved eminently satisfactory. Prior to signing on for Warrington lie played with the Pemberton R.P.C., a club with a consider- able reputation in the Wigan district. WAYWELL RE-APPEARS. Waywell makes his first appearance in the team since his accident in the match with Leigh, early on in the season. CLARE "STOOD DOWN." Clare will be absent from the forward line in Saturday's match. Lately he has not been show-</p>
      </div>
