        <p>In Victoria Park; Paddington Reserve: T. Knight (captain); J. Dwerryhouse and E. Vickers; J. Goodfellow, J. Griffiths and B. Chadwick; J. Wright, S. Summers, T. Woodhead, J. Adcroft and J. Red- ford. Reserves: J. Warburton and Connor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>GREAT_SANKEY v. FERNDALE.</head>
        <p>GREAT_SANKEY v. FERNDALE.</p>
        <p>------- At the Penketh Recreation Ground, in the Whines and District Alliance; kick-oh 2.30 :</p>
        <p>, Great Sankey: H. Brindle; E. Glover and B. J. Kelly; J. E. Glover, H. Ditehfield and H. Payne; T. Roberts, T. Whitfield, T. Hawthorn, S. Poulter and A. Houghton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>PRUDENTIAL. Great Company's Help in the %Var.</head>
        <p>THE PRUDENTIAL. Great Company's Help in the %Var.</p>
        <p>In "The Examiner" Almanac, issued last week, it would be noticed that the funds of the Prudential Assurance Co., Ltd., show -a marked increase again this year. It may interest our readers to know that to date this remarkable company has paid over four thousand claims in connection with the war, amounting to the large sum of .00,000. . _O.</p>
        <p>We believe that this company is assisting in more ways than one in the war, as it has, in addition to fully four hundred members of the Chief Office staff serving in both branches of His Majesty's Forces, a number of the lady clerks serving with the Red Cross Society. One of the company's directors • has provided a special motor car for this work, and up to date 137 calls have been responded to and the num- ber of wounded transported to hospitals has been over 8,000.</p>
        <p>It is worthy of note that the company has had for some time a rifle range situated in the basement of their palatial offices in Holborn, which has no doubt played a great part in en- abling those members of the staff who have answered their country's call to give such a good account of themselves. Four members of the club have qualified as instructors in the new Army, and one has been appointed as instructor of instructors. This range has now been placed' at the disposal of the 12th Battalion County of London Rifles (known as the Rangers), and is used daily.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <p>CLARKE'S B 41 PILLS can be reiied upon to cure in either sex all acquired or constitutional dis- charges from the Urinary Organs, Gravel and Pains in the back. Free from Mercury. Established up- wards of 50 years. In boxes 4s. 6d. each, of all Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendors throughout the world, or sent for sixty stamps by the makers— The Lincoln and Midland Counties Drug Co., Lin- coln.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>Stockton Heath Put Up Ten Goals Against Partington.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath Put Up Ten Goals Against Partington.</p>
        <p>MORE UNPLAYED MATCHES.</p>
        <p>One of the most important fixtures in last week's list was the meeting of the Earlestown Mission and Longford, and, from the commence- ment of the game there appeared to be too much confidence of what the result would b—e amongst the United players, but they were dooineii to disappointment, as on the day's play the dis- play of the United was very much inferior to that of the Mission team. The latter combined well, and had the ball under far better control than their opponents, and in the end they out- shone them in all points of the game. The first goal scored against them, a splendid shot from A. Eaton, disheartened the visitors, and they at once resorted to the losing policy of changing the position of their players. The result was that their combination was put out of gear, and the players resorted to individualism, so that their attack never had much sting in it. At the opposite end Massey on several occasions did smart work between the posts, and could not be 'blamed for the shots which went past him. The latter portion of the game was played in semi-darkness, and the game had to be stopped five minutes before the expiring of the time the captains agreed to play.</p>
        <p>The return fixture between St. Barnabas' and the Newton Cons. was an evenly contested game, which resulted in a goalless draw. Both teams missed opportunities of scoring, but the condi- tion of the Lovely-lane ground after heavy rain makes chances, which appear very 'easy to the spectators, difficult to the players.</p>
        <p>The fixture between the White Star and Fair- field was cancelled by the latter owing to some grievances which have arisen between the secre- tary and players, and a meeting has been held to consider the positiov of the club. It was decided to disband for the present season, owing to the inability to raise a satisfactory team. A good number of members have joined the Army. The matter will be brought up at the next meet- ing of the League, when an effort will be made for the club to complete their fixtures.</p>
        <p>The fixture between the Fairfield United and the Elmwood Cons, was, as expected, a very keen and vigorously contested game. The United gained a verdict by the odd goal. The smallness of the score came as a surprise to out- siders, but those present were unanimous in their opinions that on the day's play the better team had lost. The Cons. are to be congratu- lated for the display they gave, especially dur- ing the second portion, when they were minus the services of Finch, who had during the first half played a sterling game. It was unfortun- ate that be fell and twisted his knee. The accident will probably keep him out of the team for several weeks.</p>
        <p>Hollins Green made no mistake with their encounter with the Recs.., scoring five times, to which the visitors were unable to respond.</p>
        <p>Paddington team and supporters were ao-omed to disappointment in. their fixture with the Earlestown Y.M.C.A. The Secretary of the Paddington Club received a card from the secre- tary of the latter informing him on the Friday of the latter club's intention of travelling, but they failed to put in an appearance, nor even sent an apology in any form. This certainly is a very had sportsmanlike spirit on the part of the Y.M.C.A. players.</p>
        <p>To-day's fixtures will provide two specially keen encounters. namely. Paddington V . Fair- field United and Elmwood Cons. v. Latchford Baptists.</p>
        <p>The Padgate team that will to-day do battle against the Illmwood-avenue Congregationalists will be slightly different from that which triumphed over• Emmanuel Institute on Satur- day, the United having been fortunate enough to secure the services of one or two new players.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT • LEAGUE. FAIFfFIELD UNITED v. ELMWOOD CONS. On the Orford-road ground, before a good number of spectators. Fairfield: Potter; Cox and Bebbington ; Gleave, Lewis and Woodhouse; Mellor, War- burton, Millward, Hunter and S. Woodhouse. Elmwood: Holt; Jos. Phoenix and Jas. Phoe- nix; Pritchard, Finch and E. B. Mellor; W. Phoenix, Smith, Kendrick, Wells, and Jas. Mel- lor.</p>
        <p>The referee was Mr. W. Dernett. A visit was at once paia to t',e, visitors' goal, but the defence was alert, at,,, the leather was returned to midfield. Finch 1+ as playing splen- didly for the Cons., and keeping the home for- wards in check. At length Kendrick set the for- wards going with a pass out to the right, and a good long shot being sent in, Potter advanced to meet the ball, but he was beaten, and, fortun- ately for the United, one of their backs was in the goal and managed to clear. The United now took up the attack and a favourable open- ing was spoiled by a misunderstanding between Hunter and Millward. The home team con- tinued to attack and gained three successive corner kicks, but these were got away. Holt cleared very smartly on several occasions. Shortly before the interval the Cons. were un- fortunate to lose the services of Finch, who fell with his leg under him and twisted his knee. Interval: No score. On resuming, Millward quickly' made tracks for the Cons.' goal, but his final effort was directed wide. The Cons., now being minus a player, adopted the one-back policy, and the United forwards were repeatedly pulled up for off-side. The visitors made a raid on the home goal, and Bebbington had to concede a corner, being hard pressed by Kendrick. The ball was taken along by Hunter and S. Woodhouse, and " for some time the Cons. had to act on the defen- sive, but they played pluckily and managed to keep their lines intact. Then the Elmwood for- wards began to shine, and Potter was called upon several times. After this • attack was cleared the United were successful in ottaining the only goal of the match in a very lucky fashion. S. Woodhouse put in a good shot, which Holt caught, but in trying to kick the ball clear, it rebounded off one of the backs and through the posts. Kendrick came near to equalising with a shot only inches over the bar. Result: Fairfield United, 1 goal; Elmwood Cons., nil.</p>
        <p>HOLLINS GREEN v. BANK QUAY RECS. On the ground of the former, the following players taking part:— Hollins Green: Ellam; Clark and Billington ; Buckley, Mellor and Cleworth; Wright, Ather- ton, Martin, Dean and Atkinson. Recs.: Monks; Berry and Rawlinson; Ray, Smith and Cheaters; Deakin, Long, Eccleston, Webb and Davies. Referee, Mr. 0. Knight.</p>
        <p>The visitors started with only nine players, and the homesters were quickly attacking. Atkinson putting in a grand centre from the extreme left, Billington had not much difficulty in scoring the first goal. After a spell of even play, Billington scored the second goal with a splendid shot. The visitors very rarely got into the- home half, and before the interval Billing- ton again scored, thus registering the first three goals for his side. In the second half the visitors made their team complete and gave a much better display, but their attacks were met by a stubborn de- fence. Dean was very smart in accepting a pass from the, left, and scored the fourth goal, and directly after Atherton added a fifth. From now the visitors' goal was under a con-</p>
        <p>Notes by " Linesman"</p>
        <p>tinuous attack, but no further goals were added. Result: Hollins Green, 5 goals; Bank Quay Recs., nil.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN ST. JOHN'S MISSION v.</p>
        <p>LONGFORD UNITED.</p>
        <p>On the Mission ground, before a small number of spectators.- The following players turned out under the control of Mr. J. Smith : Mission: Massey; Winders and Aldridge; F. Walker, F. Eaton and Theobald; Wallas, Jones, A. Eaton, Sherlock and Plumpton. Longford: Sturgess; Holden and Mason; Stevens, Smart and Fearnley; Skidmore, Sher- i ratt, Bennett, Hands and Hughes. ' The opening exchanges did not suggest much keenness. After the visitors had visited the home goal area, where Winders made a good return, the homesters warmed to their w.ork, and made a strong attack upon the visitors' citadel. Sturgess and his backs cleared their lines, but the homesters returned more vigor- ously, and Sturgess was again called upon, and A. Eaton, securing the ball, sent in a magnifi- cent shot from twenty yards' range, which beat Sturgess all the. way. Not relishing this re- verse, the visitors played a strenuous game, but were outmanceuvred, and Sherlock scored a second goal. The homesters continued in their aggressive manner, and the same player shortly after registered their third goal. Following these reverses, the visitors made several changes in the positions of their players, and for a time held their own and made several raids into. the home quarters, but they found Winders and Aldridge equal to the occasion, and were sent back. In another attack on the visitors' goal, Sturgess saved splendidly when his backs were beaten. Stevens passed to Ben- nett, and the latter sent a long forward pass towards the home goal. Hughes, racing in, missed scoring when he had only the goalkeeper to beat. At the interval the score was three goals•to none in favour of the Mission. The second half opened in spirited fashion, and both teams were playing a very keen game. The Mission Lads, by their finer tacticsl_gradu- ally got in the visitors' half, an Inson made several good clearances, but the visiting for- wards did not show that mlthod of combina- tion which was seen amongst the Mission players, and the visitors' half-back line did not give the assistance to their forwards thot is necessary to bring about good results. Stevens, in particular, was the chief offender in wander- ing from his place, and leaving the home left wing with many openings, with the result that Jones was successful in registering a couple of goals in quick succession. The visitors made many individual attempts to break through 17:ie borne defence, but failed at the critical moment. There were chances sufficient for the visitors to have equalised, had their attack been more stea,dy in front of goat Skidmore and Hughes missed good openings, and Stevens missed scor- ing from a penalty kick. The game was stopped five minutes from time owing to darkness, when the result was: Mission, 5 goals; Longford, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>ST. BARNABAS' v. NEWTON CONS.</head>
        <p>ST. BARNABAS' v. NEWTON CONS.</p>
        <p>On the Lovely-lane ground. Teams St. Barnabas' : Davenport; Jefferson and Long; O'Neil, Muckley and Challinor; Sher- rington, Darlington, Barratt, Vernon and Ray. Newton Cons.: Dagnall; Lowrance and Tithers; Holeroft, Porter an Lennox; Clarke, Dodd, Jones, Glover and Aitken. _ _</p>
        <p>Referee, Mr. S. C. Essenhigh. The visitors began to have the best of file ex- changes, and the home defence was severely tried. Off-side against the visitors enabled them to clear their lines, and a similar decision was given against Darlington. Then the game was contested very evenly, and the defence on both sides proved equal to the attack. Both sides gained corner kicks, but failed to improve upon them, and the interval arrived with the score sheet blank.</p>
        <p>The visitors were the first to become aggres- sive in the second half, but Jefferson and Long defended stoutly, and on one occasion Daven- port saved very smartly. Good play by the home half-back line enabled their forwards to attack, but their marksmanship was bad. The home goal was attacked very strongly, Daven- port doing very well between the posts. Aitken, with a fine effort, Ica the cross-bar, the ball going dead. From the goal kick play was gradu- ally taken into the visitors' area, and good op- portunities for scoring were missed by the home forwards, and at the opposite end the visitors' front line made the same mistakes, but these to a great extent could be excused, owing to the greasy state of the ball, and the conditions under which the game had to be contested. Result: St. Barnabas', nil; Newton Cons., nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>ORFORD WHITE STAR v. FAIRFIELD</head>
        <p>ORFORD WHITE STAR v. FAIRFIELD</p>
        <p>ATHLETIC.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the fixture between the above clubs Was cancelled, owing to the latter being unable to raise a team.</p>
        <p>PADDINGTON v. EARLESTOWN This match between the above clubs should have been played on the ground of the former club, but for some reason or other the latter team &amp;He,' - put in an appearance, and there was grr ppointment amongst the Pad- dingt ' (I supporters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>tIIRE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>tIIRE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>.econd Division.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH v. PARTINGTON.</p>
        <p>The above teams met on the West-avenue ground in their return fixture. The visitors won the toss, and put the homesters to defend the Farm goal. The visitors were at a disad- vantage in only being able to play with nine players. For the first twenty minutes they managed to hold their own, but following this period of even play, the homesters set up a con- tinual attack on the visitors' goal, Noden scor- ing the first goal, this being followed by a couple from Vernon. Smith scored a fourth and Noden a fifth. Half-time: Stockton Heath, 5 goals; Partington, nil. The game was all in favour of the Heathens in the second half, but the visitors under the circumstances def Aided well. They rarely got over the half-way line, Hooper, the home cus- todian, not being called upon in this portion. Goals Were added by Lloyd, Pearse and Roden, and at the call of time the result was: Stockton Heath, 10 goals; Partington, none.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.v..WALTON. On the ground of the former. The home team, with the wind, at once made an attack on the visitors' goal, but were met by a stubborn defence. They kept up a continual attack and the visiting defence conceded numerous corner kicks, but none were improved upon, the home forwards failing to keep control of the ball when in front of goal. It was only on rare occasions that the visitors' forwards got into the home goal area, but it was from one of these raids that A. Jones beat the opposing backs and scored for the visiting side. The second portion of the game was nearly a repeti- tion cf the first half. The visitors lirid most of the play, but failed to add to their score, and on the occasions when the homesters broke away Shakeshaft and Ford played too close a game, and were repulsed by the visitors' half- backs. Result: Walton, 1 goal;;Grappenhall, nil.</p>
      </div>
