      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 19, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 19, 1914.</p>
        <p>s .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS. TERRITORIAL EQUIPMENT FUND.</head>
        <p>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS. TERRITORIAL EQUIPMENT FUND.</p>
        <p>Gratifying Results.</p>
        <p>There has been a gratifying response to the appeal for the Territorial Special Equipment Fund, as will be seen by an advertisement in to-day's "Examiner." Altogether ..t715 9s. 10d. has been subscribed, and there is a balance in hand Of £95 138. 10d. It has been specially gratifying to notice the generous way in which money, material, assistance in knitting and making comforts for the men of both Batta- lions, now 2,100 in number, have been supplied. Over 7,000 articles—shirts, helmets, socks, body belts, mittens, &amp;c.—have been dispatched, and a comparatively large stock is still in hand. Thanks are due, and are given, to all who have rendered assistance, especially the wives and relatives of the non-commissioned officers and men of the regiment. The children of Warring- ton, Stockton Heath ad Latchford have been most industrious, and the teachers are to be congratulated on the splendid result of the chil- dren's work. The Warrington Women's Liberal Association and the Stockton Heath Women's Liberal Association have been most generous. As a Christmas remembrance a special roox of cigarettes for each member of the Battalion has been sent to headquarters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>" WIDE MARGIN FOR COMFORTS." \likli‘Varrington Women Liberals</head>
        <p>" WIDE MARGIN FOR COMFORTS." \likli‘Varrington Women Liberals</p>
        <p>Help to Fill.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Women's Liberal Association, at the sewing meetings which it holds on Tues- day and Thursday afternoons, is making splen- did progress with the garments which are being made for the soldiers, and contributions to the fund have this week been received, with great appreciation, from Messrs. Payne, Tilling and F. Webster; Mesdames Armitage, Bayley, j. Bayley, P. H. Brown, Carter, Farrar, Gough, Green, Henshall, llewitt, Riggings, Hough, Israel, Jolley, R. Jones, Lingard, Lowe, Mason, Massey, Newall, Pacey, A. Rathbone, Roberts, Summerfield, Wallington, Weston, T. W. Williams, Wilkinson, Wilkinson, Wright, and Wright; the Misses Wainwright, and M. Yates. Since our last lit was published, subscrip- tions have been received from: Mr. A. H. Cros- field, Baby O'Brien, Mesdames John Crosfield, rough, Mynekyme, Booth, J. E. Balmford, Liston, Thew, F. Pendlebury, Z. Armitage, G. s. Pendlebury, Sanders and Collin (whist drives, &amp;c.), Sheath (doll draw), B. Farrar, Tanner, Lindley (Town Hall), Hamblett (17, Egerton-street), Lindley and Locker (whist drive), Massey (Marsh-street), and Whitefield (draw for cosy); proceeds of teas per Mesdames Brown, Tanner, Hawthorn, Collin, Hamson, Osborne, Young, Cowdell, Barton, Mason, Sanders, Delaney, Davies, Hough, and J. Wil- kinson, and the Misses Short and Womersley; St. Austin's per Miss Booth, Whitecross per Mrs. Percival, and Latchford per Mrs. T. Locker; Councillor Cunnington, Miss Helen Hawthorn, Messrs. Mynekyme, Williams (Rol- leston-street), West, Johnson (chrysanthe- mums), and H. Gorst. THE MOTOR AMBULANCE. With reference to the draw for a doll recently held in aid of a fund to provide a motor ambu- lance for the front, Mrs. Osborne has received the following letter from Lady Haworth (pre- sident of the Lancashire and Cheshire Union of Women's Liberal Associations), in acknow- ledgment of a substantial contribution from the Warrington women : "Dear Mrs. Osborne,—Your association will be pleased to hear that its kind donation has brought the Motor Ambulance Fund to the very satisfactory amount of £6OO. £4OO of this sum will pay for the ambulance, and the rest will go towards the running expenses. Millicent, Duchess of Sutherland, writes:— Can You express to all who have given large or small sums my profound gratitude. If they could only realise what such self-denying help means in this most frightful war! The work gets heavier and heavier; but, thank God, one is helped by so much sympathy and generosity to save lives. I have secured a "Fiat" Motor Ambulance I have long set my heart on, and I will send a photograph soon, as it must be painted first: "Gift of the Lancashire and Cheshire Women's Associations."' Copies of this photograph will be sold at a small cost to any association desiring it. and the proceeds will go to the fund.—yours sincerely, LILY HAWORTH."</p>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL DRAWS. Other draws, which have also been held, have proved most successful. Mr. James Bate very kindly presented a barrel of apples to be drawn for. As a result of this, ten guineas have been added to the sewing class funds. The winning number is 2,335. A gift of chocolate and china, from Mrs. Savage, produced over £1 for the same fund by the same means; as did also a cosy given by Mrs. Whitfield, of Chester-road. Further supplies, to be drawn for in aid of the sewing funds, have been given this week by various friends. These will be disposed of after the Christmas holidays. Meanwhile, a large consignment of parcels, containing useful garments and comforts for soldiers in all parts of the country, was dis- patched two dao,s ago. The class will now take a holiday until January 4th, 1915—although the ladies are by no means eager for any such respite from their labour of love!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>HELP FROM THE MEN.</head>
        <p>HELP FROM THE MEN.</p>
        <p>Seeing the splendid service that the women were doing, in the provision of useful garments, the men of the Warrington Liberal Association decided to assist. They recognised that the supplies of material had required a considerable outlay, which had been raised in a more or less private manner. This source of supply was inadequate, and the Senior Asso- ciation felt that to enable this valuable work to be continued, and to encourage and render effective this generous service, adequate funds for the purchase of material should be pro- vided. " It ought to be recognised," they said, "that, at the front and in the training camps, the conditions of life entail great privations upon those who are risking their life for the country's safety; and that the regulation Army provision leaves a wide margin for comforts; an(l, further, that the wear and waste in cloth- ing (where washing and repairing are almost impossible) is very great, making renewals necessary as long as the war lasts.</p>
        <p>I both</p>
        <p>THE DESERVING APPEAL. "All this involves upon those at home, who are not sharing the dangers, the imperative duty of providing for those at the front." The Liberal Association has determined to raise an additional fund of at least £3OO, with which to enable the commendable work to be con- tinued. Towards this amount, a sum nearing £lOO has already been promised. It is trusted that all will do something in helping forward this effort. Any sum—large or small—will be gratefully received by Councillor JANiss T. Locsna, at the Liberal Club, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>£2O FOR WOUNDED AVARRIORS.</head>
        <p>£2O FOR WOUNDED AVARRIORS.</p>
        <p>At the Kendrick-street School, Warrington, on Thursday evening, was held a most success- ful whist drive, promoted for the benefit of the wounded warriors in the Infirmary, by Mes- dames Dumbell, Flood and Powis. About 130 attended, the valuable prizes being gained by the Misses Reid and C. Rigby, Messrs. Crossley and Collins, and Mrs. Jeffreys. A draw for large cakes was also held, the prizes in this case being carried off by: 479, Stanley and John Abbot; 284, J. B. Wilkinson; and 11, E. Long- thaw.</p>
        <p>Accompanied by Mr. J. H. Smethurst, Miss Hammond, the matron of the Infirmary, visited the school during the evening and drew the winning tickets. Afterwards, the Rev. E. Downham, curate-in-charge of St. Paul's Church, proposed a vote of thanks to Miss Ham- mond and Mr. Smethurst for their visit, say- ing that the same patriotic spirit animated both of them in their valuable labours. Mr. W. T. Flood seconded, and the motion was carried .with acclamation. In replying, Mr. Smethurst paid a glowing tribute to the work of Miss Hammond, and also said that the soldiers tin the Infirmary had the true British spirit—they I wanted to go back to the front as soon as pos- sible. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Before the assenibly dispersed, Mr. Flood, on behalf of the promoters of the function, thanked</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <p>For Best Value</p>
        <p>For Great and Pleasing Variety FOR MODERATION IN PRICE OF</p>
        <p>CARDS, CALENDARS, FANCY LEATHER GOODS, BOOKS,</p>
        <p>ELECTRO - PLATE, STERLING SILVER NOVELTIES, JEWELLERY, PERFUMERY, FURS, LACE, GLOVES, HANDKERCHIEFS, NECKWEAR, UMBRELLAS, and the lhousand and One Things</p>
        <p>Suitable for XMAS GIVES</p>
        <p>VISIT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>Compton House,</head>
        <p>Compton House,</p>
        <p>W. 11. WATTS &amp; CO. LTD.,</p>
        <p>CHURCH-STREET,</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>I I</p>
        <p>than 458,900 persons have witnessed this spark- ling play, whilst nearly another half-million have seen it in the provinces. It is a treat that no lover of musical plays should miss. Royal visits to "The Marriage Market" have been frequent since the first night, and as a genuine marriage market the play has also a record, several of the original principals having met their "fate" in the cast, including Mr. Robert Michaelis, who married Miss Phyllys Le Grand, Mr. Prank Perfitt who ,married Miss Gladys Lancaster, and Miss Beryl Norman who mar- ried Mr. R. 3lnguier.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>MUCH-TALKED-OF PLAYLET AT THE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>MUCH-TALKED-OF PLAYLET AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The domestic morality playlet, "Should a Woman Tell?" by the Rev. A. J. Waldron, vicar of Brixton, which was the sensation of a Lon- don season, and over which there has been so much controversy in the English and American Press, is attracting exceptionally large audi- ences to the Hippodrome this week, where it has been "booked" at great cost. The Vicar has handled a delicate theme in masterly fashion, skating safely over thin ice; and his playlet is by way of being a slashing attack on the narrow-minded, selfish individual, who demands fronr woman that standard of ethics which lie refuses to concede himself. Mr. E. Story-Gofton impersonates the Vicar in this virile problem-drama, and does so in most tell- ing fashion, infusing into his delineation just the right spirit of dignified restraint. Miss Mabel Burrell makes a compelling appeal to the sympathies of the audience as the woman "with a past," and Mr. Charles V. Sandford is well placed as the man of the world, who is the cause of all the pother, and, in the end, makes reparation to the woman he has wronged. Mr. Arthur Bawtree appears as the Convert and plays i the part with consummate skill. The other turns which go to make up the programme are all very good. The Nicol rothers delight the audience with a novel trick-cycling turn. Mdlle. Millie Dagmar has trained her dogs and pony to a wonderful degree of perfection, and the animals perform all manner of tricks in a most sagacious manner. Ellis Barrieton scores a success in patriotic songs, and Johnny Dwyer is a popular comedian who never fails to please with his parodies and other laughablo numbers. Carl Miller gives an excellent display of long- boot dancing, and Morris and Moore are past masters in the art of step-dancing. Rene Grahame is a charming comedienne and male impersonator, whose two songs convulse the audience with mirth. _</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the author of "Should a Woman Tell?" attended and explained to crowded audiences hie reasons for writing his "problem" play.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>ATTRACTIVE YULETIDE FARE.</head>
        <p>ATTRACTIVE YULETIDE FARE.</p>
        <p>Albert Wyndham and Co., in a romantic play- let founded upon an incident in the life of Nell Gwynne, the big-hearted actress-courtesan, will fdrm the "star" attraction next week, and ori.er turns will be provided by Edna Lyall, the York- shire nightingale; Yettmah, in a feast of magic; Bertie Adams, in selections from her repertoire; Marie Masque, the celebrated soprano; Vic and Vie, vocalists and dancers; and Day and Deen, comedians and dancers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>PICTUREDROME.</head>
        <p>PICTUREDROME.</p>
        <p>Included in the first of the two excellent pro- grammes presented during the week at this popular place of amusement were the dramas: " The Seven Sealed Orders," " The Harbour of Love," and "Private Denis Hogan"; the topical, "Gaumont Graphic"; and the excru- ciatingly funny comedies, "Mabel's Married Life," "Buster and His Goat," and "Sammy as an Automaton." The second programme is now showing, and includes the following "star" One Who Loves Him Best," "The Gold Thief," and "Woes of a Widow"; "Mabel's Busy Day," "The Plumber's Picnic," and "A Vege- tarian Dream" (comedies); and the "Gaumont Graphic." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>PICTURE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>PICTURE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>As usual, this week's capital bills of fare at the Picture 1101150 have left nothing to be de- sired. The first included two fine, pathetic dramas, "When a Woman's Forty" and "Poppies"; the comedies, "The Train of In- cidents" and "The Champion Who Was Delayed "; and two instructive and interesting war-films, "Indian Troops in England" and the " Gaumont Graphic." The week-end programme, which will be shown for, the last time to-night, is quite equal to the first. The dramas are: "Motor Buc- caneers," "An Old Rag Doll," and "-The New Reporter"; and the comedies, "The Suspended Ceremony," and "Love v. Electricity"; whilst in addition there is the " Gaumont Graphic," as usual.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>At this popular amusement resort highly entertaining programmes have been shown dur- ing the week, and those who visit it to-night— when the second programme concludes—will not be disappointed. The bill of fare for the first three days in- eluded: "The Babes in the Wood," a beauti- fully coloured film, giving an old story in quite an original setting. " The Cruel Crown" in- troduced some new characters into the thrilling serial, "Adventures of Kathlyn." "The Dead Past Recalled" proved one of the best war- dramas yet filmed; and "Pathe's Animated Gazette" gave all the latest war news pictures. "The Adventures of Miss Tomboy "—a very amusing comedy—was a particularly good pro- duction. "A Million Bid" is the " star " film in the splendid programme to be shown for the last time this evening.</p>
        <p>Next Week. On Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday, "Tragedy and Comedy" and "Three Men and a Woman" will be the principal films, and for the remainder of the week. "Little Lord Faun- tleroy " will be the premier picture.</p>
        <p>• Climatic Conditions. The weather during the week ending Decem- ber 12th was rather better than in the pre- ceding seven days, the mean temperature being six degrees higher. The warmest day during the period under review was the 6th inst., when the glass recorded a temperature of 52 degrees. Three days later the lowest temperature--29 degrees—was recorded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>_THE POLITICAL TRUCE. Why Sir Gilbert Greenall Wants the War to End.</head>
        <p>_THE POLITICAL TRUCE. Why Sir Gilbert Greenall Wants the War to End.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Con- servative Club was held on Wednesday evening, when Sir Gilbert Greenall presided over a large attendance. MEMBERSHIP WELL MAINTAINED.</p>
        <p>Mr. If. Collin (secretary) presented his re- port, and said that the club membership was well maintained, new members having enrolled each month for both rooms. The bowling and billiard season had been exceptionally good, and, in view of the Billiard League having abandoned all games on account of the war, the club would hold the trophies they had won for another year. The Preparatory Training Corps had met with success. Several members and sons of members had joined the Forces, and the Club Committee sincerely hoped they would return home safely. A large sum of money had been raised for the tobacco fund, and the first parcel would be sent to the South Lancashire Regiment in a few (lays. The club congratu- lated the Member on his appointment on the Press Bureau and also on being given a lieuten- ancy in the Navy. (Hear, hear.) The report was adopted, on the proposition of Mr: J. L. Davenport, second by Mr. T. Cooper..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>FINANCIAL.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL.</p>
        <p>..... ..-...-. Mr. W. IL Bellian (treasurer) said the finances of the club were in a flourishing condi- tion, and they were in 'a better position than they had been for years. He thanked Mr. B. H. Daintith for the able manner in which he had kept the books, and Mr. and Mrs. Holland, the steward and stewardess, for the excellent ser- vices they had rendered.</p>
        <p>The report was adopted, on the propoition of Mr. 11. B. Turner, seconded by Mr. C. H. Wells. _ _ _</p>
        <p>OFFICERS RE-ELECTED. Messrs. Bellian and Daintith were re-ap- pointed representatives on the Conservative Council. Mr. Collin and Mr. Bellian were re- elected secretary and treasurer, respectively. In reply Mr. Collin mentioned that he had been secretary for 21 years, with only one break in between.</p>
        <p>The committee were re-elected, and it was de- cided that whilst Mr. R. F. Anderton is serving with the colours, Mr. H. Whittle shall deputise for him.</p>
        <p>"OUR GOOD FRIENDS, THE LIBERALS." Councillor W. 11. J. Jenkins proposed a vote of thanks to Sir Gilbert for presiding. In seconding, Mr. G. H. Smith said that Sir Gilbert was the active head in political war- fare, but all that had been dropped now, and 1 they had united in helping "our good friends, , the Liberals," in an endeavour to bring the war to a successful issue. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>COUNTRY BEFORE PARTY.</head>
        <p>COUNTRY BEFORE PARTY.</p>
        <p>In reply, Sir Gilbert said that at the present time political warfare was put on one side. They were all thinking of the good of the coun- try and not of any particular party. (Hear, hear.) He hoped that the war would cease before long, so that they would be able to en- gage in political warfare once again, which was far better than the warfare which was going on at the present time. (Laughter and ap- plause.) • A hearty vote of thanks was also accorded Mr. J. Fulton-Smith for his excellent services to the club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>WAR ITEMS.</head>
        <p>WAR ITEMS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AMBULANCE MAN FOR THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>Corporal Warrener (Earlestown), of the War- rington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Bri- gade, has gone on active service with the field ambulance attached to the Royal Naval Brigade.</p>
        <p>FOUR FIGHTING BROTHERS. Four sons of Mr. James Wood, of Warring- ton, are serving in the Army, viz., Alfred, in the King's Royal Rifles; Frank, in the 6th In- niskilling Dragoons; Albert, in the Bth Hussars; and William, in the Territorials.</p>
        <p>THREE BROTHERS ENLIST TOGETHER. Three brothers—William Savage (32), 19, Hale-street; David Savage (27), 1, Booth-street; and Daniel Savage (22), 9, Hesketh-street—en- listed together at the Drill Hall on Tuesday. They are all married, and they are the sons of the late Sergt. Savage, who saw service with the old 3rd Militia. The brothers have joined the 4th (Territorial) Reserve Battalion of the South Lancashires.</p>
        <p>•REST BEFORE MORE WORK!</p>
        <p>..._.._ ..-..... On Friday of last week five of the British wounded soldiers who have been under treat- ment at the Raddon Court Red Cross Hospital, and have made satisfactory recoveries, left War- rington to spend short holidays at their homes before returning to the Front. On Satur lay four convalescent Belgian warriors also left the hospital, their destination not being disclosed.</p>
        <p>- ----- HIT BY THE WAR! A traveller on commission, summoned at the County Police Court in Warrington, on Wednes- day, for non-payment of poor rates, pleaded that the war had badly affected his returns; in fact, he " was down" 30s. a week.—The usual order was \made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>PREPARATORY TRAINING CORPS.</head>
        <p>PREPARATORY TRAINING CORPS.</p>
        <p>Fairfield and Howley Company.</p>
        <p>The members of this company met at the L. and N.-W. Railway Company's Rifle Range on Wednesday evening. The best shooting was done by Messrs. D. Plinston, A. Bailie, H. Gaunt, S. Oddy, J. Langhorn and T. Healer, in the order given. No. 2 Section was well repre- sented by, Messrs. Plinston, Langhorn, Healer, T. H. Newton and W. O'Neill. No. 1 Section had the best file in Messrs. Gaunt and Oddy. Company drills are held at the Hardy-street works on Mondays and Wednesdays at 8 p.m. There will be no drills between 23rd December and 4th January. Musketry practice will take place as announced on parades.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>To-n AY.</head>
        <p>To-n AY.</p>
        <p>Meet at Drill Hall, 2.50 p.m., for Battalion parade.</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>Vp orth Monthli ,ay 7/0 15/- 20/-</head>
        <p>Vp orth Monthli ,ay 7/0 15/- 20/-</p>
        <p>GUARDIINS AND WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>No Room for Soldiers in the Workhouse.</p>
        <p>OLD MEMBER'S FAILING MULTI(</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse Os Wednesday, Mr. A. J. Jolley presiding.</p>
        <p>NO BEDS FOR " TOMMIES." It was reported that at a meeting of the Special Committee appointed to consider the question of providing accommodation for wounded soldiers, a letter was read from Dr' Hodgkinson, stating that at the most there were only 15 beds vacant at once, and on analysis he found that those in the special and larger wards were seldom full, but if one bed in each of Buell wards was occupied, the ward was of no use for other cases. The general wards were alwaYB full, and often beds had to be placed in the cor- ridor. Even if the children could be placed ill some other part of the house it would be un- satisfactory. The children's ward was not suit- able for soldiers, because at each end there were isolation wards. He did not think it pos- sible to admit any military patients, unless the. Workhouse patients were to suffer from lack et accommodation. When the new mental wards were ready, the question might be reconsidered. and even then the mental wards would be ‘'eri overcrowded. It was resolved that the Local Government Board be informed that the Guardians had fl. reserve space for purposes other than their own' but that they had a building nearing completion. which would accommodate 150 patients. and that they were prepared to offer it to the GO' ernment for the accommodation and treatment of military patients.</p>
        <p>PLAY-SHED TO BE .11E-FLOORED. , It was reported that at a meeting of the Pas' gate Homes Committee the Superintendent 04; : authorised to proceed with the re-flooring e„ the shed in the boys' playground, on the line: suggested by Mr. Brooks. A proposition Os; the work be carried out by the staff and at •'' the materials be obtained from the Workbolls''..• was defeated. The minutes were confirmed. • IF THE GUARDIANS THINK FIT A letter was read from the Local Governmen' Board stating that it was within the discretit 'of Guardians to allow beer at Christmas tiras they thought fit. DESIROUS OF RETIRING. The Rev. E. W. Kay, writing on behalf of tli! Rev. T. R. Matthews, stated that Mr. Mattlievr4 was desirous of retiring when his term of 06e1c1 as a member of the Board expired, as he 0,11,. not attend the meetings owing to failing hes' Ao It was regrettable that he should have to that after serving on the Board for 20 Ye4r:i He thanked the Board for their expressions sympathy and good wishes, which he reciPro, ' cated. LADY GUARDIAN'S LONG ABSENCE. t, Mr. Brooks gave notice that at the next 10eie0 ing be would move "that the office of Guard. t. held by Mrs. Agnes Simpson be declared vacanA as she had not attended for six months, 11; such absence had not been accounted for tile illness or any other reason approved by Board." Regret was expressed that such a CO should be be adopted, in view of the excellent 'left vices rendered by Mrs. Simpson, who hag the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-12-19_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>Photographic Society.</head>
        <p>Photographic Society.</p>
        <p>ig Mr. T. W. Briggs, of Cleveleys, Pal"A bon second visit on Tuesday, and his lecture -re "Cairo and the Nile Villages" was even ralie absorbingly interesting than that which is gave last year. Mr. Briggs, judging f1":1 lecture, was exceedingly lucky on his to a dragoman was an honest man, well bred, ail, extremely intelligent, and took a pride in ing his party (consisting of two persons) r 1 he knew about the ruins, and about 'other l'10; teresting points of Egypt, too. He took to a Bisharen camp, where Mrs. Briggs tamed splendid photographs that her husbnne, could not take on account of the religious Pro Indices of the people; and tips°, as weldi others depicting various Nubian scenes 00111'4 Assouan, Edfou, and Assiout, were shown:, the screen, and proved to be pretty and inle"- esting. The modern plague of Egypt, the i turer told his audience, is ophthalmia, many children are blinded through it. ordinary to relate, although the inflamed la attract crowds of flies, the mothers will nell itey scare them off nor allow the baby to use na little fists to scare them—" it is unlock,' the the flies do the sores good!" Incideutallylisi, occupation had Lecturer showed what a blessing the been to the natives, and he t pressed the opinion that it was certain closer connection. with the Empire Iv°tl</p>
        <p>still more profitable to Egypt.</p>
