        <p>Bros., 27, Winwick-street; Sam N. Green, the " Spot Shop," opposite the Fish Market; the Co-operative Society, Sankey-street; E. Coates, Bridge-street; Caldwell and Co., Horsemarket- street; J. S. Garland, Bridge-street? IF YOU LOVE MUSIC! You are musical; you love music? Then why not a piano?—in many ways the most satisfy- ing and attractive of all musical instruments adapted to the home. Your inclinations and your purse run to a piano—then call at Messrs. Dawson's, 59, Sankey-street. They can satisfy your yearnings. Maybe, you cannot play, but that need not deter you from your resolve to have music in the home, for there is one instrument that will delight the family ear in response to a few turns of a handle, and that instrument is the phonograph, the latest makes in which, to- gether with the latest records, may be obtained at Mr. Thomas Taylor's, 61, Priestley-street. PRESENTS FOR YOURSELF—OR OTHERS. A pair of glasses may, quite conceivably, be the very best and wisest Christmas gift you could make to yourself. If so, consult Mr. Johnston Watson, of 84, Bridge-street, or Mr. R. Jackson Moss, of Stockton Heath, both of whom are experienced oculists. Maybe, you have a camera yourself, or you want one, or want to give one! Then the shop for you is Mr. llarold Smith's, in Bridge-street. Or it may be that you want photographic materials, scents, or any of the things that the modern chemist sells! If that be so the same address will suffice. HAVE YOUR PHOTOGRAPH TAKEN! An acceptable Christmas or New Year gift to a friend is a photograph of yourself, and if you decide on making a present of that kind you cannot do better than place yourself in the hands of Mr. Walter Burrell, St. Elphin's Studio, Manchester-road; or Mr. J. Parkinson, Bridge-street—and don't forget to "smile and look pleasant !" IS IT A BOOK? For books and Christmas and New Year cards we would advise a visit to Mr. Percival Pearse's well-stocked shop in Sankey-street, where you will find all the most attractive literature of the day on sale, as well as quite one of the most up-to-date selections of appro- priate cards, &amp;c. USEFUL IRONMONGERY. Something useful for the home will constitute a long-lived reminder of Christmas, 1914, and if your tastes run in that direction you will find many suitable articles either at Garland's, the ironmongers in Bridge-street; or at Caldwell's, in Ilorsemarket-street.</p>
        <p>The biggest battle scene ever staged will be produced by Mr. Leonard F. Duren on Decem- ber 21st at the Manchester Hippodrome. The name of "Kultur" has been given to this colossal spectacle, the author having based his story on incidents of the world's war as they have actually been recorded in Belgium. Whilst there are undoubtedly many thrilling moments in store for those who witness "Kultur" by reason of the human interest of the dramatic story Mr. Durell unfolds, there are no "horrors" in the spectacle. Briefly it is a tribute to the bravery of the great little nation that withstood the might of the greatest military power the world has known since the Roman domination. Miss Georgina Milne, who takes the pretty heroine's part, has played in "False Gods," "Henry VIII.," "The War God," and "Mac- beth," at His Majesty's; in "Mind the Paint Girl !" at the Duke of York's; and succeeded Miss Phyllis Neilson Terry in "The Amazons." Miss Milne was seen also in the leading roles in "Kismet" and "The Ever Open Door." Severe, If True. Mr. Cecil Du Gue, entrusted with one of the "For any enemy ship to try to get into Dover leading roles: made his name at the Criterion, at the present time would be like entering the Haymarket and Kingsway Theatres, London. mouth of Hell.' The great feature of "Kultur" is the music, [We understand that .the Admiralty have which will be quite out of the ordinary, by received no confirmation of this.]"—" Daily reason of its composition and orchestration. Telegraph." Organ, bagpipe and bugle effects are freshly We hope that none of our contemporaries interpolated, , and the whole score has been will blame the Admiralty for its lack of infor- written and arranged by Messrs. Barnes and mation.—" Punch." Joseph England. 014'1144'11410144444014++++++0M4+444.4014 44014q444+41+4144+44,14q4014+ 440140140144444 q M. A &amp; K. BRON A. W 6•3, • 11.11111D7r6°E7S1:REET.. ESTABLISHED 1885. Friars' Gate ( NEAR .lIIPPODROME )9 Warrington. Servants' 