        <p>Prince Fearnought. May Gannon and May Thompson will be, respectively, Jack and Jill; Jimmy Pullen is to be Ginger, the Prince's page; whilst Tom 'Gott will undertake the re- spensibility of making Dame Durden as good as she ought to be. The pantomime will be in seven scenes and gives us gorgeous glimpses of the Village of Truly Rural, Laburnam Grove, the Cave of the Hunchback, and the Palace. The book is by John Tiller, the music by the London Palace Maestro, Herman Finck, and the popular melodies by the latest and up-to- (latest composers. Who Jack and Jill were the old nursery books do not inform you, so John Tiller has stepped into the breach and will tell you all about it if you pay a visit to Ardwick Empire during the corning weeks. Sounded Like Brutality. They passed a magnificent building during their travels. "That's a fine house," said Brown to Jones, "and yet I cannot bear to look at it." "Why not?" asked Jones. "Why?" repeated Brown. "Because the owner built it out of the blood, the aches, the groans of his fellow men; out of the grief of children and the wails of women." "Great Scott!" exclaimed Jones; "the brute. What is he—a money lender?" " Oh, no; he is a dentist!" 1+,14+ q4q4+44qefoil++++4444++++ v4q4</p>
