        <p>Place them in a steamer or a moderately hot oven, and let theta cook from two to three hours according to size, dip each one into cold water, and remove the skin while the root is still warm. The beetroot is used in a great variety of ways on the Continent. To those looking out for fresh ideas for the kitchen, and willing to experiment, we submit the following recipe : French stewed beetroot: Slice a cooked beet- root, and stew it for half .an hour in sufficient thin gravy to cover it. Mix loi. of arrowroot or potato flour with a gill of cream, pour the gravy over it to thicken it without curdling, bring it almost to the boil, season it with pepper and salt; pour it over the beetroot in the dish, and just before serving sprinkle a few drops of Chili vinegar over it. HERE AND THERE. If a girl won a liusbaml by making a special dish that he liked, it is the usual experience that aftor marriage she stopped making it. , Waiving Formalities. De Snail: "I suppose I'll have to say good- night." Miss Weary: " If it will help you along any I'll consider it said." The Guilty Wretch. Tomdix : "I suppose you witnessed Green's marriage to the widow as an innocent bystander, eh?" Hojax : "Hardly that. I introduced Green to the widow last fall." Less Bread and More Apples. Bread is the commonest cause of decay in teeth, according to Mr. 11. W. Norman, of the Manchester Dental Hospital, in the course of a lecture at Ancoats. Mr. Norman said that cake, biscuits—in fact, anything made from flour—caused the teeth to decay. Vegetables did not have any had effect, but the diet of the vegetarian had far too much pastry and far too much pudding in it to escape the dentist's censure: The lecturer specially commended fruit. "Apples are the best fruit," he said, " and there is nothing which cleans the teeth like biting into an apple. It is a cheap food, too—cheaper than meat, and highly nutritious."</p>
