        <p>ITEMS. Fairfield United got a shock last week. The Cons, gave them a surprise, and had they had a full eleven out during the whole of the game, probably would have won. Finch kept a Watchful eye on Millward. Mellor, at outside-left, was the weak man in the Cons.' team. Fairfield Athletic is reported to have dis- banded. Rumour says that the defeat by the Elmwood "Cons. knocked the sport out of the players. Longford United got a shock from the Mis- sion Lads. From a playing point of view, the United deserved their fate. Sturgess, in goal, gave a creditable display. " Dick" Stevens was more like a Wanderer than a United; he did not show his true form at all. Indeed, on last week's form the Mission team should forge ahead. Winders is one of the best backs in the League Jones and Sherlock scored two goals each. The Young Men from Earlestown could not find Paddington last week. It is along the Manchester-road, on the out- skirts of Warrington! Sometimes people think it is London wa.-,- Earlestown's non-arrival caused great ni3- appointment to the Paddington players and supporters. llollins Green piled it on to the Bank Quay Recs. Five points to none will give their goal aver- age a lift. The Recs. must be getting in a bad way to lose by so heavy a margin. Billington scored the first three goals. St. Barnabas' and Newton Cons, played a goal- less game. Devonport was again in good form between the posts. Long and Jefferson played a good defensive game; as also did Lawrance and Tithers for the Cons. O'Neil and Porter were good in the middle line. Dagnall did all that was required of him in the visitors' goal. Stockton Heath beat Partington with a vengeance! The Partingtonians will not forget their visit to West-avenue. The score was ten—nil in favour of the "Heathens." The Walton Club have got a strong team to- gether now. Marsh, West, Mortimer, and Jones, former "Heathens"; and Siddall, late of Latchford, are in their ranks. The Grappenhall team include Shakeshaft and the Brothers Ford, formerly of Latchford. Both clubs should be good candidates for the "Starkey" Cup. The Great Sankey defence have not yet yielded a goal in the Widnes Alliance. The tit-bit for to-day will be at Paddington, where Fairfield United are the visitors. MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Longford -United v. Hollins Green; referee, Mr. W. Bennett. Paddington v. I- }irfield United; Mr. J. Kite. Elmwood Cons. Latchford Baptists; Mr. B. G. Brandreth. Bank Quay Recs. v. S. Barnabas'; Mr. G. Knight. NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Second Division. Stockton Heath v. Altrineham St. George's; Mr. H. Smith. FRIENDLY MATCH. Grapperiliall v. Orford White Star, at Grap- penliall. THE WAR BUDGET. A new note in war is struck by the Christmas Number of the familiar " War Budget," the popular illustrated weekly published by the " Daily Chronicle." The cover is an attractive specimen of colour Printing, and depicts a group of British sold- iers at the Front enjoying their Christmas pudding beside a cheerful camp fire under the protection of one of the "dug-outs" which have been so marked a feature of the war. The numerous illustrations which go to make up the thirty-six pages of the "War Budget" are of the usual varied selection. Many scenes from the snow-clad trenches and some fine pictures of artillery at work also contribute to an excellent number. , , - • •</p>
