        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS. DRAMA AT THE ROYAL COURT. There is fine fare at the Royal Court this week-end for all such as love a sensational play sensationally writ, and it takes the form of "The Sledge Hammer," a sterling drama of Flemish extraction, which helped to build up the great reputation of that robust histrion, the late Wilson Barrett. BIG CHRISTMAS ATTRACTION. The special Christmas attraction at the Royal Court Theatre will be that brilliant light opera, "The Marriage Market," which will be pre- sented by Macdonald and Young's company, by arrangement with Mr. George Edwardes. The first performance will be on Boxing Day, when there will be a matinee at 2.30, as well as the evening performance at 7.30. There will also be matinees on Monday, December 28, and on New Year's Day. In London alone no fewer</p>
        <p>„ „„. *ibw „ , • 444 MISS STOCKDALE, THE LEADING LADY.</p>
        <p>all wbo had presented the prizes and assisted with the refreshments, &amp;c. About £2O has been raised as the result of this patriotic effort. „ PATRIOTIC FAVOURS. Mrs. Robert Pierpoint's Grateful Correspondents. Mrs. Robert Pierpoint, who was supplied with several of the patriotic favours painted by Mrs. G. S. Pendlebury, has just sent the following to Mrs. Osborne (who forwarded them to her): "8, Cleveland-square, Lancaster Gate, Lon- don, W. It may please Mrs. Pendlebury to know that I sent one of the favours to a Rus- sian friend of ours, who lives in the Crimea, and I have to-day received a letter in which he sends me a thousand thanks for the 'charming little flag representing our four allied countries. Long live these four countries, and Servia, Montenegro and Japan! . . I also nent one to a French friend in the Pyrenees, who writes: 'Thank you for your lovely flags; very proudly I have pinned them on my breast. Should my courage and my hope grow weak, they would be restored by looking at these four flags joined together.' I think that this would be of interest to you also.—Yours sincerely, M. E. PIERPOINT." SUNDAY SCHOLARS' "MITE:" The sum of 19s. has been realised by a collec- tion at St. Ann's Sunday School for Princess Mary's Fund, the purpose of which is to pro- vide Christmas boxes for our soldiers and sailors. BULKY BUNDLES OF WOOLLEN ARTICLES Mrs. Edelsten gives notice that there will be no meeting at the Town Hall on Thursday, December 24th. Splendid donations of woollen comforts were received on the 17th, and a large parcel sent to the Warrington Infirmary for the use of the wounded soldiers there. Fazakerley Hospital and the Belgian Headquarters in London also had bulky consignments sent them. Miss Broadbent again obtained belts and hel- mets for the guardians of the Ship Canal. Donations were received from the b,fayoress, the Training. College, the Women's Tariff Re- form League, Mrs. Burleigh, Miss Richardson, Nra Allison and others.</p>
